Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "continued_influence_belief", "Title": "Retracted but Not Erased: The Continued Influence of Corrected Misinformation in LLM Belief Representations", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM reads a false claim about an item that is later explicitly retracted in the same context, it verbally acknowledges the retraction yet retains an internally-decodable 'stale belief' in the retracted claim that continues to drive its downstream inferences \u2014 an LLM analogue of the human Continued Influence Effect (CIE). This internal residual belief is separable from the model's stated belief, predicts CIE-style reasoning errors, and can be causally erased by intervening on the component that carries it.", "Related Work": "The Continued Influence Effect / belief perseverance is a canonical, heavily-cited human finding: corrected misinformation continues to influence reasoning even after successful retraction (Chan et al. 2017 meta-analysis; Ecker et al. 2022 Nature Rev Psych; constraint-satisfaction models of perseverance, Read & Marcus-Newhall 2001; DIO model 2025). In LLMs, prior work studies (a) knowledge-editing reversibility and 'superficial editing' (Superficial Editing 2025; Believe It or Not 2025), (b) belief shifts under pretraining poisoning (Layer of Truth 2025), (c) intra-memory knowledge conflict localization (Where Knowledge Collides 2026), and (d) in-context knowledge conflict / sycophancy behaviorally. Crucially, NONE isolate the CIE signature: a *within-context* retraction event after which the model behaviorally acknowledges the correction yet an internally-decodable belief in the *retracted* claim persists and continues to drive inferences. Prior belief-entrenchment work (Martingale Score; confirmation-bias CoT) concerns gradual non-Bayesian drift, not persistence of an explicitly-corrected proposition. We transfer a specific human-cognition phenomenon into LLMs, dissociate stated-vs-internal belief around a discrete correction, and give it a causal mechanistic treatment (locate + intervene), which is new.", "Abstract": "People keep using misinformation even after they accept a correction \u2014 the Continued Influence Effect (CIE), one of the most robust findings in the misinformation literature. As LLMs increasingly ingest, then are corrected on, false claims within a single context (RAG updates, user retractions, multi-turn edits), we ask whether they inherit CIE at the level of their internal beliefs. We hypothesize that after an explicit in-context retraction of a false item-state claim, an LLM (i) verbally acknowledges the correction but (ii) retains a linearly-decodable residual belief in the retracted claim, (iii) this internal residual belief \u2014 not the stated belief \u2014 predicts continued reliance on the misinformation in downstream inference, and (iv) the residual can be causally erased by intervening on the component that carries it, reducing CIE-style errors. We build a controlled dataset of narratives/knowledge items in which a false claim is introduced and then retracted (with matched no-retraction and never-introduced controls), using both real-fact and synthetic-entity tracks to control priors, plus downstream inference probes whose correct answer depends on discarding the retracted claim. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read the internal belief in the retracted claim across layers, compute a per-item CIE residual (internal belief post-retraction minus never-introduced baseline), test whether it dissociates from stated belief and predicts downstream reliance (AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline), localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test a contrastive 'retraction-completion' steering intervention that drives the internal belief to baseline. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, CIE residual magnitude, stated-vs-internal dissociation, reliance-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-intervention error reduction, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Build ~5k scenarios each containing a false claim about an item's state that is later retracted, following the classic CIE paradigm. Two tracks: (a) REAL-FACT narratives (e.g., a report initially says 'the fire was caused by flammable materials in the closet' then retracts it) adapted from CIE stimuli; (b) SYNTHETIC-ENTITY track with novel entities/attributes to fully control pretraining priors (e.g., 'Zorblax was reported to orbit Kelvath. Correction: this was an error; Zorblax does not orbit Kelvath.'). Conditions per item: (i) RETRACTED (claim introduced then explicitly corrected), (ii) NO-RETRACTION (claim introduced, never corrected), (iii) NEVER-INTRODUCED (claim absent) as baseline. Vary retraction strength (mere negation vs. explanatory correction that supplies an alternative), following the CIE finding that alternatives matter. Add: a STATED-BELIEF probe ('Is it true that Zorblax orbits Kelvath?'), a DOWNSTREAM-RELIANCE probe (an open inference question whose correct answer requires discarding the retracted claim, e.g., 'What likely explains X?'), and comprehension checks (can the model restate that a correction occurred) to separate parsing failure from genuine perseverance. Include paraphrase/format perturbations to guard against surface cues. 2) Internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the retracted-claim's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the stated-belief question, in all three conditions. Train linear belief probes (P(retracted claim holds)) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks. Define CIE residual = probe belief(RETRACTED) \u2212 probe belief(NEVER-INTRODUCED) at the item, per layer. A positive residual is the internal CIE signature. 3) Dissociation: Compare the internal CIE residual to the model's STATED belief (which should reflect the correction). Test the key CIE claim that the internal residual persists even when the stated belief has updated (per-item: stated=false but internal residual>0). Compare retraction-strength conditions (mere-negation vs. explanatory-alternative), predicting explanatory alternatives reduce the residual, mirroring humans. 4) Predictiveness: Collect ground-truth DOWNSTREAM-RELIANCE (does the model use the retracted claim in its open inference?). Test whether the internal CIE residual predicts per-item reliance (AUROC), compared against (a) a log-prob confidence baseline and (b) the stated-belief readout, to show the internal residual carries reliance-relevant information beyond what the model says. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the residual belief post-retraction (patch RETRACTED-condition activations into NEVER-INTRODUCED runs, and vice versa) to find the minimal component restoring/removing the residual; compare to knowledge-conflict heads reported in prior late-layer work. 6) Causal intervention ('retraction completion'): Derive a contrastive steering direction from RETRACTED vs. NEVER-INTRODUCED activations (the residual direction); test that (a) subtracting it drives the internal belief to baseline and reduces downstream reliance/CIE errors, (b) adding it exaggerates perseverance, (c) specificity: matched random directions and the truth direction do not reduce CIE, and unrelated facts/general benchmarks (MMLU subset) and perplexity are preserved. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across the real and synthetic tracks, across domains, and across retraction phrasings. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, CIE residual magnitude by condition/layer, stated-vs-internal dissociation rate, reliance-prediction AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-intervention CIE-error reduction, specificity checks, perplexity delta.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The residual could reflect surface co-occurrence of the retracted claim's tokens rather than a genuine belief; matched NEVER-INTRODUCED controls, non-introduced distractors, comprehension checks, and paraphrase perturbations mitigate this. CIE may be absent internally (a robust negative result \u2014 LLMs fully erase corrected claims \u2014 would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with humans). Stated belief and downstream reliance may both fail simply because the model can't parse the retraction; comprehension checks and the RETRACTED-vs-NO-RETRACTION contrast disentangle parsing failure from perseverance. The residual may not be cleanly linear or single-locus; we report layerwise results and treat 'drive belief to baseline / reduce CIE' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Real-fact CIE stimuli may interact with pretraining priors; the synthetic track isolates the in-context mechanism. Downstream-reliance judgments require careful open-ended scoring (LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample). Steering could degrade fluency or over-suppress legitimately retained information; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'continued_influence_belief', 'Title': 'Retracted but Not Erased: The Continued Influence of Corrected Misinformation in LLM Belief Representations', 'Short Hypothesis': "When an LLM reads a false claim about an item that is later explicitly retracted in the same context, it verbally acknowledges the retraction yet retains an internally-decodable 'stale belief' in the retracted claim that continues to drive its downstream inferences — an LLM analogue of the human Continued Influence Effect (CIE). This internal residual belief is separable from the model's stated belief, predicts CIE-style reasoning errors, and can be causally erased by intervening on the component that carries it.", 'Related Work': "The Continued Influence Effect / belief perseverance is a canonical, heavily-cited human finding: corrected misinformation continues to influence reasoning even after successful retraction (Chan et al. 2017 meta-analysis; Ecker et al. 2022 Nature Rev Psych; constraint-satisfaction models of perseverance, Read & Marcus-Newhall 2001; DIO model 2025). In LLMs, prior work studies (a) knowledge-editing reversibility and 'superficial editing' (Superficial Editing 2025; Believe It or Not 2025), (b) belief shifts under pretraining poisoning (Layer of Truth 2025), (c) intra-memory knowledge conflict localization (Where Knowledge Collides 2026), and (d) in-context knowledge conflict / sycophancy behaviorally. Crucially, NONE isolate the CIE signature: a *within-context* retraction event after which the model behaviorally acknowledges the correction yet an internally-decodable belief in the *retracted* claim persists and continues to drive inferences. Prior belief-entrenchment work (Martingale Score; confirmation-bias CoT) concerns gradual non-Bayesian drift, not persistence of an explicitly-corrected proposition. We transfer a specific human-cognition phenomenon into LLMs, dissociate stated-vs-internal belief around a discrete correction, and give it a causal mechanistic treatment (locate + intervene), which is new.", 'Abstract': "People keep using misinformation even after they accept a correction — the Continued Influence Effect (CIE), one of the most robust findings in the misinformation literature. As LLMs increasingly ingest, then are corrected on, false claims within a single context (RAG updates, user retractions, multi-turn edits), we ask whether they inherit CIE at the level of their internal beliefs. We hypothesize that after an explicit in-context retraction of a false item-state claim, an LLM (i) verbally acknowledges the correction but (ii) retains a linearly-decodable residual belief in the retracted claim, (iii) this internal residual belief — not the stated belief — predicts continued reliance on the misinformation in downstream inference, and (iv) the residual can be causally erased by intervening on the component that carries it, reducing CIE-style errors. We build a controlled dataset of narratives/knowledge items in which a false claim is introduced and then retracted (with matched no-retraction and never-introduced controls), using both real-fact and synthetic-entity tracks to control priors, plus downstream inference probes whose correct answer depends on discarding the retracted claim. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read the internal belief in the retracted claim across layers, compute a per-item CIE residual (internal belief post-retraction minus never-introduced baseline), test whether it dissociates from stated belief and predicts downstream reliance (AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline), localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test a contrastive 'retraction-completion' steering intervention that drives the internal belief to baseline. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, CIE residual magnitude, stated-vs-internal dissociation, reliance-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-intervention error reduction, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Build ~5k scenarios each containing a false claim about an item's state that is later retracted, following the classic CIE paradigm. Two tracks: (a) REAL-FACT narratives (e.g., a report initially says 'the fire was caused by flammable materials in the closet' then retracts it) adapted from CIE stimuli; (b) SYNTHETIC-ENTITY track with novel entities/attributes to fully control pretraining priors (e.g., 'Zorblax was reported to orbit Kelvath. Correction: this was an error; Zorblax does not orbit Kelvath.'). Conditions per item: (i) RETRACTED (claim introduced then explicitly corrected), (ii) NO-RETRACTION (claim introduced, never corrected), (iii) NEVER-INTRODUCED (claim absent) as baseline. Vary retraction strength (mere negation vs. explanatory correction that supplies an alternative), following the CIE finding that alternatives matter. Add: a STATED-BELIEF probe ('Is it true that Zorblax orbits Kelvath?'), a DOWNSTREAM-RELIANCE probe (an open inference question whose correct answer requires discarding the retracted claim, e.g., 'What likely explains X?'), and comprehension checks (can the model restate that a correction occurred) to separate parsing failure from genuine perseverance. Include paraphrase/format perturbations to guard against surface cues. 2) Internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the retracted-claim's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the stated-belief question, in all three conditions. Train linear belief probes (P(retracted claim holds)) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks. Define CIE residual = probe belief(RETRACTED) − probe belief(NEVER-INTRODUCED) at the item, per layer. A positive residual is the internal CIE signature. 3) Dissociation: Compare the internal CIE residual to the model's STATED belief (which should reflect the correction). Test the key CIE claim that the internal residual persists even when the stated belief has updated (per-item: stated=false but internal residual>0). Compare retraction-strength conditions (mere-negation vs. explanatory-alternative), predicting explanatory alternatives reduce the residual, mirroring humans. 4) Predictiveness: Collect ground-truth DOWNSTREAM-RELIANCE (does the model use the retracted claim in its open inference?). Test whether the internal CIE residual predicts per-item reliance (AUROC), compared against (a) a log-prob confidence baseline and (b) the stated-belief readout, to show the internal residual carries reliance-relevant information beyond what the model says. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the residual belief post-retraction (patch RETRACTED-condition activations into NEVER-INTRODUCED runs, and vice versa) to find the minimal component restoring/removing the residual; compare to knowledge-conflict heads reported in prior late-layer work. 6) Causal intervention ('retraction completion'): Derive a contrastive steering direction from RETRACTED vs. NEVER-INTRODUCED activations (the residual direction); test that (a) subtracting it drives the internal belief to baseline and reduces downstream reliance/CIE errors, (b) adding it exaggerates perseverance, (c) specificity: matched random directions and the truth direction do not reduce CIE, and unrelated facts/general benchmarks (MMLU subset) and perplexity are preserved. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across the real and synthetic tracks, across domains, and across retraction phrasings. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, CIE residual magnitude by condition/layer, stated-vs-internal dissociation rate, reliance-prediction AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-intervention CIE-error reduction, specificity checks, perplexity delta.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The residual could reflect surface co-occurrence of the retracted claim's tokens rather than a genuine belief; matched NEVER-INTRODUCED controls, non-introduced distractors, comprehension checks, and paraphrase perturbations mitigate this. CIE may be absent internally (a robust negative result — LLMs fully erase corrected claims — would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with humans). Stated belief and downstream reliance may both fail simply because the model can't parse the retraction; comprehension checks and the RETRACTED-vs-NO-RETRACTION contrast disentangle parsing failure from perseverance. The residual may not be cleanly linear or single-locus; we report layerwise results and treat 'drive belief to baseline / reduce CIE' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Real-fact CIE stimuli may interact with pretraining priors; the synthetic track isolates the in-context mechanism. Downstream-reliance judgments require careful open-ended scoring (LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample). Steering could degrade fluency or over-suppress legitimately retained information; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

