Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "illusory_truth_internal", "Title": "Say It Again: In-Context Repetition Inflates LLMs' Internal Belief Confidence Independent of Truth", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human illusory-truth effect, merely repeating a claim within an LLM's context monotonically inflates the model's internally-decoded belief/confidence in that claim as a dose-response of repetition count, independent of (and even against) the claim's factual truth. This repetition-driven inflation is carried by an identifiable internal component (a 'familiarity/fluency' direction or set of heads) that is causally dissociable from the truth-value direction: ablating it restores truth-tracking without removing the model's ability to represent truth.", "Related Work": "The illusory-truth effect \u2014 repetition raising perceived truth and, separately, subjective confidence independent of factual truth \u2014 is among the most robust findings in cognitive psychology (Dechene et al.; Fazio et al. 2015; Hassan & Barber 2021 on repetition frequency; Unkelbach 2010 on fluency-as-cue; illusory-certainty 2024 showing repetition inflates confidence orthogonally to truth). In LLMs, repetition has been studied only at TRAINING time: 'Scaling Laws of Learning from Repeated Data' (2022) links repeated pretraining data to induction-head damage and memorization; 'Learning and Unlearning of Fabricated Knowledge' (2024) studies durability of injected facts; 'Layer of Truth' (2025) studies continual-pretraining poisoning shifting late-layer beliefs. The truth-probe literature (Geometry of Truth 2023; Azaria & Mitchell 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024) isolates a linear truth direction; CCPS (2025) probes confidence via representation stability. CRITICALLY, no work asks whether IN-CONTEXT (inference-time) repetition inflates an INTERNALLY-DECODED belief/confidence as a dose-response, orthogonally to truth, and whether that inflation is a locatable, ablatable fluency/familiarity component distinct from the truth direction. We transfer a canonical human-cognition phenomenon into LLM internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "People judge repeated statements as truer and grow more confident in them, even when the statements are false \u2014 the illusory-truth effect, a fluency-driven bias separable from actual discrimination ability. As LLMs increasingly ingest contexts where the same claim recurs (repeated user assertions, echoed retrieval passages, multi-document redundancy), we ask whether they inherit this bias at the level of their internal beliefs. We hypothesize that (i) merely repeating a claim within the context monotonically inflates the model's internally-decoded belief/confidence in it as a dose-response of repetition count, (ii) this inflation is largely independent of the claim's factual truth and can push false claims toward 'believed', (iii) it is mediated by an identifiable internal 'familiarity/fluency' component that is causally dissociable from the well-studied truth-value direction, and (iv) ablating that component restores truth-tracking without destroying the model's representation of truth. We build a controlled dataset of matched true and false item-state claims each embedded in contexts that repeat the claim 0 to N times (with paraphrased-repetition and unrelated-filler controls to separate meaning-level repetition from token/length confounds), including a synthetic-entity track to control pretraining priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief and confidence probes, measure the repetition dose-response of the internal readout separately for true and false claims, quantify the truth-independent inflation, geometrically relate the repetition-inflation direction to the truth direction, localize responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering/ablating the inflation direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-count belief/confidence dose-response slopes, truth-independence of inflation, subspace angle vs. truth, patching effect sizes, post-ablation truth-tracking restoration, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k item-state claims, balanced TRUE and FALSE, across domains (geography, science, history, trivia). Two tracks: (a) REAL facts the models reliably know/disbelieve; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. For each claim, build matched contexts that present the claim REPEATED k times, k in {0,1,2,4,8}, interspersed with neutral filler. Include controls: (i) VERBATIM repetition, (ii) PARAPHRASED repetition (same proposition, different surface form \u2014 tests meaning-level vs. token-level repetition), (iii) LENGTH-MATCHED filler with the claim shown once (controls for context length), (iv) REPEATED-DISTRACTOR (an unrelated claim repeated k times, controls for generic repetition). Add a behavioral truth-judgment probe ('Is it true that X? Answer and rate your confidence') and comprehension checks. Apply format/paraphrase perturbations. 2) Internal probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim's key tokens (final query occurrence) across layers. Train (i) a linear BELIEF probe (P(claim true)) and (ii) a CONFIDENCE probe (following CCPS-style / representation-based confidence) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Dose-response measurement: For each k, read the internal belief and confidence at the query, SEPARATELY for true and false claims. Fit dose-response slopes (internal readout vs. k). Test the key illusory-truth prediction: belief/confidence rises monotonically with k for BOTH true and false claims (positive slope even for false), i.e., inflation is not just correct evidence accumulation. Compare verbatim vs. paraphrased repetition (a robust effect for paraphrase implicates a meaning-level familiarity signal), and confirm repeated-distractor / length-matched controls do not inflate the target claim's readout (specificity). 4) Truth-independence & geometry: Quantify the truth-INDEPENDENT component of inflation (belief shift attributable to k after conditioning on ground truth). Compute cosine/subspace angle between the repetition-inflation direction (difference-in-means: high-k vs. k=0 activations) and the truth-value direction; test whether inflation is decodable after projecting out the truth direction. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from high-k to k=0 runs to find the minimal heads/layers whose states carry the repetition-inflation signal; contrast with copying/induction heads (from repeated-data work) and truth/belief heads. 6) Causal control: Derive an 'inflation' steering direction (contrastive high-k vs. k=0). Test (a) subtracting/ablating it drives the internal belief on repeated FALSE claims back toward k=0 baseline and improves behavioral truth-judgment accuracy on repeated-false items; (b) adding it exaggerates illusory belief; (c) specificity: matched random directions and the truth direction itself do not selectively fix repeated-false items; (d) collateral checks: general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-repeated claim accuracy, and perplexity are preserved. 7) Predictiveness & generalization: Test whether the internal inflation readout predicts behavioral illusory-truth errors (AUROC) beyond a log-prob baseline; test probe/steering transfer across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and repetition types. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-count dose-response slopes (true vs. false), truth-independent inflation magnitude, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. truth, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-ablation truth-tracking restoration and error-reduction deltas, prediction AUROC, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Repetition-driven belief change might be rational evidence accumulation rather than a fluency bias; the false-claim dose-response (repetition should NOT raise belief in a false claim if the model is rational), the paraphrased-repetition condition, and the repeated-distractor/length-matched controls are designed to dissociate genuine illusory-truth from evidence integration and length/token confounds. The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result \u2014 LLMs resist in-context repetition bias \u2014 would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with humans). Belief and confidence probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks and separate the two probes. The inflation and truth directions may be more entangled than hypothesized, in which case the finding characterizes their entanglement (still informative). Localization may yield a distributed rather than discrete component; we treat 'ablate to restore truth-tracking' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Real-fact repetition interacts with pretraining priors; the synthetic track isolates the in-context mechanism. Steering could degrade fluency or blunt legitimate in-context updating; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-repeated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
