Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "self_authored_belief_endowment", "Title": "I Thought of It, So It Must Be True: Do LLMs Over-Believe Their Own Self-Generated Claims?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human 'generation effect' and endowment bias, an LLM holds a stronger internal belief in a proposition when the model itself generated it (e.g., as a chain-of-thought conclusion) than when the identical proposition is presented as externally-given text, holding content and truth constant. This self-authorship endowment is (i) linearly decodable as an internal belief-strength inflation, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, (iii) carried by an identifiable internal component, and (iv) causally reducible by steering, thereby lowering the model's tendency to trust its own unverified reasoning.", "Related Work": "Prior interpretability work isolates a linear truth-value/belief direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Azaria & Mitchell 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024) and studies social overrides of belief (When Truth Is Overridden: sycophancy 2025; Signal Competition 2025; Trust Me I'm an Expert 2026). Notably, 'When Truth Is Overridden' finds user *expertise* is NOT internally encoded, and authority/credibility work concerns *external* source cues. My own prior proposal on belief-provenance tags source generically (prior vs. context vs. retrieval) but does NOT isolate self-authorship as a belief-STRENGTH modulator, nor connect it to the human generation/endowment effect, nor test the safety-critical case of a model over-trusting its OWN chain-of-thought conclusions. Confirmation-bias/entrenchment work (Martingale Score; CoT confirmation bias) concerns temporal drift, not authorship. No prior work asks whether merely being the *author* of a claim inflates an LLM's internal belief in it relative to the identical externally-supplied claim, nor gives this a mechanistic, causal treatment. We transfer a canonical human-cognition phenomenon (generation effect / endowment) into LLM belief internals.", "Abstract": "Humans believe and remember information more strongly when they generate it themselves than when they merely read it \u2014 the 'generation effect', a cousin of the endowment bias. As LLMs increasingly reason via chain-of-thought and then act on their own intermediate conclusions, a safety-critical question arises: does a model over-trust a claim simply because it produced the claim itself? We hypothesize that an LLM holds a stronger internal belief in a proposition it self-generated than in the identical proposition presented as external text, with content and factual truth held constant. We build a controlled dataset of matched item-state claims that appear in two conditions: SELF-AUTHORED (the model derives/states the claim as its own chain-of-thought conclusion) and EXTERNAL (the same verbatim claim is supplied as given text the model reads), plus true/false variants and a synthetic-entity track to control pretraining priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read internal belief strength at the claim, compute a per-item authorship-endowment score (self-authored belief minus external belief), test whether it is positive even for false claims (so it is not rational evidence), measure its geometric relationship to the truth direction, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering an 'authorship-endowment' direction to restore parity. We further test whether the endowment predicts a downstream failure: over-reliance on unverified self-generated claims in multi-step tasks. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, authorship-endowment score, truth-independence, subspace angle vs. truth, patching effect sizes, post-steering parity restoration, downstream over-reliance reduction, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k item-state claims, balanced TRUE/FALSE, across domains (arithmetic/logic conclusions, factual attributions, entity-attribute claims). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims the models can plausibly derive/read; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims with stipulated ground truth to control priors. For each claim create MATCHED conditions with identical verbatim proposition at the read-out point: (i) SELF-AUTHORED \u2014 elicit the claim as the model's own conclusion via a short CoT/derivation ('Reason step by step, then state your conclusion'), then read internal belief at the model-produced conclusion token; (ii) EXTERNAL \u2014 the identical conclusion sentence is supplied verbatim as given text ('Here is a statement: <claim>') from a neutral source, and belief is read at the same tokens. Control conditions: (iii) EXTERNAL-ATTRIBUTED-TO-SELF (text says 'You previously concluded: <claim>' without the model actually generating it) to separate authorship from mere self-attribution; (iv) LENGTH/EFFORT-MATCHED external (a filler derivation precedes the external claim) to control for context length and preceding reasoning tokens. Include comprehension checks and paraphrase/format perturbations. 2) Internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim tokens across layers; train linear BELIEF probes (P(claim true)) and a representation-based CONFIDENCE probe, with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Endowment measurement: Define authorship-endowment = belief(SELF-AUTHORED) \u2212 belief(EXTERNAL) per item and layer. Test the key prediction that endowment > 0 for BOTH true and false claims (positive even for FALSE self-generated claims \u2192 not rational evidence accumulation). Verify control conditions: mere self-attribution (iii) yields smaller endowment than genuine generation, and length-matched (iv) rules out context-length confounds. 4) Truth-independence & geometry: Quantify the truth-independent endowment (belief shift after conditioning on ground truth); compute cosine/subspace angle between the endowment direction (difference-in-means SELF vs. EXTERNAL activations) and the truth-value direction; test whether endowment remains decodable after projecting out the truth direction. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from SELF-AUTHORED to EXTERNAL runs to find the minimal heads/layers carrying the endowment signal; contrast with truth/belief heads and with any 'self'/authorship representation from prior self-recognition work. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'authorship-endowment' steering direction (SELF vs. EXTERNAL). Test (a) subtracting it drives self-authored belief on FALSE claims toward external-parity and reduces over-confidence; (b) adding it exaggerates self-endowment; (c) specificity: matched random directions and the truth direction do not selectively restore parity; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated claim accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Downstream over-reliance & predictiveness: In multi-step tasks (multi-hop QA, arithmetic word problems) where an intermediate self-generated claim may be wrong, test whether the internal endowment predicts the model failing to revisit/verify its own erroneous intermediate conclusion (AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline), and whether endowment-reducing steering increases self-correction and final accuracy on items with a faulty self-generated step. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and authorship elicitation styles. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, authorship-endowment score (true vs. false), truth-independent endowment magnitude, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. truth, control-condition contrasts, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering parity restoration, downstream self-correction / accuracy deltas, over-reliance prediction AUROC, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The endowment could reflect rational reasons \u2014 a self-generated claim followed a derivation the model 'trusts', which is legitimate when the derivation is correct; the FALSE-claim contrast, the self-attribution control (iii), and the length/effort-matched control (iv) are designed to isolate authorship-per-se from valid derivation and from context confounds (though for correct derivations the two are hard to fully separate). The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result \u2014 LLMs weight self- and externally-authored claims equally \u2014 is itself publishable and reassuring for safety). Reading belief at 'identical' tokens across conditions is imperfect because surrounding context differs; we anchor probes at matched claim tokens, use paraphrase perturbations, and report layerwise results. Endowment and truth directions may be more entangled than hypothesized, in which case the finding characterizes their entanglement (still informative). Localization may be distributed; we treat 'steer to restore parity' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Downstream over-reliance scoring on open-ended steps needs careful automatic + human-validated judging. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model distrust its own correct reasoning (harming accuracy on items with valid self-generated steps); we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, valid-derivation accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
