tional reasons — a self-generated claim followed a derivation the model 'trusts', which is legitimate when the derivation is correct; the FALSE-claim contrast, the self-attribution control (iii), and the length/effort-matched control (iv) are designed to isolate authorship-per-se from valid derivation and from context confounds (though for correct derivations the two are hard to fully separate). The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result — LLMs weight self- and externally-authored claims equally — is itself publishable and reassuring for safety). Reading belief at 'identical' tokens across conditions is imperfect because surrounding context differs; we anchor probes at matched claim tokens, use paraphrase perturbations, and report layerwise results. Endowment and truth directions may be more entangled than hypothesized, in which case the finding characterizes their entanglement (still informative). Localization may be distributed; we treat 'steer to restore parity' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Downstream over-reliance scoring on open-ended steps needs careful automatic + human-validated judging. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model distrust its own correct reasoning (harming accuracy on items with valid self-generated steps); we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, valid-derivation accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

