Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "source_independence_discounting", "Title": "Counting Voices, Not Sources: Do LLMs Internally Track Whether Consensus Is Independent?", "Short Hypothesis": "When multiple retrieved documents assert the same claim, a rational agent should update its belief more if the sources are independent than if they are copies of one origin (the 'illusion of consensus' failure in humans). We hypothesize that LLMs DO form an internal, linearly-decodable representation of source independence-vs-dependence, but that this representation is causally under-weighted: the belief-update magnitude tracks raw report COUNT far more than the internally-available independence signal. We further hypothesize an identifiable internal component gates independence-discounting, and steering it can restore appropriate discounting of redundant sources \u2014 improving RAG faithfulness on repeated-but-non-independent evidence.", "Related Work": "Human social-learning work shows people partially discount correlated/dependent sources and distinguish true from false consensus (Bayesian belief polarization via source-independence perceptions, 2024; belief revision sensitive to statistical dependencies, 2020; social meta-inference / evidentiary value of consensus, 2021; getting to the source of the illusion of consensus, 2021). In LLMs, behavioral RAG work finds models are 'heuristic followers': paraphrased repetition is MORE persuasive than distinct independent support, and models show primacy/repetition biases (GroupQA: Rational Synthesizers or Heuristic Followers?, 2026; DynamicQA 2024). Knowledge-conflict probing shows parametric-vs-contextual source and conflict are linearly decodable (Probing Language Models on Their Knowledge Source, 2024; ProbeRAG/CLEAR, 2025), and Belief Dynamics (2025) gives a Bayesian account of in-context evidence accumulation as sigmoidal in report count. My prior 'illusory_truth_internal' proposal studies repetition-count dose-response of a single claim's belief, and 'belief_source_tags' studies provenance (prior/context/retrieval). NONE isolate an internal representation of source INDEPENDENCE (are these N reports genuinely distinct origins or copies of one?), nor test whether the belief-update under-weights that internally-available signal relative to raw count, nor causally restore independence-discounting. We transfer the canonical 'illusion of consensus' into LLM internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "If ten news outlets independently confirm a claim, it is far stronger evidence than the same story copied ten times from one wire report. Humans partially honor this distinction; recent behavioral work shows LLMs largely do not, treating paraphrased repetition as more persuasive than genuinely independent support \u2014 an 'illusion of consensus' with direct consequences for retrieval-augmented generation. We ask a sharper, mechanistic question: does an LLM internally represent whether a set of agreeing sources is independent or dependent, and if so, why does it fail to use that signal? We hypothesize that (i) source independence-vs-dependence is linearly decodable from residual-stream activations at the point the model reads a group of agreeing reports, (ii) the model's internal belief-update magnitude nonetheless tracks raw report COUNT far more than this independence signal (a decodable-but-under-used representation), (iii) an identifiable internal component gates independence-discounting, and (iv) steering that component restores appropriate discounting of redundant sources. We build a controlled dataset of item-state claims each supported by N reports whose INDEPENDENCE is varied (verbatim copies, paraphrases attributed to one origin, and genuinely distinct independent sources) at matched surface repetition counts, with a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode independence and to read internal belief, quantify how belief tracks count vs. independence against a Bayesian oracle, localize the gating component via activation patching, and test a contrastive steering intervention that restores discounting. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, count-vs-independence belief sensitivity, oracle-deviation, patching effect sizes, post-steering discounting restoration and RAG-faithfulness gains, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~5k item-state claims across domains (news-style facts, science claims, entity-attributes). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims models plausibly evaluate; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. For each claim, construct evidence groups that vary INDEPENDENCE while holding report COUNT (N in {1,2,4,8}) constant: (i) INDEPENDENT \u2014 N genuinely distinct sources with different authors/outlets/wording/reasoning; (ii) DEPENDENT-VERBATIM \u2014 the same report copied N times (explicitly attributed to one origin, e.g., 'reprinted from Source A'); (iii) DEPENDENT-PARAPHRASE \u2014 N paraphrases explicitly derived from one origin ('Sources B\u2013E all cite Source A'). Cross with TRUE/FALSE claims. Include a Bayesian ORACLE label: independent reports should raise posterior more than dependent copies at equal N (compute an explicit likelihood-ratio oracle on the synthetic track). Add a downstream BELIEF/ANSWER probe ('Given these reports, how confident are you that X? Answer + confidence'), comprehension checks (can the model restate which sources are independent?), and paraphrase/format/order perturbations (to separate independence from primacy/length confounds). 2) Independence probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations after the model reads the evidence group, across layers; train linear probes to decode INDEPENDENCE (independent vs. dependent) at matched N, with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. This tests whether the signal is internally available. 3) Belief probing & count-vs-independence sensitivity: Train linear BELIEF/confidence probes reading internal posterior in the claim. For each condition, measure how the internal belief scales with (a) report COUNT and (b) INDEPENDENCE. Fit belief ~ count + independence and report standardized coefficients; the key prediction is a large count coefficient and a small independence coefficient relative to the Bayesian oracle (under-weighting the available signal). Compute per-item oracle-deviation. Compare verbal-output sensitivity to internal-belief sensitivity. 4) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying the independence signal and those gating the discounting of dependent evidence (patch INDEPENDENT-group activations into DEPENDENT-group runs at matched N; find the minimal component that increases/decreases the belief update); contrast with knowledge-source/conflict heads from prior probing work and with copying/induction heads. 5) Causal restoration: Derive a contrastive 'independence-discounting' steering direction (independent vs. dependent activations at matched N). Test (a) applying it on DEPENDENT groups reduces the (unwarranted) belief update toward oracle-appropriate discounting; (b) removing/negating it on INDEPENDENT groups collapses the update, confirming causality; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively restore discounting; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), independent-evidence accuracy, and perplexity. 6) Predictiveness & RAG application: Test whether the internal independence probe predicts the model's behavioral over-persuasion by redundant sources (AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline), and whether discounting-steering improves faithfulness/accuracy on a RAG-style task where the majority of retrieved passages are non-independent copies of a false claim while a minority independent source is correct. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, dependence types (verbatim vs. paraphrase), and report counts. Metrics: independence-probe accuracy/AUROC, belief count vs. independence coefficients and oracle-deviation, internal-vs-verbal gap, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering discounting-restoration and RAG-faithfulness/accuracy deltas, over-persuasion prediction AUROC, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The independence signal may not be internally decodable at all \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs simply count reports and never represent independence) is itself valuable and would sharpen the 'heuristic follower' picture for RAG design. Conversely, if belief already tracks independence appropriately, the interesting finding shifts to characterizing the (smaller-than-expected) illusion \u2014 still informative. Independence might be decoded via surface cues (explicit 'reprinted from A' tags) rather than an abstract representation; we mitigate with diverse framings, paraphrase/order perturbations, comprehension checks, and a condition where independence must be inferred from author/wording diversity rather than an explicit tag. Count and independence can be partially confounded with context length and primacy; matched-N designs, length-matched fillers, and order perturbations dissociate them. The Bayesian oracle is exact only on the synthetic track; real-claim oracles are approximate. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately distrust all repeated evidence (harming legitimate independent consensus); we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, monitor perplexity, independent-evidence accuracy, and general benchmarks. Downstream RAG-faithfulness scoring on open-ended answers needs automatic + human-validated judging. Localization may be distributed; we treat 'steer to restore discounting' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
