Response Content: {"total": 2, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "871504f3bf8c3752dbeb11dd5df7508d8e50317d", "title": "Further N-Frame networking dynamics of conscious observer-self agents via a functional contextual interface: predictive coding, double-slit quantum mechanical experiment, and decision-making fallacy modeling as applied to the measurement problem in humans and AI", "venue": "Frontiers Comput. Neurosci.", "year": 2025, "citationCount": 2, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fncom.2025
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "internal_belief_coherence_gap", "Title": "Rational Inside, Fallacious Out: Is the Conjunction Fallacy a Readout Bug in LLM Belief Representations?", "Short Hypothesis": "The conjunction fallacy and related probabilistic incoherence in LLMs are largely a *verbalization/readout* failure rather than a failure of the model's internal belief representation: a linearly-decoded internal belief-strength readout for conjunctions (P(A\u2227B)) respects probability-theory monotonicity (\u2264 P(A) and \u2264 P(B)) far more often than the model's stated probability judgments do. The internal representation is thus more coherent than the output, the residual internal incoherence is carried by an identifiable component, and steering it can bring stated judgments back into coherence.", "Related Work": "Behavioral work shows LLMs produce incoherent probability judgments mirroring human systematic deviations (Incoherent Probability Judgments in LLMs, Zhu et al. 2024) and commit the conjunction fallacy driven by token bias (A Peek into Token Bias, 2024; Thinking Fast and Slow in LLMs, 2022). Others attempt to *recover* coherent probabilities from embeddings by imposing axiomatic constraints via a trained VAE (Recovering Event Probabilities from LLM Embeddings, 2025) or find LLMs fail to give rational probabilistic beliefs at the output (Exploring the Potential..., 2025). Interpretability work isolates linear truth/belief directions (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024), plausibility/modal-category vectors (Is This Just Fantasy? 2025), and belief-state geometry (Constrained Belief Updates 2025). CRITICALLY, all prior probabilistic-coherence work is either purely behavioral or *engineers* a coherent latent via extra training; none ask whether the model's *own, natively-decoded* belief-strength representation already respects the conjunction rule while its verbal output violates it \u2014 i.e., whether the fallacy is representational or a readout artifact \u2014 nor localize/causally steer the incoherence. We reframe a canonical human-cognition bias as a decodable internal-vs-stated coherence gap and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from all my prior single-belief attribute proposals (this concerns *coherence relations among multiple beliefs*).", "Abstract": "People judge 'Linda is a bank teller and a feminist' as more probable than 'Linda is a bank teller' \u2014 the conjunction fallacy, a signature violation of probability theory. LLMs reproduce this and related incoherences in their stated judgments, and prior work either documents this behaviorally or trains auxiliary models to squeeze coherent probabilities out of embeddings. We ask a sharper, mechanistic question: is the fallacy a bug in the model's internal belief representation, or a bug in how that representation is read out into words? We hypothesize that a linearly-decoded internal belief-strength readout for a conjunction P(A\u2227B) respects the conjunction rule (it is \u2264 the readouts for P(A) and P(B)) substantially more often than the model's verbalized probability judgments do; that the internal representation is therefore more coherent than the output; that the residual internal incoherence is carried by an identifiable component; and that steering it restores coherence in the stated judgments. We construct a controlled dataset of item propositions and their conjunctions/disjunctions (Linda-style representativeness pairs, plus neutral and synthetic-entity conjunctions to control priors and token-bias confounds). On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief-strength probes, compute per-triple internal vs. stated conjunction-rule violation rates, geometrically relate a 'coherence-violation' direction to the truth/plausibility directions, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test whether steering reduces both internal and stated violations. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal vs. stated violation-rate gap, monotonicity error, subspace angle, patching effect sizes, post-steering coherence gains, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Build ~5k proposition triples (A, B, A\u2227B), spanning: (a) REPRESENTATIVENESS pairs in the Linda style where B is representative/typical (the classic fallacy trigger), (b) NEUTRAL pairs where A and B are unrelated (weak representativeness pull), (c) a SYNTHETIC-ENTITY track with novel entities/attributes (e.g., 'Zorblax is a miner' / 'Zorblax is a miner and a poet') to control pretraining priors and to make base rates stipulable, and (d) DISJUNCTION triples (A, B, A\u2228B) to test the dual monotonicity (P(A\u2228B) \u2265 P(A)). Include matched controls that swap which conjunct is representative to separate token/representativeness bias from genuine incoherence, plus paraphrase/format/order perturbations. For synthetic items, stipulate base rates so a ground-truth coherent ordering exists. 2) Stated-judgment baseline: Elicit the model's verbal probability for each of A, B, A\u2227B (and A\u2228B) via direct probability prompts and via forced binary comparison ('which is more probable, A or A\u2227B?'); compute the STATED conjunction-rule violation rate (P(A\u2227B) > P(A)) and disjunction violation rate. Include comprehension checks. 3) Internal belief-strength probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, present each proposition and extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the proposition's key tokens across layers; train a linear belief-STRENGTH probe (regressing a graded belief/plausibility score, calibrated on a held-out set of propositions with known relative probabilities, with shuffled-label control tasks). Read the internal belief-strength for A, B, and A\u2227B WITHOUT asking the comparison question. Define INTERNAL conjunction-rule violation = readout(A\u2227B) > min(readout(A), readout(B)). 4) Key test \u2014 internal vs. stated coherence gap: Compare per-triple internal violation rate to stated violation rate. The core prediction: internal violation rate is significantly LOWER than stated (the representation is more coherent than the output), especially on representativeness pairs. Report the gap per model, per condition (representativeness/neutral/synthetic), and per layer, and test whether the internal readout preserves monotonicity even when the verbal output flips. 5) Geometry: Compute the 'coherence-violation' direction (difference-in-means between violating vs. non-violating conjunctions after controlling for representativeness) and its subspace angle to the truth/plausibility direction (following Is This Just Fantasy? modal vectors); test whether violation is decodable after projecting out plausibility, isolating incoherence from mere typicality. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers whose states inject the representativeness pull that converts a coherent internal readout into an incoherent stated judgment (patch representativeness-pair activations into neutral-pair runs and vice versa); contrast with token-bias-related heads and plausibility/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'coherence' steering direction (coherent vs. violating conjunction activations). Test (a) steering reduces the STATED conjunction/disjunction violation rate toward the internally-implied coherent ordering; (b) negating it exaggerates the fallacy; (c) specificity: random and plausibility-direction steering do not selectively fix coherence; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-conjunction judgment accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Predictiveness & generalization: Test whether the internal readout predicts, per triple, when the model will emit an incoherent stated judgment (AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline); test probe/steering transfer across representativeness/neutral/synthetic tracks and to disjunctions and simple base-rate-neglect items. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal vs. stated violation-rate gap, monotonicity error, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. plausibility, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering violation-rate reduction and specificity, incoherence-prediction AUROC, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The internal belief-strength probe requires a calibrated graded readout, which is harder than a binary truth probe; miscalibration could artificially inflate or deflate the internal violation rate. We mitigate by calibrating on held-out propositions with known relative probabilities, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on the *relative* ordering (monotonicity) rather than absolute probabilities. It is possible the internal representation is *equally* incoherent as the output \u2014 a robust null (no internal-vs-stated gap) would still be a valuable, publishable finding, showing the fallacy is representational, not a readout artifact, and constraining the 'recover coherent probabilities from embeddings' program. The coherence-violation signal may be entangled with the representativeness/plausibility direction (violating conjunctions are exactly the typical ones); the swapped-conjunct controls, neutral pairs, synthetic track, and projected-out analyses are designed to dissociate representativeness pull from genuine incoherence, though full separation is difficult. Reading belief at 'matched' tokens across A, B, and A\u2227B is imperfect since the conjunction is longer; we anchor probes at shared conjunct tokens and use paraphrase perturbations. Localization may be distributed; we treat 'steer to restore coherence' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately rank conjunctions as improbable (harming cases where a conjunction is legitimately near a conjunct); we use small magnitudes, random/plausibility-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-conjunction accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'internal_belief_coherence_gap', 'Title': 'Rational Inside, Fallacious Out: Is the Conjunction Fallacy a Readout Bug in LLM Belief Representations?', 'Short Hypothesis': "The conjunction fallacy and related probabilistic incoherence in LLMs are largely a *verbalization/readout* failure rather than a failure of the model's internal belief representation: a linearly-decoded internal belief-strength readout for conjunctions (P(A∧B)) respects probability-theory monotonicity (≤ P(A) and ≤ P(B)) far more often than the model's stated probability judgments do. The internal representation is thus more coherent than the output, the residual internal incoherence is carried by an identifiable component, and steering it can bring stated judgments back into coherence.", 'Related Work': "Behavioral work shows LLMs produce incoherent probability judgments mirroring human systematic deviations (Incoherent Probability Judgments in LLMs, Zhu et al. 2024) and commit the conjunction fallacy driven by token bias (A Peek into Token Bias, 2024; Thinking Fast and Slow in LLMs, 2022). Others attempt to *recover* coherent probabilities from embeddings by imposing axiomatic constraints via a trained VAE (Recovering Event Probabilities from LLM Embeddings, 2025) or find LLMs fail to give rational probabilistic beliefs at the output (Exploring the Potential..., 2025). Interpretability work isolates linear truth/belief directions (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024), plausibility/modal-category vectors (Is This Just Fantasy? 2025), and belief-state geometry (Constrained Belief Updates 2025). CRITICALLY, all prior probabilistic-coherence work is either purely behavioral or *engineers* a coherent latent via extra training; none ask whether the model's *own, natively-decoded* belief-strength representation already respects the conjunction rule while its verbal output violates it — i.e., whether the fallacy is representational or a readout artifact — nor localize/causally steer the incoherence. We reframe a canonical human-cognition bias as a decodable internal-vs-stated coherence gap and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from all my prior single-belief attribute proposals (this concerns *coherence relations among multiple beliefs*).", 'Abstract': "People judge 'Linda is a bank teller and a feminist' as more probable than 'Linda is a bank teller' — the conjunction fallacy, a signature violation of probability theory. LLMs reproduce this and related incoherences in their stated judgments, and prior work either documents this behaviorally or trains auxiliary models to squeeze coherent probabilities out of embeddings. We ask a sharper, mechanistic question: is the fallacy a bug in the model's internal belief representation, or a bug in how that representation is read out into words? We hypothesize that a linearly-decoded internal belief-strength readout for a conjunction P(A∧B) respects the conjunction rule (it is ≤ the readouts for P(A) and P(B)) substantially more often than the model's verbalized probability judgments do; that the internal representation is therefore more coherent than the output; that the residual internal incoherence is carried by an identifiable component; and that steering it restores coherence in the stated judgments. We construct a controlled dataset of item propositions and their conjunctions/disjunctions (Linda-style representativeness pairs, plus neutral and synthetic-entity conjunctions to control priors and token-bias confounds). On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief-strength probes, compute per-triple internal vs. stated conjunction-rule violation rates, geometrically relate a 'coherence-violation' direction to the truth/plausibility directions, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test whether steering reduces both internal and stated violations. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal vs. stated violation-rate gap, monotonicity error, subspace angle, patching effect sizes, post-steering coherence gains, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Build ~5k proposition triples (A, B, A∧B), spanning: (a) REPRESENTATIVENESS pairs in the Linda style where B is representative/typical (the classic fallacy trigger), (b) NEUTRAL pairs where A and B are unrelated (weak representativeness pull), (c) a SYNTHETIC-ENTITY track with novel entities/attributes (e.g., 'Zorblax is a miner' / 'Zorblax is a miner and a poet') to control pretraining priors and to make base rates stipulable, and (d) DISJUNCTION triples (A, B, A∨B) to test the dual monotonicity (P(A∨B) ≥ P(A)). Include matched controls that swap which conjunct is representative to separate token/representativeness bias from genuine incoherence, plus paraphrase/format/order perturbations. For synthetic items, stipulate base rates so a ground-truth coherent ordering exists. 2) Stated-judgment baseline: Elicit the model's verbal probability for each of A, B, A∧B (and A∨B) via direct probability prompts and via forced binary comparison ('which is more probable, A or A∧B?'); compute the STATED conjunction-rule violation rate (P(A∧B) > P(A)) and disjunction violation rate. Include comprehension checks. 3) Internal belief-strength probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, present each proposition and extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the proposition's key tokens across layers; train a linear belief-STRENGTH probe (regressing a graded belief/plausibility score, calibrated on a held-out set of propositions with known relative probabilities, with shuffled-label control tasks). Read the internal belief-strength for A, B, and A∧B WITHOUT asking the comparison question. Define INTERNAL conjunction-rule violation = readout(A∧B) > min(readout(A), readout(B)). 4) Key test — internal vs. stated coherence gap: Compare per-triple internal violation rate to stated violation rate. The core prediction: internal violation rate is significantly LOWER than stated (the representation is more coherent than the output), especially on representativeness pairs. Report the gap per model, per condition (representativeness/neutral/synthetic), and per layer, and test whether the internal readout preserves monotonicity even when the verbal output flips. 5) Geometry: Compute the 'coherence-violation' direction (difference-in-means between violating vs. non-violating conjunctions after controlling for representativeness) and its subspace angle to the truth/plausibility direction (following Is This Just Fantasy? modal vectors); test whether violation is decodable after projecting out plausibility, isolating incoherence from mere typicality. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers whose states inject the representativeness pull that converts a coherent internal readout into an incoherent stated judgment (patch representativeness-pair activations into neutral-pair runs and vice versa); contrast with token-bias-related heads and plausibility/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'coherence' steering direction (coherent vs. violating conjunction activations). Test (a) steering reduces the STATED conjunction/disjunction violation rate toward the internally-implied coherent ordering; (b) negating it exaggerates the fallacy; (c) specificity: random and plausibility-direction steering do not selectively fix coherence; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-conjunction judgment accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Predictiveness & generalization: Test whether the internal readout predicts, per triple, when the model will emit an incoherent stated judgment (AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline); test probe/steering transfer across representativeness/neutral/synthetic tracks and to disjunctions and simple base-rate-neglect items. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal vs. stated violation-rate gap, monotonicity error, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. plausibility, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering violation-rate reduction and specificity, incoherence-prediction AUROC, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The internal belief-strength probe requires a calibrated graded readout, which is harder than a binary truth probe; miscalibration could artificially inflate or deflate the internal violation rate. We mitigate by calibrating on held-out propositions with known relative probabilities, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on the *relative* ordering (monotonicity) rather than absolute probabilities. It is possible the internal representation is *equally* incoherent as the output — a robust null (no internal-vs-stated gap) would still be a valuable, publishable finding, showing the fallacy is representational, not a readout artifact, and constraining the 'recover coherent probabilities from embeddings' program. The coherence-violation signal may be entangled with the representativeness/plausibility direction (violating conjunctions are exactly the typical ones); the swapped-conjunct controls, neutral pairs, synthetic track, and projected-out analyses are designed to dissociate representativeness pull from genuine incoherence, though full separation is difficult. Reading belief at 'matched' tokens across A, B, and A∧B is imperfect since the conjunction is longer; we anchor probes at shared conjunct tokens and use paraphrase perturbations. Localization may be distributed; we treat 'steer to restore coherence' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately rank conjunctions as improbable (harming cases where a conjunction is legitimately near a conjunct); we use small magnitudes, random/plausibility-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-conjunction accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

