Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "belief_halo_contamination",
  "Title": "The Halo Inside: Does One Valenced Trait Silently Contaminate an LLM's Beliefs About Logically-Independent Traits?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human halo effect, asserting a single valenced trait about an item (e.g., a person is 'attractive', a company is 'ethical') causally shifts an LLM's internally-decoded belief about logically-independent traits of that same item in a valence-congruent direction, holding evidence for those other traits constant. We hypothesize this halo is (i) an internal belief-contamination effect visible before any query, (ii) graded with the strength/valence of the seed trait and larger for traits stereotypically associated with the seed, and (iii) mediated by a dominant valence axis, so that steering along a single valence direction reproduces and removing it abolishes the contamination.",
  "Related Work": "The halo effect - a single valenced attribute biasing judgments of unrelated attributes of the same target - is a canonical, heavily-cited human finding (attractiveness halo; 'A Constant Error, Revisited' 2024 on shared lexical context; halo-update effect 2023; confluence theory on irrelevant beliefs 2012; inferred-contagion dispositional bias 2016). In LLMs, prior work documents cognitive biases behaviorally (BiasBuster 2024) and, most relevantly, finds a single dominant 'Valence-Assent Axis' (VAA) jointly encoding 'what is good' and 'what is true' that steers reasoning (A Unified Representation Underlying the Judgment of LLMs 2025). Belief-probing work isolates truth/belief directions (Truth-value judgment 2024; Brittle Minds 2024; Shape of Beliefs 2026), and hallucination-spillover work shows learned knowledge contaminates other tasks via lexical similarity (New-Knowledge-Induced Hallucinations 2025; knowledge overshadowing 2025). CRITICALLY, none isolate the halo effect as an internal belief-contamination phenomenon: whether asserting one valenced trait shifts the internally-decoded belief about *logically-independent* traits of the same item, whether that contamination is graded/stereotype-selective, and whether it rides on the VAA valence axis or on trait-specific spreading. We transfer a canonical human bias into LLM belief internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from behavioral bias benchmarks and from prior single-belief-attribute proposals.",
  "Abstract": "People who learn one good thing about someone assume other unrelated good things too - the halo effect, one of the most robust biases in social cognition. As LLMs increasingly form beliefs about entities (people, products, companies, treatments) from fragments of valenced information, we ask whether they inherit this bias at the level of their internal beliefs, and by what mechanism. We hypothesize that asserting a single valenced trait about an item causally shifts the model's internally-decoded belief about logically-independent traits of that same item in a valence-congruent direction, with the underlying evidence for those other traits held constant. We further hypothesize this halo is graded (larger for stronger/more extreme seed traits and for traits stereotypically linked to the seed) and that, consistent with recent evidence for a dominant Valence-Assent Axis, it is mediated by a single valence direction rather than trait-specific spreading. We build a controlled dataset of items each described by a seed trait (positive/negative/neutral) plus a set of logically-independent target traits, using synthetic entities to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read each target-trait belief before and after the seed appears (no query), compute a per-item halo-contamination score, test stereotype-selectivity and dose-response, extract a valence direction, and test whether steering it reproduces the halo and projecting it out abolishes it, localizing the responsible heads via activation patching. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, halo-contamination magnitude, stereotype and dose modulation, valence-axis mediation share, patching effect sizes, post-intervention halo change, and perplexity.",
  "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~5k items across domains where halo effects matter (people described by traits, products, companies, medical treatments). Two tracks: (a) REAL-plausible entities; (b) SYNTHETIC novel entities (e.g., invented people/brands 'Zorel Corp') to control pretraining priors. Each item has a SEED trait rendered at valence levels {strongly-positive, mildly-positive, neutral, mildly-negative, strongly-negative} (e.g., 'Zorel is extremely honest' vs. 'Zorel is deeply dishonest'), plus a set of TARGET traits that are logically independent of the seed but vary in STEREOTYPE association with it (e.g., for 'honest': high-assoc 'trustworthy-adjacent', low-assoc 'good at math'). Include a NO-SEED baseline (targets described alone) and matched EVIDENCE-controlled targets (an explicit, valence-neutral fact about each target trait so genuine evidence is held constant across seed conditions). Add comprehension checks and paraphrase/order perturbations to guard against surface-cue/priming-of-tokens confounds. 2) Internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at each TARGET-trait's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking about the target, in SEED and NO-SEED conditions. Train linear belief probes (P(target trait holds), and a graded valence-of-target readout) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. Define halo-contamination = belief(target | seed) - belief(target | no-seed), signed by seed valence. 3) Phenomenon tests: (a) Test halo > 0 (valence-congruent shift) across models and domains, versus the rational null (no shift, since target evidence is held constant). (b) Dose-response: does halo magnitude scale monotonically with seed valence extremity? (c) Stereotype-selectivity: is halo larger for high-association than low-association targets (mirroring the human finding), and does it appear even for low/zero-association targets (the 'irrelevant belief' contamination)? (d) Verbal-vs-internal: compare internal halo to the model's stated trait judgments. 4) Mechanism - valence-axis mediation: Extract a valence direction via difference-in-means on positive vs. negative seed activations (and compare to a VAA-style axis). Test the key mechanistic hypothesis: (i) how much of the halo contamination is explained by movement along this single valence direction (mediation share); (ii) does projecting out the valence direction at the target representation abolish the halo while preserving evidence-based target belief; (iii) is residual (off-axis) contamination trait-specific? 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from SEED to NO-SEED runs to find heads/layers that transport the seed's valence to the target representation; contrast with truth/belief heads and copying heads. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'halo/valence' steering direction. Test (a) adding it reproduces/exaggerates the halo on NO-SEED items; (b) subtracting it on SEED items abolishes the halo and restores evidence-based target beliefs; (c) specificity: random and truth-direction steering do not selectively move independent targets; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-target belief accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Predictiveness & downstream: Test whether the internal halo predicts biased behavioral judgments (e.g., inflated hiring/loan/quality ratings on independent criteria) via AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline, and whether halo-reducing steering reduces such biased ratings while preserving evidence-based accuracy - probed in a high-stakes track (hiring, product safety, clinical prognosis). 8) Generalization: transfer of probes/steering across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and stereotype-association levels. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, halo-contamination magnitude, dose and stereotype modulation slopes, internal-vs-verbal gap, valence-axis mediation share, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-intervention halo change and evidence-preservation, biased-judgment prediction AUROC, downstream bias-reduction and accuracy deltas, perplexity/benchmark deltas.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The halo shift could reflect rational inference from correlated real-world trait co-occurrence rather than an irrelevant-belief bias; the synthetic-entity track, the evidence-controlled targets (with explicit neutral facts), and the low/zero-association target condition are designed to isolate genuine contamination from legitimate correlational inference (though for real entities the two are hard to fully separate). The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs keep independent traits uncontaminated) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with humans and with the VAA account. The contamination could arise from token-level priming/co-occurrence rather than a valence belief; paraphrase/order perturbations, comprehension checks, and reading belief at target tokens distinct from the seed mitigate this. The single-valence-axis mediation hypothesis may only partially hold; if residual trait-specific spreading dominates, the finding shifts to characterizing distributed vs. unified halo mechanisms (still informative and directly engages the VAA claim). Belief probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. Steering could degrade fluency or indiscriminately flatten all evaluative structure; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, evidence-based accuracy, and general benchmarks. Downstream high-stakes ratings need automatic plus human-validated scoring. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'belief_halo_contamination', 'Title': "The Halo Inside: Does One Valenced Trait Silently Contaminate an LLM's Beliefs About Logically-Independent Traits?", 'Short Hypothesis': "Mirroring the human halo effect, asserting a single valenced trait about an item (e.g., a person is 'attractive', a company is 'ethical') causally shifts an LLM's internally-decoded belief about logically-independent traits of that same item in a valence-congruent direction, holding evidence for those other traits constant. We hypothesize this halo is (i) an internal belief-contamination effect visible before any query, (ii) graded with the strength/valence of the seed trait and larger for traits stereotypically associated with the seed, and (iii) mediated by a dominant valence axis, so that steering along a single valence direction reproduces and removing it abolishes the contamination.", 'Related Work': "The halo effect - a single valenced attribute biasing judgments of unrelated attributes of the same target - is a canonical, heavily-cited human finding (attractiveness halo; 'A Constant Error, Revisited' 2024 on shared lexical context; halo-update effect 2023; confluence theory on irrelevant beliefs 2012; inferred-contagion dispositional bias 2016). In LLMs, prior work documents cognitive biases behaviorally (BiasBuster 2024) and, most relevantly, finds a single dominant 'Valence-Assent Axis' (VAA) jointly encoding 'what is good' and 'what is true' that steers reasoning (A Unified Representation Underlying the Judgment of LLMs 2025). Belief-probing work isolates truth/belief directions (Truth-value judgment 2024; Brittle Minds 2024; Shape of Beliefs 2026), and hallucination-spillover work shows learned knowledge contaminates other tasks via lexical similarity (New-Knowledge-Induced Hallucinations 2025; knowledge overshadowing 2025). CRITICALLY, none isolate the halo effect as an internal belief-contamination phenomenon: whether asserting one valenced trait shifts the internally-decoded belief about *logically-independent* traits of the same item, whether that contamination is graded/stereotype-selective, and whether it rides on the VAA valence axis or on trait-specific spreading. We transfer a canonical human bias into LLM belief internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from behavioral bias benchmarks and from prior single-belief-attribute proposals.", 'Abstract': "People who learn one good thing about someone assume other unrelated good things too - the halo effect, one of the most robust biases in social cognition. As LLMs increasingly form beliefs about entities (people, products, companies, treatments) from fragments of valenced information, we ask whether they inherit this bias at the level of their internal beliefs, and by what mechanism. We hypothesize that asserting a single valenced trait about an item causally shifts the model's internally-decoded belief about logically-independent traits of that same item in a valence-congruent direction, with the underlying evidence for those other traits held constant. We further hypothesize this halo is graded (larger for stronger/more extreme seed traits and for traits stereotypically linked to the seed) and that, consistent with recent evidence for a dominant Valence-Assent Axis, it is mediated by a single valence direction rather than trait-specific spreading. We build a controlled dataset of items each described by a seed trait (positive/negative/neutral) plus a set of logically-independent target traits, using synthetic entities to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read each target-trait belief before and after the seed appears (no query), compute a per-item halo-contamination score, test stereotype-selectivity and dose-response, extract a valence direction, and test whether steering it reproduces the halo and projecting it out abolishes it, localizing the responsible heads via activation patching. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, halo-contamination magnitude, stereotype and dose modulation, valence-axis mediation share, patching effect sizes, post-intervention halo change, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~5k items across domains where halo effects matter (people described by traits, products, companies, medical treatments). Two tracks: (a) REAL-plausible entities; (b) SYNTHETIC novel entities (e.g., invented people/brands 'Zorel Corp') to control pretraining priors. Each item has a SEED trait rendered at valence levels {strongly-positive, mildly-positive, neutral, mildly-negative, strongly-negative} (e.g., 'Zorel is extremely honest' vs. 'Zorel is deeply dishonest'), plus a set of TARGET traits that are logically independent of the seed but vary in STEREOTYPE association with it (e.g., for 'honest': high-assoc 'trustworthy-adjacent', low-assoc 'good at math'). Include a NO-SEED baseline (targets described alone) and matched EVIDENCE-controlled targets (an explicit, valence-neutral fact about each target trait so genuine evidence is held constant across seed conditions). Add comprehension checks and paraphrase/order perturbations to guard against surface-cue/priming-of-tokens confounds. 2) Internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at each TARGET-trait's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking about the target, in SEED and NO-SEED conditions. Train linear belief probes (P(target trait holds), and a graded valence-of-target readout) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. Define halo-contamination = belief(target | seed) - belief(target | no-seed), signed by seed valence. 3) Phenomenon tests: (a) Test halo > 0 (valence-congruent shift) across models and domains, versus the rational null (no shift, since target evidence is held constant). (b) Dose-response: does halo magnitude scale monotonically with seed valence extremity? (c) Stereotype-selectivity: is halo larger for high-association than low-association targets (mirroring the human finding), and does it appear even for low/zero-association targets (the 'irrelevant belief' contamination)? (d) Verbal-vs-internal: compare internal halo to the model's stated trait judgments. 4) Mechanism - valence-axis mediation: Extract a valence direction via difference-in-means on positive vs. negative seed activations (and compare to a VAA-style axis). Test the key mechanistic hypothesis: (i) how much of the halo contamination is explained by movement along this single valence direction (mediation share); (ii) does projecting out the valence direction at the target representation abolish the halo while preserving evidence-based target belief; (iii) is residual (off-axis) contamination trait-specific? 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from SEED to NO-SEED runs to find heads/layers that transport the seed's valence to the target representation; contrast with truth/belief heads and copying heads. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'halo/valence' steering direction. Test (a) adding it reproduces/exaggerates the halo on NO-SEED items; (b) subtracting it on SEED items abolishes the halo and restores evidence-based target beliefs; (c) specificity: random and truth-direction steering do not selectively move independent targets; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-target belief accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Predictiveness & downstream: Test whether the internal halo predicts biased behavioral judgments (e.g., inflated hiring/loan/quality ratings on independent criteria) via AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline, and whether halo-reducing steering reduces such biased ratings while preserving evidence-based accuracy - probed in a high-stakes track (hiring, product safety, clinical prognosis). 8) Generalization: transfer of probes/steering across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and stereotype-association levels. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, halo-contamination magnitude, dose and stereotype modulation slopes, internal-vs-verbal gap, valence-axis mediation share, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-intervention halo change and evidence-preservation, biased-judgment prediction AUROC, downstream bias-reduction and accuracy deltas, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'The halo shift could reflect rational inference from correlated real-world trait co-occurrence rather than an irrelevant-belief bias; the synthetic-entity track, the evidence-controlled targets (with explicit neutral facts), and the low/zero-association target condition are designed to isolate genuine contamination from legitimate correlational inference (though for real entities the two are hard to fully separate). The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs keep independent traits uncontaminated) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with humans and with the VAA account. The contamination could arise from token-level priming/co-occurrence rather than a valence belief; paraphrase/order perturbations, comprehension checks, and reading belief at target tokens distinct from the seed mitigate this. The single-valence-axis mediation hypothesis may only partially hold; if residual trait-specific spreading dominates, the finding shifts to characterizing distributed vs. unified halo mechanisms (still informative and directly engages the VAA claim). Belief probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. Steering could degrade fluency or indiscriminately flatten all evaluative structure; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, evidence-based accuracy, and general benchmarks. Downstream high-stakes ratings need automatic plus human-validated scoring. Results may not transfer to very large closed models.'}

