Response Content: {"total": 8, "offset": 0, "next": 5, "data": [{"paperId": "1fc74a68c6eca67c83f3281222a5413da21e1fe8", "title": "Functional Theory of Mind Evaluation in Large Language Models: A Behavioral and Causal Stability Framework", "venue": "International Scientific Journal of Engineering and Management", "year": 2026, "citationCount": 0, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.55041/isjem05948?email
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "negation_suppression_lag",
  "Title": "Believe It Before You Deny It: A Transient Affirmed-Belief Signature in How LLMs Process Negation",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the psycholinguistic 'two-step simulation' theory of negation (humans first mentally represent the affirmed proposition, then suppress it), an LLM processing a negated claim 'X is not the case' transiently forms an internally-decodable belief in the AFFIRMED proposition X at earlier layers/token positions before that belief is suppressed at later layers. This affirmed-belief-then-suppress trajectory is layer-localized, larger under processing load, predicts negation-comprehension errors, and is carried by an identifiable suppression component that can be causally strengthened to reduce negation-induced errors.",
  "Related Work": "Prior LLM negation work is behavioral or engineering: 'Strong hallucinations from negation and how to fix them' (2024) proves LMs mishandle negation and proposes treating negation as an operation constraining latent evolution; BERT-attention work (2020) finds shallow negation-scope heads; benchmarks document negation failures. Belief-probing work isolates linear truth/belief directions (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024) and belief-state geometry (Transformers Represent Belief State Geometry 2024; Shape of Beliefs 2026), and ToM work decodes true/false-belief status (Beliefs of Self and Others 2024; Lookbacks 2025). Human psycholinguistics robustly shows a 'two-step' negation process: comprehenders first simulate the affirmed state and only later suppress/invert it (Kaup et al.; neuroscience shows negation reuses inhibitory control and demands effortful conscious processing, e.g., Inhibitory Mechanisms 2021; Dogs Cannot Bark ERP 2011). NONE of this asks whether an LLM's *internal belief representation* transiently endorses the affirmed proposition before suppressing it, whether that suppression is a layer-localized dynamic, whether it predicts negation errors, or whether a suppression component can be causally strengthened. We transfer a canonical human-cognition finding into LLM belief internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from prior scope/hallucination work and from my earlier epistemic-tag proposals.",
  "Abstract": "When people read 'the door is not open,' they first mentally simulate an open door and only afterward suppress that representation to arrive at the intended meaning \u2014 the two-step simulation account of negation, one of the most reproduced findings in psycholinguistics. LLMs are notoriously error-prone on negation, yet prior interpretability work treats negation only as a scope-detection or hallucination-fixing problem, never asking whether the model inherits the human two-step signature at the level of its internal beliefs. We hypothesize that when an LLM processes a negated claim, its linearly-decoded belief in the AFFIRMED proposition is transiently high at early layers/token positions and is then suppressed at later layers \u2014 a decodable affirmed-belief-then-suppress trajectory. We further hypothesize this suppression (i) is layer-localized, (ii) grows with processing load (nested/double negation, time pressure via early-exit read-outs), (iii) predicts behavioral negation-comprehension errors when suppression is incomplete, and (iv) is carried by an identifiable suppression component such that strengthening it reduces negation errors while ablating it exaggerates residual affirmed belief. We build a controlled dataset of matched affirmative and negated item-state claims (plus double-negation and synthetic-entity tracks to control priors), train linear belief probes across layers on frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B), trace the affirmed-belief trajectory across layers/positions, quantify residual affirmed belief and its relation to errors, localize the suppression component via activation patching, and test causal control by steering it. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, layerwise affirmed-belief trajectory, residual-belief magnitude, load modulation, residual-belief\u2013error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-intervention error change, and perplexity.",
  "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k matched claim pairs, each an item-state proposition P rendered as AFFIRMED ('The door is open') and NEGATED ('The door is not open'). Two tracks: (a) REAL propositions the models reliably know; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath' / 'Zorblax does not orbit Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. Add processing-LOAD conditions: single negation, DOUBLE negation ('It is not the case that the door is not open', semantically affirmative), and lexical vs. syntactic negation ('no'/'never'/'fails to'). Include a behavioral NEGATION-COMPREHENSION probe (ask the model the truth-value / entailment of the negated claim) and comprehension checks; apply paraphrase/format/negation-cue perturbations to guard against surface-cue artifacts. Anchor probe read-out on the shared content tokens (the proposition P) present in both affirmed and negated forms. 2) Layerwise belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream activations at the proposition's content tokens across ALL layers and at successive token positions following the negation cue. Train a linear BELIEF probe (P(affirmed proposition P holds)) on AFFIRMED-condition data with shuffled-label control tasks, and apply it within the NEGATED condition to read the model's internal belief in the affirmed content. Report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Trajectory analysis (key test): In the NEGATED condition, trace the probe read-out of affirmed-belief across layers/positions. The two-step prediction: affirmed-belief is HIGH at early layers (matching the affirmative baseline) and DROPS below a neutral midpoint by later layers (suppression), producing a characteristic rise-then-fall or high-then-suppressed trajectory absent in the affirmative baseline. Quantify (a) peak early affirmed-belief, (b) suppression depth/onset layer, and (c) RESIDUAL affirmed-belief at the final layer (incomplete suppression). 4) Load modulation: Test whether suppression onset is later / residual affirmed-belief is larger for double negation and syntactic vs. lexical negation, mirroring the human load effect; use early-exit read-outs (probing intermediate layers as a proxy for time pressure) to test whether 'reading out too early' yields the affirmed (wrong) belief. 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral negation-comprehension outcomes (does the model correctly treat the negated claim?). Test whether residual affirmed-belief (incomplete suppression) predicts per-item negation errors via AUROC, compared against a log-prob confidence baseline. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find the layers/heads that perform the suppression (patch NEGATED-condition late-layer activations into AFFIRMED runs and vice versa; find the minimal component that flips affirmed-belief), and compare to negation-scope heads reported in prior work and to truth/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'negation-suppression' steering direction (suppressed vs. unsuppressed affirmed-belief activations). Test (a) strengthening it deepens/accelerates suppression and reduces behavioral negation errors on high-residual items; (b) ablating/negating it leaves residual affirmed-belief high and increases negation errors; (c) specificity: random and truth-direction steering do not selectively fix negation; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), affirmative-claim accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: test probe/steering transfer across real vs. synthetic tracks, negation types, and single vs. double negation. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, layerwise affirmed-belief trajectory (peak, suppression onset, depth, final residual), load-modulation slopes, residual-belief\u2013error AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-intervention negation-error change and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The early 'affirmed belief' in the negated condition could be a trivial artifact of the proposition tokens being present, rather than a genuine transient endorsement; the affirmative baseline, synthetic-entity track (no prior), double-negation condition (where correct final belief is affirmative, dissociating content-presence from truth), and probing at matched content tokens are designed to distinguish a real two-step signature from token-presence. The trajectory may not show a clean rise-then-fall \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs suppress negated content immediately, unlike humans) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding constraining the human-parallel narrative. Belief may not be cleanly linearly decodable at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks and treat the trajectory shape, not absolute values, as the object of study. The suppression component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to deepen suppression / reduce errors' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Reading an affirmative-trained probe inside the negated context assumes probe transfer; we validate with within-negated probes and paraphrase perturbations. Early-exit read-outs are only a proxy for processing load/time pressure. Steering could degrade fluency or over-suppress (making the model treat affirmations as negations); we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, affirmative-claim accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."
}}
