g a characteristic rise-then-fall or high-then-suppressed trajectory absent in the affirmative baseline. Quantify (a) peak early affirmed-belief, (b) suppression depth/onset layer, and (c) RESIDUAL affirmed-belief at the final layer (incomplete suppression). 4) Load modulation: Test whether suppression onset is later / residual affirmed-belief is larger for double negation and syntactic vs. lexical negation, mirroring the human load effect; use early-exit read-outs (probing intermediate layers as a proxy for time pressure) to test whether 'reading out too early' yields the affirmed (wrong) belief. 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral negation-comprehension outcomes (does the model correctly treat the negated claim?). Test whether residual affirmed-belief (incomplete suppression) predicts per-item negation errors via AUROC, compared against a log-prob confidence baseline. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find the layers/heads that perform the suppression (patch NEGATED-condition late-layer activations into AFFIRMED runs and vice versa; find the minimal component that flips affirmed-belief), and compare to negation-scope heads reported in prior work and to truth/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'negation-suppression' steering direction (suppressed vs. unsuppressed affirmed-belief activations). Test (a) strengthening it deepens/accelerates suppression and reduces behavioral negation errors on high-residual items; (b) ablating/negating it leaves residual affirmed-belief high and increases negation errors; (c) specificity: random and truth-direction steering do not selectively fix negation; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), affirmative-claim accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: test probe/steering transfer across real vs. synthetic tracks, negation types, and single vs. double negation. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, layerwise affirmed-belief trajectory (peak, suppression onset, depth, f