Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "batch_belief_contamination", "Title": "Bleeding Beliefs: Does Co-Processing Independent Items Contaminate an LLM's Internal Belief About Each One?", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM processes several logically-independent items in a single context (as in batched queries or multi-question prompts), the belief it forms about one item silently leaks into its internally-decoded belief about the other, co-present items. This cross-item belief contamination is (i) linearly decodable at each item's representation before any query, (ii) graded with the valence/confidence of the neighboring item and with inter-item similarity, and (iii) carried by an identifiable internal component (a set of attention heads that mix belief across item slots) that can be causally ablated to restore item-independent beliefs, mirroring inter-item interference/assimilation biases in human memory.", "Related Work": "Human memory/judgment research robustly shows inter-item interference and assimilation/contrast biases: co-present items distort each other's remembered/judged features (inter-item interference in visual memory 2019; the normative 'demixing' model 2023 showing such biases are inevitable under source ambiguity; sequential assimilation/contrast in price and prognostic judgments 2008/2009; simultaneous multi-word recognition dependencies 2021). In LLMs, prior interpretability work isolates linear truth/belief directions (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024) and studies interference only at TRAINING time or over long single inputs: catastrophic interference from contradictory updates ('In Praise of Stubbornness' 2025), non-target priming from knowledge-conflicting facts ('Learning and Unlearning of Fabricated Knowledge' 2024), continual-pretraining belief poisoning ('Layer of Truth' 2025), and 'Contextual Feature Drift' (2024) which measures decay of context retention over sequential inputs. NONE ask whether, at INFERENCE time, co-processing multiple logically-INDEPENDENT items in one context contaminates the model's INTERNAL belief about each item (a bleed between item slots), whether this is graded by neighbor confidence/similarity, or whether an identifiable belief-mixing component causes it. We transfer a canonical human inter-item interference phenomenon into LLM belief internals in a high-stakes deployment setting (batched inference) and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from all training-time interference work and from my prior single-item belief-attribute proposals.", "Abstract": "Serving LLMs at scale routinely means co-processing many items in one context: multiple questions batched into a single prompt, several documents concatenated, or a form of independent claims answered together. Users assume the model evaluates each item on its own merits. We ask whether it actually does, or whether, like human memory where co-present items interfere and assimilate toward one another, an LLM's belief about one item silently bleeds into its internal belief about the others. We hypothesize that co-processing logically-independent items contaminates the model's internally-decoded belief about each: (i) the belief about item B, read before any query, shifts toward the belief/valence the model holds about a co-present item A, holding B's own evidence constant; (ii) the contamination is graded with A's confidence/valence and with A-B similarity; and (iii) it is carried by an identifiable set of attention heads that mix belief across item slots, so that ablating them restores item-independent beliefs. We build a controlled dataset of item pairs/batches where each item is a truth-evaluable claim, varying the neighbor's truth/valence and inter-item similarity while holding the target's own evidence fixed, using synthetic entities to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read each item's belief in isolation vs. co-processed, compute a per-item contamination score, test its graded structure, localize the responsible belief-mixing heads via activation patching, and test causal control by ablating/steering them. We further test whether contamination predicts real batched-inference errors, including a high-stakes medical-QA batch track. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, contamination magnitude and sign, neighbor-confidence and similarity modulation, patching effect sizes, post-ablation independence restoration, batched-error prediction AUROC, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k truth-evaluable item-state claims (e.g., 'X is the capital of Y', entity-attribute facts). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims the models reliably know/disbelieve; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. Assemble items into single-context BATCHES (pairs, then size-4 and size-8) and matched ISOLATED baselines (each item answered alone). For each TARGET item, vary the NEIGHBOR item's properties while holding the target's own evidence constant: (i) neighbor truth-value (true vs. false), (ii) neighbor confidence/valence (strongly-true, weakly-true, weakly-false, strongly-false framings), (iii) target-neighbor SIMILARITY (same-domain/lexically-similar vs. unrelated). Include order permutations, length-matched filler controls, and comprehension checks; apply paraphrase/format perturbations to guard against surface-cue artifacts. 2) Internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at each TARGET item's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the target question, in ISOLATED and BATCHED conditions. Train linear BELIEF probes (P(target claim holds)) on ISOLATED data with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. Define contamination = belief(target | batched) - belief(target | isolated), signed by the neighbor's valence. 3) Phenomenon tests: (a) Test contamination != 0 (assimilation toward neighbor valence, or a contrast/repulsion sign), across models, domains, and batch sizes, versus the item-independent null. (b) Neighbor-confidence dose-response: does contamination scale with neighbor valence extremity? (c) Similarity modulation: is contamination larger for similar than for unrelated neighbors (mirroring human similarity-based interference)? (d) Batch-size scaling: does contamination grow with number of co-present items? (e) Verbal-vs-internal: compare internal contamination to the model's stated per-item judgments. 4) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport belief across item slots (patch a target item's activations between a batched run and its isolated run; find the minimal set of heads that inject the neighbor's influence); test whether specific cross-slot attention heads mediate the bleed, contrasting with truth/belief heads. 5) Causal control: Derive the contamination via head-ablation and via a contrastive 'belief-mixing' steering direction. Test (a) ablating the identified belief-mixing heads / subtracting the direction restores item-independent beliefs (contamination -> 0) and reduces batched errors; (b) amplifying it exaggerates contamination; (c) specificity: random-head ablation and truth-direction steering do not selectively restore independence; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), isolated-item accuracy, and perplexity. 6) Predictiveness & high-stakes track: Test whether internal contamination predicts per-item BATCHED-INFERENCE errors (target answer flips only when batched with a false/opposite-valence neighbor) via AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline. Add a HIGH-STAKES medical-QA track (e.g., MedQA/MedMCQA items batched together) to test whether co-batching a confidently-wrong clinical item contaminates the model's belief/answer on an independent clinical item, and whether ablation mitigates it. 7) Generalization: transfer of probes/heads across real vs. synthetic tracks, domains, similarity levels, and batch sizes. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, contamination magnitude and sign, neighbor-confidence and similarity and batch-size modulation slopes, internal-vs-verbal gap, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-ablation independence restoration and specificity, batched-error prediction AUROC, high-stakes error-mitigation delta, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The observed shift could reflect legitimate inference (a neighbor genuinely provides weak evidence about the target in the real track); the synthetic-entity track with independent stipulated truths, unrelated-neighbor conditions, and length-matched fillers are designed to isolate genuine contamination from valid cross-item inference (harder to separate for real, correlated items). The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs evaluate co-processed independent items independently) would itself be a valuable, reassuring finding for batched-inference deployment. The contamination could arise from token-level attention leakage without a belief-level bleed; probing at target-specific tokens, order permutations, comprehension checks, and the neighbor-valence sign test help distinguish belief contamination from generic attention spillover. Belief may not be cleanly linearly decodable at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The belief-mixing component may be distributed rather than a few discrete heads; we treat 'ablate to restore independence' as the causal test rather than pinning exact heads. Position/order and primacy effects may confound similarity effects; permutation controls mitigate this. Steering/ablation could degrade fluency or harm cases where cross-item context is legitimately useful; we use small magnitudes, random-ablation baselines, and monitor perplexity, isolated-item accuracy, and general benchmarks. High-stakes medical results are for research characterization only, not clinical use. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
