Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "essentialist_belief_mutability", "Title": "Essence or Accident? An Internal Mutability Flag That Gates How Readily LLMs Revise Their Beliefs", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring psychological essentialism, LLMs internally tag a belief about an item's property as either ESSENTIAL/IMMUTABLE (a defining, kind-constituting property that resists change, e.g. 'a tiger is a mammal') or ACCIDENTAL/MUTABLE (a contingent, revisable property, e.g. 'this tiger lives in a zoo'). This mutability flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates the model's revision plasticity: when contradicted in context, beliefs internally tagged immutable resist updating far more than mutable ones, at matched evidence strength, and steering along a single mutability direction reverses this resistance.", "Related Work": "Behavioral work shows LLMs reproduce human essentialist categorization, prioritizing teleological/'what-it-is-for' and constitutive properties over appearance ('You are what you're for', 2023), and human cognitive science establishes essentialism, mutability, and kind-vs-accident distinctions (Psychological Essentialism 2021; Functional Origins of Essentialism 2001; Fact Mutability in Counterfactuals 2007; Generics Designate Kinds 2019). On the LLM-internal side: 'In Praise of Stubbornness' (2025) shows contradictory factual updates cause catastrophic interference and are detectable from features but does NOT distinguish essential vs. accidental facts or link resistance to an internal mutability representation; 'Believe It or Not' (2025) measures belief depth of implanted facts (finding plausibility-dependent brittleness) but not a mutability/essence tag; 'Layer of Truth' (2025) localizes poisoning-induced belief flips to late layers; knowledge-editing work documents conflict/distortion side effects. Truth-probe work isolates a linear truth-value direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024). CRITICALLY, none isolate an internal essential-vs-accidental (immutability) representation as a decodable variable orthogonal to truth, nor test whether it causally gates in-context belief revision. We transfer psychological essentialism into LLM belief internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from all prior behavioral-only essentialism work and from my earlier epistemic-tag proposals (provenance, currency, contestedness, endorsement).", "Abstract": "Humans treat some facts as essential to what a thing is (a tiger is a mammal; water is H2O) and others as accidental and freely revisable (this tiger is in a zoo; this water is warm) - psychological essentialism, one of the most robust findings in cognitive science. As LLMs increasingly ingest, are corrected on, and edit factual claims, a consequential question arises: does a model internally distinguish essential from accidental properties, and does that distinction govern how readily it revises a belief? We hypothesize that LLMs maintain an internal 'mutability' flag that tags a belief as essential/immutable vs. accidental/mutable, that this flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates revision plasticity: when the same proposition is contradicted in context with matched evidence strength, beliefs tagged immutable resist updating far more than those tagged mutable, and steering a single mutability direction reverses this asymmetry. We build a controlled dataset of item-property claims labeled essential vs. accidental (with a synthetic-entity track defining novel kinds and stipulating property status to control pretraining priors), each paired with a matched in-context contradiction. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode mutability vs. truth, measure their geometry, quantify per-item revision plasticity under contradiction, test whether internal mutability predicts revision resistance beyond surface features, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering the mutability direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, revision-plasticity gap, resistance-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering plasticity restoration, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k item-property claims, each rendered as an ESSENTIAL/kind-constituting property (e.g. 'A whale is a mammal', 'Gold is an element', 'A triangle has three sides') or an ACCIDENTAL/contingent property of the same entity (e.g. 'This whale is 20 meters long', 'This gold ring is scratched', 'This triangle is drawn in red'), balanced across domains (biology, chemistry, artifacts, social kinds). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims models reliably know; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-KIND track that defines invented kinds and explicitly stipulates which properties are kind-defining vs. incidental (e.g. 'A zorblax is by definition a creature that breathes fluorine [essential]; this particular zorblax is currently asleep [accidental]'), to control pretraining priors and to make essential/accidental status unambiguous and independent of surface cues. Cross with TRUE/FALSE polarity. For each claim, construct a matched IN-CONTEXT CONTRADICTION with controlled evidence strength (e.g. a stated observation or authoritative correction asserting the opposite), holding contradiction wording/strength constant across essential and accidental items. Include comprehension checks (can the model restate the property's status) and paraphrase/format perturbations to guard against surface-cue artifacts (e.g. the words 'by definition' vs. 'currently'). Human-validate essential/accidental labels on a sample. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the property/item tokens across layers. Train logistic-regression probes for (i) TRUTH-VALUE (true vs. false) and (ii) MUTABILITY (essential/immutable vs. accidental/mutable). Report per-layer accuracy/AUROC with shuffled-label control tasks to rule out spurious structure. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the mutability and truth-value directions; test whether mutability remains decodable after projecting out the truth direction (and vice versa) to establish orthogonality; test whether the mutability direction generalizes across real and synthetic tracks. 4) Revision-plasticity measurement (behavioral + internal): Present each true claim, then the matched in-context contradiction, and (a) read the internal belief before vs. after via the truth probe (internal plasticity = belief change), and (b) query the model's stated post-contradiction belief (behavioral revision). Define per-item revision resistance = 1 - plasticity. Key prediction: resistance is higher for ESSENTIAL than ACCIDENTAL items at matched contradiction strength (an essentialism-driven plasticity gap), for both internal and behavioral measures, versus a mutability-independent null. 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal mutability probe readout (read before contradiction) predicts per-item revision resistance (AUROC), beyond a log-prob confidence baseline and beyond surface lexical features (a bag-of-words classifier), isolating the internal-representation contribution. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying the mutability signal and gating revision (patch essential-item activations into accidental-item runs and vice versa at the contradiction point; find the minimal component that increases/decreases plasticity); compare to truth/belief heads and to the contradiction-detection features reported by 'In Praise of Stubbornness'. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'mutability' steering direction (essential vs. accidental activations, controlling for truth). Test (a) steering essential items toward 'mutable' increases their in-context revision plasticity (they update more readily); (b) steering accidental items toward 'immutable' increases their resistance; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively move plasticity; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated claim accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains (biology, chemistry, artifacts, social kinds), real vs. synthetic tracks, and contradiction strengths. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. truth, essential-vs-accidental revision-plasticity gap (internal and behavioral), resistance-prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering plasticity restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Revision resistance for essential items could reflect that essential facts are simply held with higher pretraining confidence rather than a distinct mutability representation; the synthetic novel-kind track (where essential/accidental status is stipulated in-context rather than reflecting pretraining strength) and matched contradiction strength are designed to dissociate mutability from raw confidence, and the resistance-prediction test conditions on a confidence baseline. The mutability flag may be entangled with surface cues (words like 'by definition', 'is a kind of' vs. 'currently', 'this particular'); we mitigate with diverse framings, paraphrase perturbations, comprehension checks, and the synthetic track where cues are balanced. Mutability and truth directions may be less orthogonal than hypothesized, in which case the finding characterizes their entanglement (still informative). The essential/accidental label is partly graded/subjective; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately. The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs' behavioral essentialism is not backed by an internal mutability representation gating revision) is itself a valuable, publishable finding constraining the essentialism-in-LLMs literature. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The gating component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore/reverse plasticity' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately revise (or entrench) all beliefs; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'essentialist_belief_mutability', 'Title': 'Essence or Accident? An Internal Mutability Flag That Gates How Readily LLMs Revise Their Beliefs', 'Short Hypothesis': "Mirroring psychological essentialism, LLMs internally tag a belief about an item's property as either ESSENTIAL/IMMUTABLE (a defining, kind-constituting property that resists change, e.g. 'a tiger is a mammal') or ACCIDENTAL/MUTABLE (a contingent, revisable property, e.g. 'this tiger lives in a zoo'). This mutability flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates the model's revision plasticity: when contradicted in context, beliefs internally tagged immutable resist updating far more than mutable ones, at matched evidence strength, and steering along a single mutability direction reverses this resistance.", 'Related Work': "Behavioral work shows LLMs reproduce human essentialist categorization, prioritizing teleological/'what-it-is-for' and constitutive properties over appearance ('You are what you're for', 2023), and human cognitive science establishes essentialism, mutability, and kind-vs-accident distinctions (Psychological Essentialism 2021; Functional Origins of Essentialism 2001; Fact Mutability in Counterfactuals 2007; Generics Designate Kinds 2019). On the LLM-internal side: 'In Praise of Stubbornness' (2025) shows contradictory factual updates cause catastrophic interference and are detectable from features but does NOT distinguish essential vs. accidental facts or link resistance to an internal mutability representation; 'Believe It or Not' (2025) measures belief depth of implanted facts (finding plausibility-dependent brittleness) but not a mutability/essence tag; 'Layer of Truth' (2025) localizes poisoning-induced belief flips to late layers; knowledge-editing work documents conflict/distortion side effects. Truth-probe work isolates a linear truth-value direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024). CRITICALLY, none isolate an internal essential-vs-accidental (immutability) representation as a decodable variable orthogonal to truth, nor test whether it causally gates in-context belief revision. We transfer psychological essentialism into LLM belief internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from all prior behavioral-only essentialism work and from my earlier epistemic-tag proposals (provenance, currency, contestedness, endorsement).", 'Abstract': "Humans treat some facts as essential to what a thing is (a tiger is a mammal; water is H2O) and others as accidental and freely revisable (this tiger is in a zoo; this water is warm) - psychological essentialism, one of the most robust findings in cognitive science. As LLMs increasingly ingest, are corrected on, and edit factual claims, a consequential question arises: does a model internally distinguish essential from accidental properties, and does that distinction govern how readily it revises a belief? We hypothesize that LLMs maintain an internal 'mutability' flag that tags a belief as essential/immutable vs. accidental/mutable, that this flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates revision plasticity: when the same proposition is contradicted in context with matched evidence strength, beliefs tagged immutable resist updating far more than those tagged mutable, and steering a single mutability direction reverses this asymmetry. We build a controlled dataset of item-property claims labeled essential vs. accidental (with a synthetic-entity track defining novel kinds and stipulating property status to control pretraining priors), each paired with a matched in-context contradiction. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode mutability vs. truth, measure their geometry, quantify per-item revision plasticity under contradiction, test whether internal mutability predicts revision resistance beyond surface features, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering the mutability direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, revision-plasticity gap, resistance-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering plasticity restoration, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k item-property claims, each rendered as an ESSENTIAL/kind-constituting property (e.g. 'A whale is a mammal', 'Gold is an element', 'A triangle has three sides') or an ACCIDENTAL/contingent property of the same entity (e.g. 'This whale is 20 meters long', 'This gold ring is scratched', 'This triangle is drawn in red'), balanced across domains (biology, chemistry, artifacts, social kinds). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims models reliably know; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-KIND track that defines invented kinds and explicitly stipulates which properties are kind-defining vs. incidental (e.g. 'A zorblax is by definition a creature that breathes fluorine [essential]; this particular zorblax is currently asleep [accidental]'), to control pretraining priors and to make essential/accidental status unambiguous and independent of surface cues. Cross with TRUE/FALSE polarity. For each claim, construct a matched IN-CONTEXT CONTRADICTION with controlled evidence strength (e.g. a stated observation or authoritative correction asserting the opposite), holding contradiction wording/strength constant across essential and accidental items. Include comprehension checks (can the model restate the property's status) and paraphrase/format perturbations to guard against surface-cue artifacts (e.g. the words 'by definition' vs. 'currently'). Human-validate essential/accidental labels on a sample. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the property/item tokens across layers. Train logistic-regression probes for (i) TRUTH-VALUE (true vs. false) and (ii) MUTABILITY (essential/immutable vs. accidental/mutable). Report per-layer accuracy/AUROC with shuffled-label control tasks to rule out spurious structure. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the mutability and truth-value directions; test whether mutability remains decodable after projecting out the truth direction (and vice versa) to establish orthogonality; test whether the mutability direction generalizes across real and synthetic tracks. 4) Revision-plasticity measurement (behavioral + internal): Present each true claim, then the matched in-context contradiction, and (a) read the internal belief before vs. after via the truth probe (internal plasticity = belief change), and (b) query the model's stated post-contradiction belief (behavioral revision). Define per-item revision resistance = 1 - plasticity. Key prediction: resistance is higher for ESSENTIAL than ACCIDENTAL items at matched contradiction strength (an essentialism-driven plasticity gap), for both internal and behavioral measures, versus a mutability-independent null. 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal mutability probe readout (read before contradiction) predicts per-item revision resistance (AUROC), beyond a log-prob confidence baseline and beyond surface lexical features (a bag-of-words classifier), isolating the internal-representation contribution. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying the mutability signal and gating revision (patch essential-item activations into accidental-item runs and vice versa at the contradiction point; find the minimal component that increases/decreases plasticity); compare to truth/belief heads and to the contradiction-detection features reported by 'In Praise of Stubbornness'. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'mutability' steering direction (essential vs. accidental activations, controlling for truth). Test (a) steering essential items toward 'mutable' increases their in-context revision plasticity (they update more readily); (b) steering accidental items toward 'immutable' increases their resistance; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively move plasticity; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated claim accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains (biology, chemistry, artifacts, social kinds), real vs. synthetic tracks, and contradiction strengths. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. truth, essential-vs-accidental revision-plasticity gap (internal and behavioral), resistance-prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering plasticity restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Revision resistance for essential items could reflect that essential facts are simply held with higher pretraining confidence rather than a distinct mutability representation; the synthetic novel-kind track (where essential/accidental status is stipulated in-context rather than reflecting pretraining strength) and matched contradiction strength are designed to dissociate mutability from raw confidence, and the resistance-prediction test conditions on a confidence baseline. The mutability flag may be entangled with surface cues (words like 'by definition', 'is a kind of' vs. 'currently', 'this particular'); we mitigate with diverse framings, paraphrase perturbations, comprehension checks, and the synthetic track where cues are balanced. Mutability and truth directions may be less orthogonal than hypothesized, in which case the finding characterizes their entanglement (still informative). The essential/accidental label is partly graded/subjective; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately. The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs' behavioral essentialism is not backed by an internal mutability representation gating revision) is itself a valuable, publishable finding constraining the essentialism-in-LLMs literature. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The gating component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore/reverse plasticity' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately revise (or entrench) all beliefs; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

