Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "curse_of_knowledge_leakage", "Title": "It Can't Unknow: A Privileged-Knowledge Leak That Biases LLMs' Internal Representation of an Ignorant Belief", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human curse of knowledge / hindsight bias, when an LLM knows the true state of an item, its internally-decoded representation of what an *ignorant* agent (a naive character, a past self, or a stipulated uninformed user) should believe is systematically biased TOWARD the true state that the model privately knows. This 'knowledge leak' is (i) linearly decodable as a shift of the ignorant-belief readout toward the true answer, (ii) graded with the plausibility/salience of the true answer, (iii) predicts egocentric false-belief errors, and (iv) is carried by an identifiable component such that ablating it restores an unbiased ignorant-belief representation.", "Related Work": "ToM interpretability decodes the belief status of a protagonist and an omniscient oracle from attention heads and steers ToM performance (Language Models Represent Beliefs of Self and Others 2024; Brittle Minds 2024), and identifies a 'lookback' mechanism binding character-object-state triples and updating beliefs via a visibility lookback (Language Models use Lookbacks to Track Beliefs 2025). Human cognitive science establishes the curse of knowledge / hindsight bias as a robust egocentric contamination of ignorant-perspective judgments (Are the classic false belief tasks cursed? 2021; Do Adults Show a Curse of Knowledge 2014; Hindsight Bias and Investment Performance 2008), and shows plausibility of the privileged answer modulates its size. CRITICALLY, the ToM interpretability work decodes WHETHER a model can represent a false belief, but none ask whether the model's OWN privileged knowledge of the true answer BIASES the internally-decoded ignorant/false belief toward that truth (a decodable knowledge-leak magnitude), whether that leak is graded by answer plausibility, predicts egocentric errors, or can be causally removed. We reframe the curse of knowledge as an internal representational leak from the truth channel into the ignorant-belief channel and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from prior ToM-decoding work and from my earlier epistemic-tag proposals.", "Abstract": "Once you know the answer, it is hard to imagine not knowing it: people systematically project their privileged knowledge onto less-informed others and onto their own past selves \u2014 the curse of knowledge and its cousin, hindsight bias, among the most robust findings in cognitive science. LLMs are increasingly asked to reason about what an uninformed agent believes (a character in a false-belief story, a novice user, a past state of the world), tasks that require *withholding* what the model itself knows. We ask whether LLMs inherit the curse at the level of their internal beliefs: does the model's own knowledge of the true state of an item leak into, and bias, its internal representation of what an ignorant agent should believe? We hypothesize that (i) the internally-decoded ignorant-belief readout is shifted toward the true answer the model privately knows, (ii) this leak grows with the plausibility/salience of the true answer, (iii) it predicts egocentric false-belief errors when suppression is incomplete, and (iv) it is carried by an identifiable component that can be ablated to restore an unbiased ignorant-belief representation. We build controlled false-belief and hindsight scenarios in which the true state and the ignorant agent's evidence are dissociated, with matched conditions where the model is or is not itself told the truth, plus a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to read the ignorant-belief and the true-state, quantify a per-item knowledge-leak score, test its graded structure and predictiveness, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by ablating/steering a leak direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, knowledge-leak magnitude, plausibility modulation, leak\u2013error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-ablation debiasing, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k scenarios in which the TRUE state of an item and an IGNORANT agent's evidence are dissociated, spanning three framings: (a) classic false-belief stories (an object is moved while a character is absent; the character should believe the old location), (b) hindsight scenarios (an outcome is revealed; the model must judge what a naive predictor would have expected beforehand), (c) audience-ignorance (a novice user who has not been told the fact). Two tracks: (i) REAL items models reliably know, and (ii) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (e.g., 'Zorblax's ball was moved from box A to box B while Zorblax was away') to control pretraining priors and stipulate ground truth. KEY manipulation: matched conditions where the model itself IS vs. IS NOT told the true state (privileged-knowledge present vs. absent), holding the ignorant agent's evidence constant \u2014 this isolates leakage from the model's own knowledge. Vary the PLAUSIBILITY/salience of the true answer (how strongly the true state pulls, e.g. a highly typical vs. arbitrary new location) to test the human plausibility-modulation finding. Add a behavioral FALSE-BELIEF/hindsight probe (ask what the ignorant agent believes / would have predicted) and comprehension checks (can the model restate who saw what); apply paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. Human-validate a sample. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the item/state tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the belief question. Train linear probes for (A) the TRUE state and (B) the IGNORANT agent's belief (which, under a false belief, should point AWAY from the true state), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. Define per-item KNOWLEDGE-LEAK = shift of the ignorant-belief readout toward the true state in the privileged-knowledge-present vs. -absent condition (equivalently, contamination of channel B by the true state carried in channel A). 3) Phenomenon tests: (a) Test leak > 0 (ignorant-belief biased toward the truth) across models and framings, versus the unbiased null; (b) plausibility dose-response: does leak scale with the true answer's salience? (c) does leak appear even in the SYNTHETIC track where the true state is only known in-context (isolating in-context knowledge leak from parametric priors)? (d) verbal-vs-internal: compare internal leak to stated ignorant-belief judgments. 4) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral egocentric errors (model wrongly attributes the true state to the ignorant agent / hindsight-distorted prior expectation). Test whether internal leak (read before the query) predicts per-item egocentric errors (AUROC), beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface features. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport the true-state signal into the ignorant-belief channel (patch privileged-knowledge-present activations into -absent runs and vice versa; find the minimal component that induces/removes the leak); compare to the belief/visibility-lookback heads and truth heads reported in prior ToM work. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'knowledge-leak' steering direction (privileged-present vs. -absent activations, controlling for the ignorant agent's evidence). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores an unbiased ignorant-belief readout and reduces egocentric errors; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the curse; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively debias the ignorant channel, and the model's OWN factual belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across framings (false-belief, hindsight, audience-ignorance), real vs. synthetic tracks, and plausibility levels. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, knowledge-leak magnitude, plausibility-modulation slope, internal-vs-verbal gap, leak\u2013error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-ablation debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The leak could reflect legitimate inference (an ignorant agent might rationally still guess the true state if it is the default); the false-belief design (where the ignorant agent has POSITIVE evidence for the old/false state), the synthetic track, and the privileged-present-vs-absent contrast are built to isolate genuine contamination from valid inference, though for highly plausible true answers the two are hard to fully separate. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs cleanly firewall their own knowledge from the ignorant-belief channel) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding, refining the ToM-representation and safety picture. The leak could arise from token-level co-occurrence rather than a belief-channel contamination; comprehension checks, paraphrase/order perturbations, and reading belief at agent-specific tokens mitigate this. The two channels (true-state and ignorant-belief) may be entangled such that the 'leak' is really imperfect channel separation \u2014 still an informative characterization. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The leak component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'ablate to debias' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model unable to reason about true beliefs at all (over-suppression); we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, the model's own factual accuracy, and general benchmarks. Plausibility labels are partly subjective; we human-validate a sample. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
