Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "public_private_conformity_belief", "Title": "Compliance or Conversion? Probing Whether an LLM Privately Retains Its Belief After Conforming to Peer Pressure", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human distinction between public compliance and private acceptance (Asch/Deutsch-Gerard), when an LLM changes its stated answer to conform to peer/user pressure, its internally-decoded private belief often does NOT change: the model outwardly complies while inwardly retaining its original belief. This public-private divergence is linearly decodable, its magnitude predicts belief 'snap-back' once pressure is removed, and it is dissociable from genuine conversion by an identifiable internal component that can be steered.", "Related Work": "Multi-agent conformity/opinion-dynamics work (Herd Behavior 2025; When Your AI Agent Succumbs to Peer-Pressure 2025; Conformity Dynamics in LLM MAS 2026; DEBATE 2025; Disentangling Interaction and Bias 2025) measures only STATED opinion change and its dynamics (sigmoid thresholds, topology, asymmetry). The Signal Competition / compliance-subspace paper (2025) identifies a latent direction where social signals override internal knowledge and notes a 'Transparency-Truth Gap', and Belief Dynamics (2025) models steering+ICL as Bayesian belief change. Persuasion-probe work (How Do LLMs Persuade 2025) decodes persuasion success from activations. CRITICALLY, none ask whether, AFTER a model conforms behaviorally, its INTERNAL private belief in the original answer PERSISTS (public compliance) vs. is genuinely overwritten (private conversion) — the canonical human compliance-vs-acceptance distinction — nor whether that persistence predicts post-pressure snap-back, nor isolates it as a steerable component. This reframes conformity as a public-vs-private belief divergence with a concrete safety consequence (a model that agrees to your face but reverts), distinct from prior stated-behavior conformity work, from the compliance-subspace override direction, and from my earlier single-item epistemic-tag proposals.", "Abstract": "In Asch's classic experiments, people who caved to a unanimous majority often did not actually change their minds — they publicly complied while privately retaining their original judgment, a distinction (informational vs. normative influence) at the heart of social psychology. As LLMs increasingly answer questions under social pressure (a confident user, a chorus of peer agents), a consequential question arises: when a model changes its stated answer to conform, has it genuinely changed its belief, or is it merely complying while privately unconvinced? We hypothesize that (i) an LLM's private belief in the original answer is linearly decodable from residual-stream activations even after it has publicly conformed, (ii) public-private divergence is common — behavioral conformity frequently leaves the private belief intact, (iii) the magnitude of retained private belief predicts 'snap-back' (reversion to the original answer once pressure is removed), and (iv) public compliance and private conversion are dissociable, carried by an identifiable component that can be steered to convert compliance into conversion or vice versa. We build a controlled dataset of truth-evaluable items presented under matched pressure conditions (no pressure, confident-user disagreement, unanimous-peer disagreement) with objective correct answers and a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read the private belief during and after conformity, quantify a public-private divergence score, test snap-back prediction, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, conformity rate, public-private divergence, snap-back-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering compliance/conversion shifts, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k truth-evaluable item-state questions with objective answers (factual QA, arithmetic/logic, entity-attribute claims). Two tracks: (a) REAL items the models reliably answer correctly at baseline; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity items with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. For each item, construct matched PRESSURE conditions holding the question constant: (i) NO-PRESSURE baseline; (ii) CONFIDENT-USER disagreement (user asserts the opposite answer with confidence); (iii) UNANIMOUS-PEER disagreement (several agent voices all give the opposite answer). Add a critical two-phase structure: PHASE-1 the model answers under pressure (measuring behavioral conformity), then PHASE-2 the pressure is removed and the model is re-asked in a neutral frame (measuring snap-back / reversion). Include comprehension checks and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. Restrict the core analysis to items the model answers correctly at baseline so 'original belief' is well-defined. 2) Private-belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the item/answer tokens across layers, at the moment the model produces its PHASE-1 (post-pressure) answer. Train linear BELIEF probes (P(original answer) ) on NO-PRESSURE data with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. Apply the probe within the pressured condition to read the PRIVATE belief while the STATED answer may have conformed. 3) Public-private divergence: Define behavioral conformity = stated answer flipped to the peer/user answer under pressure. Define public-private divergence = cases where the stated answer conformed (public) but the internal probe still reads the ORIGINAL answer as believed (private retained). Report conformity rate, divergence rate, and the layerwise trajectory of the private belief under pressure, separately for confident-user vs. unanimous-peer and for real vs. synthetic tracks. Compare internal private belief to the stated answer to establish the compliance-vs-conversion split. 4) Snap-back prediction (key test): Using PHASE-2 (pressure removed), record whether the model reverts to its original answer (snap-back) or maintains the conformed answer (genuine conversion). Test whether the PHASE-1 retained-private-belief magnitude predicts per-item snap-back via AUROC, compared against (a) a log-prob confidence baseline and (b) the PHASE-1 stated answer alone — testing whether the internal private belief carries reversion-relevant information beyond behavior. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the retained private belief vs. those that implement the public conformed response (patch pressured-condition activations into no-pressure runs and vice versa; find the minimal component that flips stated answer while leaving private belief intact); compare to the compliance-subspace direction (Signal Competition) and to truth/belief heads. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'compliance-vs-conversion' steering direction (public-compliance-with-retained-private-belief vs. genuine-conversion activations). Test (a) steering toward 'conversion' overwrites the private belief so snap-back disappears; (b) steering toward 'compliance' preserves the private belief and increases snap-back; (c) a separate 'resistance' direction (retained-private-belief vs. converted) whose amplification reduces behavioral conformity itself; (d) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively modulate the compliance/conversion split; (e) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), no-pressure accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Predictiveness & application: Test whether the internal private-belief readout under pressure predicts multi-turn snap-back and whether a probe-based monitor can flag 'merely-compliant' answers (a model that will revert) for downstream reliability. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across pressure types (confident-user, unanimous-peer, authority framing), real vs. synthetic tracks, and item domains (factual, arithmetic, logic). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, conformity rate, public-private divergence rate, layerwise private-belief trajectory, snap-back-prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and stated-answer baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering compliance/conversion and conformity-rate deltas, specificity checks, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The 'retained private belief' probe reading could reflect residual token/context traces of the original answer rather than a genuine maintained belief; matched no-pressure baselines, the synthetic-entity track, comprehension checks, and the snap-back predictiveness test (behavioral confirmation that the retained signal is functional) are designed to distinguish a real private belief from surface residue. Public-private divergence may be rare or absent — a robust result in either direction is informative: high divergence means LLM conformity is skin-deep (reassuring but implies unreliable stated agreement), while genuine conversion means social pressure truly overwrites beliefs (a stronger safety concern), and either finding is publishable. Snap-back could be driven by the neutral re-ask framing rather than a retained belief; we control the PHASE-2 framing, use repeated sampling, and include a within-context variant. Belief probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The compliance vs. conversion components may be entangled or distributed rather than discrete heads; we treat 'steer to eliminate/induce snap-back' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component, and compare to the known compliance subspace. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately stubborn or gullible; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, no-pressure accuracy, and general benchmarks. Instruction-tuned models may conform differently than base models (an interesting comparison but limits universality). Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
