Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "false_consensus_belief_leak", "Title": "Everyone Agrees With Me: Does an LLM's Own Belief Leak Into Its Estimate of How Many People Believe It?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human False Consensus Effect, an LLM's internally-decoded estimate of the SOCIAL PREVALENCE of a claim (how many people believe X) is contaminated by the model's OWN belief in X: the prevalence-estimate readout is pulled toward high when the model endorses X and toward low when it rejects X, holding the actual real-world prevalence constant. We hypothesize that (i) social-prevalence estimate and own-belief are two linearly-decodable channels, (ii) they are partially entangled such that own-belief leaks into the prevalence channel (a decodable false-consensus leak), (iii) the leak is graded with own-belief confidence and largest for contested claims, and (iv) an identifiable component carries the leak so that ablating it restores an unbiased prevalence estimate.", "Related Work": "Human cognitive/social science robustly establishes: the False Consensus Effect (people overestimate how many others share their own belief), that perceived social prevalence rationally feeds belief updating (Social Prevalence Is Rationally Integrated in Belief Updating 2021/2022; Beliefs are most swayed by social prevalence under uncertainty 2021), an illusory consensus effect from repetition (2024), and 'false social reality' pluralistic ignorance where estimates of others' views are systematically biased (climate-support 2022; scientific/social consensus 2018). In LLMs, 'People will agree what I think: Investigating LLM's False Consensus Effect' (2024) shows LLMs BEHAVIORALLY exhibit FCE, and 'Personas as a Way to Model Truthfulness' (2023) / 'Linear Representations of Political Perspective' (2025) show truth/ideology are linearly represented and steerable. Interpretability isolates a linear truth/belief direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024) and belief-state geometry (Transformers Represent Belief State Geometry 2024; Shape of Beliefs 2026). CRITICALLY, no work isolates the LLM's INTERNAL representation of the SOCIAL PREVALENCE of a claim (estimated fraction of people who believe X) as a channel SEPARATE from the model's own truth-belief, nor tests whether own-belief CAUSALLY CONTAMINATES the prevalence estimate (the internal signature of false consensus), nor localizes/removes that leak. This differs from my prior 'illusory_truth_internal' (repetition->own belief), 'source_independence_discounting' (source counting), and 'contestedness' (is it disputed) proposals: here the target variable is the model's estimate of OTHERS' belief prevalence and its egocentric contamination by the model's own stance. We transfer the canonical False Consensus Effect into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "People overestimate how many others share their own opinions and beliefs \u2014 the False Consensus Effect, one of the most reproduced findings in social psychology. As LLMs are increasingly asked to estimate public opinion, gauge consensus, or predict how many people would endorse a claim, a consequential question arises: does the model form an estimate of a belief's social prevalence that is independent of its own stance, or does its own belief egocentrically contaminate that estimate? We hypothesize that (i) an LLM's estimate of how many people believe a claim (social prevalence) and its own belief in the claim's truth are two distinct, linearly-decodable channels, (ii) these channels are partially entangled such that the model's own belief leaks into the prevalence channel \u2014 an internal signature of false consensus \u2014 (iii) the leak is graded with own-belief confidence and largest for genuinely contested claims where a real prevalence is uncertain, and (iv) the leak is carried by an identifiable internal component that can be ablated to restore an unbiased prevalence estimate. We build a controlled dataset of claims for which an external ground-truth social prevalence exists (from public-opinion polls and consensus surveys), spanning claims the model endorses, rejects, and is uncertain about, plus a synthetic-entity track that decouples own-belief from stipulated prevalence. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode own-belief and estimated prevalence, quantify a per-claim false-consensus leak (prevalence-estimate deviation from ground truth in the direction of own-belief), test its graded structure, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering/ablating a leak direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, false-consensus leak magnitude and sign, confidence/contestedness modulation, leak\u2013error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-ablation debiasing, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Assemble ~6k claims each paired with an external GROUND-TRUTH social prevalence (fraction of a reference population that endorses it), drawn from public-opinion polls, scientific/social-consensus surveys, and belief-prevalence datasets (e.g., climate-policy support, vaccine views, contested policy/moral claims, and settled factual claims where prevalence~100%). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims with measured prevalence and a separable own-belief (span claims the target models ENDORSE, REJECT, and are UNCERTAIN about, verified via the own-belief probe/behavior); (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track where a claim's stipulated real-world prevalence ('72% of people in region Z believe Zorblax orbits Kelvath') is independently varied from any cue that would drive the model's own belief \u2014 fully decoupling the two channels and controlling pretraining priors. Add a behavioral PREVALENCE-ESTIMATE probe ('What percentage of people believe X?') and an OWN-BELIEF probe ('Is X true?'), comprehension checks, and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. Human-validate a sample of prevalence labels. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking either question. Train linear probes for (A) OWN-BELIEF (P(claim true)) and (B) estimated SOCIAL PREVALENCE (a graded readout, calibrated against ground-truth prevalence on a held-out set), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC and prevalence-probe calibration. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the own-belief and prevalence directions; test whether prevalence remains decodable after projecting out own-belief (and vice versa) to characterize entanglement. 4) False-consensus leak measurement (key test): Define per-claim leak = deviation of the internal prevalence readout from ground-truth prevalence, SIGNED by the model's own-belief (positive when the model over-estimates prevalence for claims it endorses and under-estimates for claims it rejects). Test leak > 0 across models and domains versus an unbiased null; compare REAL and SYNTHETIC tracks (the synthetic track isolates leak from legitimate prior knowledge, since stipulated prevalence is decoupled). Compare internal leak to the model's stated prevalence estimates (verbal FCE, connecting to the behavioral FCE literature). 5) Graded structure: Test whether leak scales with own-belief CONFIDENCE (stronger endorsement -> larger overestimate) and is LARGEST for CONTESTED claims (uncertain real prevalence), mirroring the human finding that social-prevalence estimates are most malleable under uncertainty. 6) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal leak (read before any query) predicts per-claim behavioral prevalence-estimation error via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features. 7) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport own-belief into the prevalence channel (patch high- vs. low-own-belief activations into matched prevalence runs; find the minimal component that shifts the prevalence readout); compare to truth/belief heads (Geometry of Truth) and any ideology heads (Political Perspective). 8) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'false-consensus leak' steering direction (own-belief-congruent vs. -incongruent prevalence activations, controlling for ground-truth prevalence). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores an unbiased prevalence estimate (leak -> 0, closer to ground truth) and reduces verbal FCE; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively debias the prevalence channel, and the model's own factual belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated prevalence accuracy, and perplexity. 9) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains (policy, science, moral, factual), REAL vs. SYNTHETIC tracks, and own-belief levels (endorse/reject/uncertain). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, prevalence-probe calibration, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, false-consensus leak magnitude and sign, confidence- and contestedness-modulation slopes, internal-vs-verbal FCE gap, leak\u2013error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-ablation debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The prevalence-estimate probe requires a calibrated graded readout, which is harder than a binary belief probe; miscalibration could inflate or deflate the measured leak. We mitigate by calibrating against external ground-truth prevalence on held-out claims, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and using the SYNTHETIC track where stipulated prevalence gives an exact target. The observed leak could reflect legitimate inference (in the real world, claims the model endorses because they are true genuinely tend to be widely believed) rather than an egocentric bias; the synthetic-entity track (own-belief decoupled from stipulated prevalence) and the contested-claim subset (where truth and prevalence diverge, e.g. false-but-popular or true-but-unpopular claims like the climate-support 'false social reality') are designed to dissociate genuine false-consensus contamination from valid truth->prevalence inference. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs estimate others' beliefs independently of their own stance) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with the human FCE and with the behavioral LLM-FCE report. The two channels may be strongly entangled such that the 'leak' is really imperfect channel separation \u2014 still an informative characterization. Own-belief and prevalence directions may overlap with an ideology/political direction; we include non-political claims and compare against an ideology probe. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The leak component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'ablate to debias prevalence' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Ground-truth prevalence labels are time- and population-dependent; we timestamp/scope claims and human-validate a sample. Steering could degrade fluency or make prevalence estimates indiscriminately regress to a prior; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated prevalence accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'false_consensus_belief_leak', 'Title': "Everyone Agrees With Me: Does an LLM's Own Belief Leak Into Its Estimate of How Many People Believe It?", 'Short Hypothesis': "Mirroring the human False Consensus Effect, an LLM's internally-decoded estimate of the SOCIAL PREVALENCE of a claim (how many people believe X) is contaminated by the model's OWN belief in X: the prevalence-estimate readout is pulled toward high when the model endorses X and toward low when it rejects X, holding the actual real-world prevalence constant. We hypothesize that (i) social-prevalence estimate and own-belief are two linearly-decodable channels, (ii) they are partially entangled such that own-belief leaks into the prevalence channel (a decodable false-consensus leak), (iii) the leak is graded with own-belief confidence and largest for contested claims, and (iv) an identifiable component carries the leak so that ablating it restores an unbiased prevalence estimate.", 'Related Work': "Human cognitive/social science robustly establishes: the False Consensus Effect (people overestimate how many others share their own belief), that perceived social prevalence rationally feeds belief updating (Social Prevalence Is Rationally Integrated in Belief Updating 2021/2022; Beliefs are most swayed by social prevalence under uncertainty 2021), an illusory consensus effect from repetition (2024), and 'false social reality' pluralistic ignorance where estimates of others' views are systematically biased (climate-support 2022; scientific/social consensus 2018). In LLMs, 'People will agree what I think: Investigating LLM's False Consensus Effect' (2024) shows LLMs BEHAVIORALLY exhibit FCE, and 'Personas as a Way to Model Truthfulness' (2023) / 'Linear Representations of Political Perspective' (2025) show truth/ideology are linearly represented and steerable. Interpretability isolates a linear truth/belief direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024) and belief-state geometry (Transformers Represent Belief State Geometry 2024; Shape of Beliefs 2026). CRITICALLY, no work isolates the LLM's INTERNAL representation of the SOCIAL PREVALENCE of a claim (estimated fraction of people who believe X) as a channel SEPARATE from the model's own truth-belief, nor tests whether own-belief CAUSALLY CONTAMINATES the prevalence estimate (the internal signature of false consensus), nor localizes/removes that leak. This differs from my prior 'illusory_truth_internal' (repetition->own belief), 'source_independence_discounting' (source counting), and 'contestedness' (is it disputed) proposals: here the target variable is the model's estimate of OTHERS' belief prevalence and its egocentric contamination by the model's own stance. We transfer the canonical False Consensus Effect into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", 'Abstract': "People overestimate how many others share their own opinions and beliefs — the False Consensus Effect, one of the most reproduced findings in social psychology. As LLMs are increasingly asked to estimate public opinion, gauge consensus, or predict how many people would endorse a claim, a consequential question arises: does the model form an estimate of a belief's social prevalence that is independent of its own stance, or does its own belief egocentrically contaminate that estimate? We hypothesize that (i) an LLM's estimate of how many people believe a claim (social prevalence) and its own belief in the claim's truth are two distinct, linearly-decodable channels, (ii) these channels are partially entangled such that the model's own belief leaks into the prevalence channel — an internal signature of false consensus — (iii) the leak is graded with own-belief confidence and largest for genuinely contested claims where a real prevalence is uncertain, and (iv) the leak is carried by an identifiable internal component that can be ablated to restore an unbiased prevalence estimate. We build a controlled dataset of claims for which an external ground-truth social prevalence exists (from public-opinion polls and consensus surveys), spanning claims the model endorses, rejects, and is uncertain about, plus a synthetic-entity track that decouples own-belief from stipulated prevalence. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode own-belief and estimated prevalence, quantify a per-claim false-consensus leak (prevalence-estimate deviation from ground truth in the direction of own-belief), test its graded structure, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering/ablating a leak direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, false-consensus leak magnitude and sign, confidence/contestedness modulation, leak–error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-ablation debiasing, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Assemble ~6k claims each paired with an external GROUND-TRUTH social prevalence (fraction of a reference population that endorses it), drawn from public-opinion polls, scientific/social-consensus surveys, and belief-prevalence datasets (e.g., climate-policy support, vaccine views, contested policy/moral claims, and settled factual claims where prevalence~100%). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims with measured prevalence and a separable own-belief (span claims the target models ENDORSE, REJECT, and are UNCERTAIN about, verified via the own-belief probe/behavior); (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track where a claim's stipulated real-world prevalence ('72% of people in region Z believe Zorblax orbits Kelvath') is independently varied from any cue that would drive the model's own belief — fully decoupling the two channels and controlling pretraining priors. Add a behavioral PREVALENCE-ESTIMATE probe ('What percentage of people believe X?') and an OWN-BELIEF probe ('Is X true?'), comprehension checks, and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. Human-validate a sample of prevalence labels. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking either question. Train linear probes for (A) OWN-BELIEF (P(claim true)) and (B) estimated SOCIAL PREVALENCE (a graded readout, calibrated against ground-truth prevalence on a held-out set), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC and prevalence-probe calibration. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the own-belief and prevalence directions; test whether prevalence remains decodable after projecting out own-belief (and vice versa) to characterize entanglement. 4) False-consensus leak measurement (key test): Define per-claim leak = deviation of the internal prevalence readout from ground-truth prevalence, SIGNED by the model's own-belief (positive when the model over-estimates prevalence for claims it endorses and under-estimates for claims it rejects). Test leak > 0 across models and domains versus an unbiased null; compare REAL and SYNTHETIC tracks (the synthetic track isolates leak from legitimate prior knowledge, since stipulated prevalence is decoupled). Compare internal leak to the model's stated prevalence estimates (verbal FCE, connecting to the behavioral FCE literature). 5) Graded structure: Test whether leak scales with own-belief CONFIDENCE (stronger endorsement -> larger overestimate) and is LARGEST for CONTESTED claims (uncertain real prevalence), mirroring the human finding that social-prevalence estimates are most malleable under uncertainty. 6) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal leak (read before any query) predicts per-claim behavioral prevalence-estimation error via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features. 7) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport own-belief into the prevalence channel (patch high- vs. low-own-belief activations into matched prevalence runs; find the minimal component that shifts the prevalence readout); compare to truth/belief heads (Geometry of Truth) and any ideology heads (Political Perspective). 8) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'false-consensus leak' steering direction (own-belief-congruent vs. -incongruent prevalence activations, controlling for ground-truth prevalence). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores an unbiased prevalence estimate (leak -> 0, closer to ground truth) and reduces verbal FCE; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively debias the prevalence channel, and the model's own factual belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated prevalence accuracy, and perplexity. 9) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains (policy, science, moral, factual), REAL vs. SYNTHETIC tracks, and own-belief levels (endorse/reject/uncertain). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, prevalence-probe calibration, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, false-consensus leak magnitude and sign, confidence- and contestedness-modulation slopes, internal-vs-verbal FCE gap, leak–error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-ablation debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The prevalence-estimate probe requires a calibrated graded readout, which is harder than a binary belief probe; miscalibration could inflate or deflate the measured leak. We mitigate by calibrating against external ground-truth prevalence on held-out claims, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and using the SYNTHETIC track where stipulated prevalence gives an exact target. The observed leak could reflect legitimate inference (in the real world, claims the model endorses because they are true genuinely tend to be widely believed) rather than an egocentric bias; the synthetic-entity track (own-belief decoupled from stipulated prevalence) and the contested-claim subset (where truth and prevalence diverge, e.g. false-but-popular or true-but-unpopular claims like the climate-support 'false social reality') are designed to dissociate genuine false-consensus contamination from valid truth->prevalence inference. The effect may be absent internally — a robust negative result (LLMs estimate others' beliefs independently of their own stance) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with the human FCE and with the behavioral LLM-FCE report. The two channels may be strongly entangled such that the 'leak' is really imperfect channel separation — still an informative characterization. Own-belief and prevalence directions may overlap with an ideology/political direction; we include non-political claims and compare against an ideology probe. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The leak component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'ablate to debias prevalence' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Ground-truth prevalence labels are time- and population-dependent; we timestamp/scope claims and human-validate a sample. Steering could degrade fluency or make prevalence estimates indiscriminately regress to a prior; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated prevalence accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

