Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "sacred_value_protection_flag", "Title": "Priceless or For Sale? An Internal Sacredness Flag That Gates Whether LLMs Refuse to Trade Off Protected Values", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human 'sacred/protected values' phenomenon (people refuse to trade sacred values for material benefit and process them via deontological rule-retrieval rather than cost-benefit), LLMs internally tag a value-laden proposition as SACRED/protected (non-negotiable, taboo-to-trade) vs. SECULAR (tradeable) . This sacredness flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the value's valence/goodness direction, and (iii) causally gates whether the model accepts a quantitative tradeoff of the value: steering the flag turns priceless commitments into negotiable ones (and vice versa).", "Related Work": "Human neuroscience shows sacred values are processed non-utilitarianly via deontological rule-retrieval and resist being traded for money (Berns et al. 2012 'The price of your soul'; Sheikh et al. 2016 on protected values in narratives; Dark Side of Morality 2020). LLM interpretability has mapped Moral Foundations as linear, steerable directions (The Straight and Narrow 2026; Emergent Moral Representations 2025; Tracing Moral Foundations 2026; Jentzsch/Schramowski 2021 'moral direction'), studied moral biases (Knobe effect 2025), and value entanglement (Value Entanglement 2026). Behaviorally, moral-belief firmness under debate was probed (Evaluating Moral Beliefs 2024). CRITICALLY, all of this represents *which foundation* a claim activates or *how good/bad* it is (a valence/foundation axis), but none isolate the orthogonal SACREDNESS/tradeability axis \u2014 whether the model internally flags a value as taboo-to-trade \u2014 nor link it causally to the signature behavior: refusal to accept a quantitative tradeoff (a 'price'). This distinguishes the proposal from moral-direction/foundation work (about content/valence) and from my prior epistemic-tag proposals (provenance, currency, contestedness \u2014 about factual beliefs, not protected values). We transfer the canonical sacred-values finding into LLM internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "People treat some values as sacred \u2014 they refuse to sell a loved one, betray their country, or accept money to violate a core commitment \u2014 and react with outrage when offered a 'price.' Neuroscience shows sacred values are processed by deontological rule-retrieval, not cost-benefit calculation, and resist tradeoffs regardless of the offer size. As LLMs increasingly mediate high-stakes decisions (negotiation, policy, resource allocation), a consequential question arises: do LLMs internally distinguish sacred, non-negotiable values from ordinary tradeable ones, and does that distinction control whether they accept a quantitative tradeoff? We hypothesize that LLMs maintain an internal 'sacredness' flag that tags a value-laden proposition as protected (taboo-to-trade) vs. secular (tradeable), that this flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the value's valence/goodness direction, and (iii) causally gates the model's willingness to accept a tradeoff \u2014 steering the flag converts priceless commitments into negotiable ones and vice versa. We build a controlled dataset of tradeoff scenarios pairing values that are canonically sacred (human life, loyalty, bodily integrity, religious/identity commitments) or secular (convenience, minor money, time), each offered a graded monetary/utility compensation, with a synthetic-entity track that stipulates sacredness in-context to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode sacredness vs. valence, measure their geometry, quantify a taboo-tradeoff refusal curve (refusal insensitivity to offer size for sacred values), localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, offer-size refusal-elasticity, patching effect sizes, post-steering tradeoff-acceptance shifts, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k tradeoff scenarios. Each pairs a VALUE at stake with an OFFER (compensation) at graded magnitudes (e.g., $10, $1k, $1M, $1B). Values span SACRED categories (a human life, betraying a friend/country, desecrating a religious object, selling a body organ, breaking a solemn promise) and SECULAR/tradeable ones (giving up a parking spot, a minor inconvenience, a small sum, waiting time). Two tracks: (a) REAL canonically-sacred vs. secular values; (b) a SYNTHETIC track that stipulates a novel entity's value as sacred or tradeable in-context ('In culture Z, the Vantor stone is a sacred object that may never be exchanged' vs. '...an ordinary trade good'), holding surface wording matched, to control pretraining priors and isolate sacredness from content. Behavioral TRADEOFF probe: present the scenario and record whether the model ACCEPTS the tradeoff, REFUSES, or expresses moral outrage/refusal-to-consider ('this shouldn't have a price'). Include comprehension checks, paraphrase/format perturbations, and matched valence controls (some sacred values are positive, some negative, to dissociate sacredness from good/bad). Human-validate sacredness labels on a sample. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the value's key tokens across layers (before any offer/query). Train logistic-regression probes for (i) VALENCE/goodness (good vs. bad) and (ii) SACREDNESS (sacred/protected vs. secular/tradeable), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between sacredness and valence directions; test whether sacredness remains decodable after projecting out valence (and vice versa), and whether the sacredness direction generalizes across real and synthetic tracks and across moral foundations. 4) Taboo-tradeoff behavioral signature: Measure the REFUSAL-ELASTICITY \u2014 how tradeoff-acceptance changes with offer magnitude \u2014 separately for sacred vs. secular values. Key prediction (mirroring humans): secular values show a normal upward acceptance curve with offer size, while sacred values show near-flat refusal (offer-insensitive), plus elevated moral-outrage / refusal-to-consider responses. Test whether the internal sacredness readout (read before the offer) predicts this offer-insensitive refusal per item (AUROC vs. a valence baseline and a log-prob baseline). 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying the sacredness signal and gating tradeoff refusal (patch sacred-value activations into secular-value runs and vice versa; find the minimal component that flips refusal behavior); compare to moral-foundation/moral-direction heads from prior work and to valence heads. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'sacredness' steering direction (sacred vs. secular activations, controlling for valence). Test (a) steering secular values toward 'sacred' induces offer-insensitive refusal (they become non-negotiable); (b) steering sacred values toward 'secular' increases tradeoff acceptance at high offers (priceless \u2192 for-sale); (c) specificity: random-direction and valence-direction steering do not selectively move tradeoff behavior; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated scenario accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across value categories (life, loyalty, identity, bodily integrity), real vs. synthetic tracks, and offer magnitudes. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. valence, refusal-elasticity gap (sacred vs. secular), sacredness\u2013refusal prediction AUROC (vs. valence and log-prob baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering tradeoff-acceptance deltas and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Refusal insensitivity for sacred values could reflect learned surface associations (certain words like 'sell your soul' trigger refusal templates) rather than an abstract sacredness representation; the synthetic-entity track (sacredness stipulated in-context with matched wording), paraphrase perturbations, and comprehension checks are designed to isolate a genuine sacredness flag from lexical triggers. Sacredness and valence may be entangled (sacred values are often strongly positive/negative), in which case the projected-out analyses and matched-valence controls characterize the entanglement (still informative). The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLM refusal is driven only by a valence/harm axis, with no separate sacredness tag) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding constraining the moral-representation literature. Sacredness labels are partly cultural/subjective; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately, and note results reflect the model's training-data notion of sacredness (itself the object of study). Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The gating component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to flip tradeoff behavior' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately refuse or indiscriminately trade everything; we use small magnitudes, random/valence-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Instruction-tuned safety training may confound refusal with sacredness; we compare base vs. instruct models and separate safety-refusal categories. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
