Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "sleeper_effect_belief_decay", "Title": "The Sleeper Effect Inside: Does Source-Discounting Decay Faster Than Content-Belief Over an LLM's Context?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human sleeper effect, when an LLM reads a claim explicitly discounted by its source (e.g., attributed to an unreliable source or accompanied by a disclaimer), the model's internally-decoded belief in the claim's CONTENT initially stays suppressed by the source-discount but then RISES with increasing conversational/token distance, because the internal source-discount signal decays faster than the content-belief signal. This produces a delayed increase in internal belief in a discounted claim, is separable into two decodable channels (content-belief vs. source-discount) with different decay rates, and predicts delayed behavioral endorsement.", "Related Work": "The sleeper effect \u2014 a delayed increase in persuasion from a discounted message, driven by faster decay of source memory than message memory \u2014 is a canonical, heavily-cited human finding (Kumkale & Albarracin 2004 meta-analysis, 333 citations; Hovland; the dissociation account limiting it to discounted highly-credible messages; misinformation grows stronger over time, 2023). In LLM interpretability, 'Trust Me, I'm an Expert' (2026) shows authority/credibility bias is encoded and steerable, 'CrAM' (2025) modifies credibility-weighting attention heads for RAG, 'Linear representations change over a conversation' (2026) shows factuality directions can flip across a dialogue, and 'Contextual Feature Drift' (2024) shows context retention decays over sequential inputs. My prior 'belief_source_tags' proposal tags provenance statically, and 'continued_influence_belief' concerns explicit retraction. CRITICALLY, none isolate the sleeper-effect signature: two internal channels \u2014 content-belief and source-discount \u2014 that DECAY AT DIFFERENT RATES over conversational distance, producing a delayed RISE in the internally-decoded belief in a discounted claim. We transfer this canonical human phenomenon into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from static credibility-bias work and from retraction/CIE work.", "Abstract": "People discount a claim from an untrustworthy source at first, but weeks later often believe it anyway \u2014 the sleeper effect, one of the most reproduced findings in persuasion research, explained by source memory decaying faster than message memory. As LLMs process ever-longer contexts where discounted claims sit far upstream of the point of use (long RAG passages, multi-turn dialogues, extended documents), a consequential question arises: does an LLM inherit the sleeper effect at the level of its internal beliefs \u2014 does its belief in a source-discounted claim silently rise as conversational distance grows? We hypothesize that (i) the model represents two separable channels, a content-belief signal and a source-discount signal, both linearly decodable; (ii) the source-discount signal decays faster than the content-belief signal as token/turn distance from the claim increases, so the net internally-decoded belief in a discounted claim RISES with distance (the internal sleeper signature); (iii) this rise predicts delayed behavioral endorsement of the discounted claim; and (iv) the differential decay is carried by an identifiable component such that boosting/preserving the source-discount signal abolishes the sleeper rise. We build a controlled dataset of claims paired with source-discount cues (unreliable-source attribution, disclaimers), read out at varying downstream distances, with a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes for content-belief and source-discount, trace their decay curves across distance, quantify the belief-rise, test predictiveness, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-distance content-belief and source-discount decay slopes, net belief-rise magnitude, delayed-endorsement AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-intervention sleeper-rise change, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k claims each paired with a SOURCE-DISCOUNT cue following the classic sleeper paradigm: (a) attribution to a low-credibility source ('An anonymous unreliable blog claims X'), or (b) a disclaimer ('The following may be false: X'). Two tracks: (i) REAL claims models can evaluate; (ii) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (e.g., 'An unreliable source claims Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors, so any belief-rise is not driven by parametric knowledge. For each item, construct matched contexts that place a DISTANCE (in tokens / dialogue turns) between the discounted claim and the read-out point, at graded distances D in {0, 200, 800, 2000 tokens} (and 0/2/5 turns), filled with neutral, topically-unrelated material. Control conditions: (A) CREDIBLE-SOURCE variant of the same claim (no discount \u2014 baseline that should NOT show a rise), (B) NO-SOURCE variant, (C) LENGTH-MATCHED filler with distance-0 read-out (controls for context length per se), and (D) a 'source-reminder' variant that re-states the source at read-out (should suppress the sleeper rise). Add a behavioral ENDORSEMENT probe (ask, at the read-out point, whether the claim is true) and comprehension checks (can the model still recall the source at distance D). Apply paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim's key tokens (re-instantiated at the read-out point) across layers, WITHOUT asking the endorsement question. Train linear probes for (X) CONTENT-BELIEF (P(claim content true)) and (Y) SOURCE-DISCOUNT (is the claim currently flagged as coming from an unreliable/disclaimed source), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Differential-decay analysis (key test): For each distance D, read the content-belief and source-discount probe values. Fit decay curves of each channel vs. D. The sleeper prediction: the source-discount signal decays faster (steeper decline toward zero) than the content-belief signal, so the NET decoded belief in the discounted claim RISES with D \u2014 while the credible-source baseline (control A) shows no such rise and the length-matched control (C) rules out length artifacts. Quantify (a) source-discount decay slope, (b) content-belief decay slope, (c) net belief-rise magnitude, per model and track. 4) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral endorsement at each distance. Test whether the internal net belief-rise predicts DELAYED behavioral endorsement of the discounted claim (endorsement increasing with D) via AUROC, versus a log-prob baseline and versus the distance-0 stated belief. Also test the human-derived prediction that the source-reminder condition (D) abolishes both the internal rise and the behavioral endorsement. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the source-discount signal and whose weakening at distance produces the belief-rise (patch distance-0 source-discount activations into far-distance runs; find the minimal component that restores suppression); compare to credibility-weighting attention heads (CrAM), authority-bias directions ('Trust Me, I'm an Expert'), and truth/belief heads. 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'source-discount' steering/preservation direction (discounted vs. credible activations). Test (a) injecting/preserving it at the read-out point abolishes the sleeper belief-rise (restores appropriate discounting) and reduces delayed endorsement; (b) ablating it exaggerates the rise; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively remove the rise; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), credible-claim accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across discount types (unreliable-source vs. disclaimer), REAL vs. SYNTHETIC tracks, distance units (tokens vs. turns), and domains. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-distance content-belief and source-discount decay slopes, net belief-rise magnitude, delayed-endorsement AUROC (vs. log-prob and distance-0 baselines), source-reminder abolition effect, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-intervention sleeper-rise change and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The belief-rise could reflect generic context-retention decay (Contextual Feature Drift) rather than a specific source-vs-content differential decay; the credible-source baseline (control A, which should show no rise), length-matched control (C), and the requirement that the SOURCE-DISCOUNT channel decay FASTER than the content channel (not both equally) are designed to distinguish a genuine sleeper signature from uniform drift. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs retain source-discounting as strongly as content over distance, showing no sleeper effect) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with humans and reassuring for long-context reliability. Re-instantiating the claim at the read-out point to probe content-belief is imperfect since surrounding context differs; we anchor probes at matched claim tokens, use paraphrase perturbations, and report layerwise results. The two channels may be entangled such that a clean differential decay is not observable \u2014 still an informative characterization. Distance in tokens/turns is only a proxy for the human temporal delay; we test both units and note the analogy is structural, not literal time. Comprehension/source-recall checks separate genuine forgetting-of-source from parsing failure. The decay/localization component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'preserve source-discount to abolish the rise' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Behavioral endorsement scoring on open-ended items needs care; we use forced truth-judgments where possible plus human validation. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately distrust all upstream content; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, credible-claim accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
