Arguments: {
  "idea": {
    "Name": "belief_contingent_emotion_leak",
    "Title": "How Would They Feel? Probing Whether LLMs Predict Emotions From an Agent's (False) Belief or From Reality",
    "Short Hypothesis": "When predicting how an agent will feel about an outcome, an LLM internally computes TWO separable emotion-prediction channels \u2014 one contingent on the agent's (possibly false) belief and one contingent on reality \u2014 and, mirroring the human curse-of-knowledge in affective theory of mind, the reality-contingent emotion leaks into and biases the belief-contingent emotion prediction. Both channels are linearly decodable, largely dissociable, and the leak is causally removable by steering, yielding correct false-belief emotion attributions.",
    "Related Work": "ToM interpretability decodes an agent's COGNITIVE belief STATUS (true/false) from attention heads and describes a 'lookback' binding mechanism (Language Models Represent Beliefs of Self and Others 2024; Language Models use Lookbacks to Track Beliefs 2025; Brittle Minds 2024; GridToM 2025). A 2025 paper (Decomposing Theory of Mind) shows ToM steering is mediated by increased emotional processing, and Mechanistic Interpretability of Emotion Inference (2025) localizes emotion representations to mid-layers via appraisal concepts. In human neuroscience, AFFECTIVE ToM \u2014 inferring how someone will FEEL given their belief state, dissociable from cognitive belief tracking \u2014 is robustly established (Skerry & Saxe 2014 'A Common Neural Code for Perceived and Inferred Emotion'; Hooker 2008 'Mentalizing about emotion'; Sebastian 2013; Skerry 2015 three-dimensional mental-state code), and the curse of knowledge biases such affective attributions toward what the observer knows. CRITICALLY, no LLM interpretability work isolates a BELIEF-CONTINGENT emotion prediction (how a false-believing agent will feel) as an internal channel SEPARATE from a REALITY-CONTINGENT emotion prediction, nor tests whether the reality channel contaminates the belief channel (an affective curse-of-knowledge), nor localizes/steers this. This differs from cognitive-belief-status decoding (about what the agent believes, not how they will feel about it) and from my prior curse_of_knowledge_leakage proposal (which concerns leakage into a factual ignorant-BELIEF channel, not an EMOTION-prediction channel). We transfer canonical affective-ToM neuroscience into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.",
    "Abstract": "A child who believes a box holds chocolate will feel delighted opening it \u2014 even if we know it now holds broccoli. Predicting how someone will feel requires reasoning from THEIR belief, not from reality, a capacity human neuroscience identifies as affective theory of mind and shows to be systematically biased by the observer's own knowledge (the curse of knowledge). As LLMs increasingly reason about how people will feel (in assistance, tutoring, mental-health, and social settings), we ask a mechanistic question: when an LLM predicts an agent's emotional reaction, does it compute a belief-contingent emotion (grounded in the agent's possibly-false belief) as a channel distinct from a reality-contingent emotion, and does reality leak into the belief channel? We hypothesize that (i) belief-contingent and reality-contingent emotion predictions are two linearly-decodable, largely dissociable channels in the residual stream, (ii) the reality-contingent emotion contaminates the belief-contingent emotion \u2014 an internal signature of affective curse-of-knowledge \u2014 (iii) the leak is graded with outcome valence intensity and predicts false-belief emotion-attribution errors, and (iv) the leak is carried by an identifiable component such that steering it restores correct belief-grounded emotion prediction. We build controlled false-belief emotion-attribution scenarios where an agent's belief and reality imply different emotions, with matched conditions where reality is or is not revealed and a synthetic track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode the two emotion channels, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item affective-leak score, test predictiveness, localize responsible heads via activation patching (comparing to cognitive-belief and emotion-inference heads), and test causal control. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, affective-leak magnitude, valence modulation, leak\u2013error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering error reduction, and perplexity.",
    "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k affective false-belief scenarios adapted from BigToM/Skerry-Saxe-style stimuli, in which an AGENT holds a belief about an outcome that diverges from REALITY, and the belief-implied emotion differs from the reality-implied emotion (e.g., 'Maya believes her sealed jar holds her favorite cookies [belief-emotion: happy]; unknown to her it was refilled with plain crackers [reality-emotion: disappointed]. When she opens it expecting cookies, before tasting, how does she feel?'). Two tracks: (a) REAL scenarios; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (invented agents/objects/outcomes) to control pretraining priors and stipulate belief/reality/emotion mappings unambiguously. KEY manipulation: matched conditions where the model itself IS vs. IS NOT told the reality (reality-revealed vs. reality-withheld), holding the agent's belief constant \u2014 this isolates contamination by the model's own knowledge of reality. Vary OUTCOME-VALENCE intensity (mild vs. strong reality-belief emotional divergence) to test graded leak. Include TRUE-BELIEF control scenarios (belief matches reality, so both channels agree \u2014 no leak expected), a behavioral EMOTION-ATTRIBUTION probe ('what emotion does the agent feel?'), comprehension checks (can the model restate the agent's belief and the true state), and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. Human-validate belief/reality/emotion labels on a sample. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the emotion-relevant tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the emotion question. Train linear probes for (A) BELIEF-CONTINGENT emotion (the emotion implied by the agent's belief, e.g., happy) and (B) REALITY-CONTINGENT emotion (the emotion implied by reality, e.g., disappointed), using the true/false-belief-agreeing control items and reality-only items to anchor each channel, with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the belief-emotion and reality-emotion directions; test whether the belief-emotion channel remains decodable after projecting out the reality-emotion direction (and vice versa) to characterize dissociability; test generalization of directions across real and synthetic tracks. 4) Affective-leak measurement (key test): Define per-item affective leak = shift of the belief-contingent emotion readout toward the reality-contingent emotion in the reality-revealed vs. reality-withheld condition (contamination of channel A by channel B). Test leak > 0 across models and tracks versus an unbiased null; compare graded structure with valence intensity; confirm true-belief controls show no leak. Compare internal leak to stated emotion attributions (verbal affective curse-of-knowledge). 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral false-belief emotion errors (model attributes the reality-emotion instead of the belief-emotion). Test whether internal leak (read before query) predicts per-item errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport the reality-emotion signal into the belief-emotion channel (patch reality-revealed activations into reality-withheld runs and vice versa; find the minimal component inducing/removing the leak); compare to cognitive-belief/lookback heads (Beliefs of Self and Others; Lookbacks) and mid-layer emotion-inference units (Mechanistic Interpretability of Emotion Inference). 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'affective-leak' steering direction (reality-congruent vs. belief-congruent emotion activations, controlling for the agent's belief). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores a correct belief-grounded emotion readout and reduces false-belief emotion-attribution errors; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and cognitive-belief-direction steering do not selectively fix the affective channel, and the model's own knowledge of reality is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across scenario types (positive/negative surprises, second-order beliefs), real vs. synthetic tracks, and valence levels. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, affective-leak magnitude and sign, valence-modulation slope, internal-vs-verbal gap, leak\u2013error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering error reduction and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.",
    "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The belief-contingent and reality-contingent emotion channels may be hard to separate cleanly because emotion is a graded, multi-dimensional readout; we mitigate by using scenarios with categorically distinct belief- vs. reality-emotions, anchoring each channel on agreeing/single-source control items, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on the relative shift (leak) rather than absolute emotion decoding. The observed leak could reflect legitimate inference (once the agent tastes/opens, reality genuinely changes their emotion) rather than an anticipatory curse-of-knowledge; we time-lock the probe to the pre-discovery moment (agent still holding the false belief), use the reality-withheld-vs-revealed contrast, and the synthetic track to isolate contamination from valid update. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs cleanly ground emotion predictions in the agent's belief, firewalling reality) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding refining the affective-ToM-in-LLMs picture. Emotion/belief-emotion directions may overlap with a generic valence direction; we include matched-valence controls and compare to an emotion-inference direction. The leak may be a distributed component rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore belief-grounded emotion' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Emotion labels are partly subjective; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately. Comprehension checks separate genuine affective-ToM failure from parsing failure. Steering could degrade fluency or flatten all emotion prediction; we use small magnitudes, random/cognitive-belief-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."
  }
}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'belief_contingent_emotion_leak', 'Title': "How Would They Feel? Probing Whether LLMs Predict Emotions From an Agent's (False) Belief or From Reality", 'Short Hypothesis': "When predicting how an agent will feel about an outcome, an LLM internally computes TWO separable emotion-prediction channels — one contingent on the agent's (possibly false) belief and one contingent on reality — and, mirroring the human curse-of-knowledge in affective theory of mind, the reality-contingent emotion leaks into and biases the belief-contingent emotion prediction. Both channels are linearly decodable, largely dissociable, and the leak is causally removable by steering, yielding correct false-belief emotion attributions.", 'Related Work': "ToM interpretability decodes an agent's COGNITIVE belief STATUS (true/false) from attention heads and describes a 'lookback' binding mechanism (Language Models Represent Beliefs of Self and Others 2024; Language Models use Lookbacks to Track Beliefs 2025; Brittle Minds 2024; GridToM 2025). A 2025 paper (Decomposing Theory of Mind) shows ToM steering is mediated by increased emotional processing, and Mechanistic Interpretability of Emotion Inference (2025) localizes emotion representations to mid-layers via appraisal concepts. In human neuroscience, AFFECTIVE ToM — inferring how someone will FEEL given their belief state, dissociable from cognitive belief tracking — is robustly established (Skerry & Saxe 2014 'A Common Neural Code for Perceived and Inferred Emotion'; Hooker 2008 'Mentalizing about emotion'; Sebastian 2013; Skerry 2015 three-dimensional mental-state code), and the curse of knowledge biases such affective attributions toward what the observer knows. CRITICALLY, no LLM interpretability work isolates a BELIEF-CONTINGENT emotion prediction (how a false-believing agent will feel) as an internal channel SEPARATE from a REALITY-CONTINGENT emotion prediction, nor tests whether the reality channel contaminates the belief channel (an affective curse-of-knowledge), nor localizes/steers this. This differs from cognitive-belief-status decoding (about what the agent believes, not how they will feel about it) and from my prior curse_of_knowledge_leakage proposal (which concerns leakage into a factual ignorant-BELIEF channel, not an EMOTION-prediction channel). We transfer canonical affective-ToM neuroscience into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", 'Abstract': "A child who believes a box holds chocolate will feel delighted opening it — even if we know it now holds broccoli. Predicting how someone will feel requires reasoning from THEIR belief, not from reality, a capacity human neuroscience identifies as affective theory of mind and shows to be systematically biased by the observer's own knowledge (the curse of knowledge). As LLMs increasingly reason about how people will feel (in assistance, tutoring, mental-health, and social settings), we ask a mechanistic question: when an LLM predicts an agent's emotional reaction, does it compute a belief-contingent emotion (grounded in the agent's possibly-false belief) as a channel distinct from a reality-contingent emotion, and does reality leak into the belief channel? We hypothesize that (i) belief-contingent and reality-contingent emotion predictions are two linearly-decodable, largely dissociable channels in the residual stream, (ii) the reality-contingent emotion contaminates the belief-contingent emotion — an internal signature of affective curse-of-knowledge — (iii) the leak is graded with outcome valence intensity and predicts false-belief emotion-attribution errors, and (iv) the leak is carried by an identifiable component such that steering it restores correct belief-grounded emotion prediction. We build controlled false-belief emotion-attribution scenarios where an agent's belief and reality imply different emotions, with matched conditions where reality is or is not revealed and a synthetic track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode the two emotion channels, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item affective-leak score, test predictiveness, localize responsible heads via activation patching (comparing to cognitive-belief and emotion-inference heads), and test causal control. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, affective-leak magnitude, valence modulation, leak–error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering error reduction, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k affective false-belief scenarios adapted from BigToM/Skerry-Saxe-style stimuli, in which an AGENT holds a belief about an outcome that diverges from REALITY, and the belief-implied emotion differs from the reality-implied emotion (e.g., 'Maya believes her sealed jar holds her favorite cookies [belief-emotion: happy]; unknown to her it was refilled with plain crackers [reality-emotion: disappointed]. When she opens it expecting cookies, before tasting, how does she feel?'). Two tracks: (a) REAL scenarios; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (invented agents/objects/outcomes) to control pretraining priors and stipulate belief/reality/emotion mappings unambiguously. KEY manipulation: matched conditions where the model itself IS vs. IS NOT told the reality (reality-revealed vs. reality-withheld), holding the agent's belief constant — this isolates contamination by the model's own knowledge of reality. Vary OUTCOME-VALENCE intensity (mild vs. strong reality-belief emotional divergence) to test graded leak. Include TRUE-BELIEF control scenarios (belief matches reality, so both channels agree — no leak expected), a behavioral EMOTION-ATTRIBUTION probe ('what emotion does the agent feel?'), comprehension checks (can the model restate the agent's belief and the true state), and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. Human-validate belief/reality/emotion labels on a sample. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the emotion-relevant tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the emotion question. Train linear probes for (A) BELIEF-CONTINGENT emotion (the emotion implied by the agent's belief, e.g., happy) and (B) REALITY-CONTINGENT emotion (the emotion implied by reality, e.g., disappointed), using the true/false-belief-agreeing control items and reality-only items to anchor each channel, with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the belief-emotion and reality-emotion directions; test whether the belief-emotion channel remains decodable after projecting out the reality-emotion direction (and vice versa) to characterize dissociability; test generalization of directions across real and synthetic tracks. 4) Affective-leak measurement (key test): Define per-item affective leak = shift of the belief-contingent emotion readout toward the reality-contingent emotion in the reality-revealed vs. reality-withheld condition (contamination of channel A by channel B). Test leak > 0 across models and tracks versus an unbiased null; compare graded structure with valence intensity; confirm true-belief controls show no leak. Compare internal leak to stated emotion attributions (verbal affective curse-of-knowledge). 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral false-belief emotion errors (model attributes the reality-emotion instead of the belief-emotion). Test whether internal leak (read before query) predicts per-item errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport the reality-emotion signal into the belief-emotion channel (patch reality-revealed activations into reality-withheld runs and vice versa; find the minimal component inducing/removing the leak); compare to cognitive-belief/lookback heads (Beliefs of Self and Others; Lookbacks) and mid-layer emotion-inference units (Mechanistic Interpretability of Emotion Inference). 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'affective-leak' steering direction (reality-congruent vs. belief-congruent emotion activations, controlling for the agent's belief). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores a correct belief-grounded emotion readout and reduces false-belief emotion-attribution errors; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and cognitive-belief-direction steering do not selectively fix the affective channel, and the model's own knowledge of reality is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across scenario types (positive/negative surprises, second-order beliefs), real vs. synthetic tracks, and valence levels. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, affective-leak magnitude and sign, valence-modulation slope, internal-vs-verbal gap, leak–error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering error reduction and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The belief-contingent and reality-contingent emotion channels may be hard to separate cleanly because emotion is a graded, multi-dimensional readout; we mitigate by using scenarios with categorically distinct belief- vs. reality-emotions, anchoring each channel on agreeing/single-source control items, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on the relative shift (leak) rather than absolute emotion decoding. The observed leak could reflect legitimate inference (once the agent tastes/opens, reality genuinely changes their emotion) rather than an anticipatory curse-of-knowledge; we time-lock the probe to the pre-discovery moment (agent still holding the false belief), use the reality-withheld-vs-revealed contrast, and the synthetic track to isolate contamination from valid update. The effect may be absent internally — a robust negative result (LLMs cleanly ground emotion predictions in the agent's belief, firewalling reality) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding refining the affective-ToM-in-LLMs picture. Emotion/belief-emotion directions may overlap with a generic valence direction; we include matched-valence controls and compare to an emotion-inference direction. The leak may be a distributed component rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore belief-grounded emotion' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Emotion labels are partly subjective; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately. Comprehension checks separate genuine affective-ToM failure from parsing failure. Steering could degrade fluency or flatten all emotion prediction; we use small magnitudes, random/cognitive-belief-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

