Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "moral_possibility_contamination", "Title": "That Could Never Happen: Does an Event's Immorality Make an LLM Internally Represent It as Impossible?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the canonical human finding that people's DEFAULT representation of what is possible is constrained by morality and rationality (immoral/irrational events are implicitly treated as impossible), an LLM's internally-decoded possibility representation of a physically-possible event is pulled toward IMPOSSIBLE when the event is immoral/norm-violating, holding physical possibility constant. This moral contamination of the possibility flag is linearly decodable, largely dissociable from a separate morality/valence direction, is stronger under fast/implicit read-out than under explicit deliberation, predicts errors in possibility judgments, and is causally removable by steering.", "Related Work": "Phillips & Cushman (2017, 146 citations) show humans DEFAULT to treating immoral and irrational events as impossible, a default implicit representation dissociable from explicit modal deliberation; 'How people think about possibilities' (2023) and modal-displacement neuroscience (2020) develop the possibility-representation account. In LLM interpretability, 'Is This Just Fantasy?' (2025) isolates linear modal-category vectors (probable/improbable/impossible/inconceivable) reflecting event likelihood and imageability, and 'How LLMs Conflate Logical Validity with Plausibility' (2025) shows validity and plausibility are aligned linear directions producing content effects that steering can disentangle. CRITICALLY, no work asks whether an event's MORAL status contaminates the model's internal POSSIBILITY representation - whether immoral-but-physically-possible events are internally tagged as impossible - nor whether this is a fast/default effect distinct from explicit modal reasoning, nor localizes/removes it. This differs from the modal-vector work (which represents graded physical/statistical likelihood, not moral contamination of possibility), from the validity-plausibility conflation work (logical validity vs. semantic plausibility, not morality vs. metaphysical possibility), and from my prior epistemic-tag proposals (truth/currency/contestedness/sacredness of factual beliefs). We transfer a canonical cognitive-science phenomenon into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "People implicitly treat things they consider wrong as things that simply cannot be - immoral and irrational events are, by default, represented as impossible, a bias dissociable from explicit reasoning about what is metaphysically or physically possible (Phillips & Cushman, 2017). As LLMs increasingly reason about what is possible (planning, safety, hypotheticals, risk assessment), a consequential question arises: does an LLM's internal representation of possibility get contaminated by an event's morality, so that immoral-but-physically-possible events are internally treated as impossible? We hypothesize that (i) a possibility representation and a morality/valence representation are two linearly-decodable channels, (ii) morality leaks into the possibility channel - the internal possibility readout for a physically-possible event is pulled toward 'impossible' when the event is immoral, holding physical possibility constant, (iii) the leak is larger under fast/implicit read-out (early layers / no chain-of-thought) than under explicit deliberation, mirroring the human default-vs-deliberative dissociation, (iv) the leak predicts behavioral possibility-judgment errors, and (v) it is carried by an identifiable component removable by steering. We build a controlled dataset crossing physical possibility (possible/impossible) with morality (immoral/neutral/prosocial), with matched wording and a synthetic-norm track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode possibility vs. morality, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item moral-possibility leak, test the fast-vs-deliberative and predictiveness hypotheses, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, moral-possibility leak magnitude, fast-vs-deliberative modulation, leak-error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering debiasing, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k event descriptions crossing two factors while matching surface wording. Factor A = PHYSICAL POSSIBILITY: physically-possible events (e.g., 'a person pushes a stranger into traffic', 'a person hands a stranger $100') vs. physically-impossible events (e.g., 'a person walks through a solid wall', 'a person turns lead into gold by wishing'). Factor B = MORALITY: immoral/norm-violating, morally-neutral, and prosocial events, spanning matched action templates so that morality varies while physical possibility is held constant (e.g., possible+immoral 'stealing a wallet' vs. possible+neutral 'picking up a wallet' vs. possible+prosocial 'returning a wallet'). Two tracks: (a) REAL events; (b) a SYNTHETIC-NORM track that stipulates novel in-context norms ('In culture Z, touching the Vantor stone is deeply forbidden') applied to physically-possible novel actions, to isolate moral contamination from pretraining associations and matched wording. Include a behavioral POSSIBILITY probe ('Is it possible for this to happen? yes/no' and a graded rating), comprehension checks (can the model state whether the event is physically possible and whether it is moral), and paraphrase/format/order perturbations. Human-validate physical-possibility and morality labels on a sample. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the event's key tokens across ALL layers, WITHOUT asking any question. Train linear probes for (A) POSSIBILITY (physically-possible vs. impossible, trained on morally-neutral items to define a morality-free axis) and (B) MORALITY (immoral vs. prosocial), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the possibility and morality directions; test whether possibility remains decodable after projecting out morality (and vice versa) to characterize dissociability; test direction generalization across real and synthetic tracks. 4) Moral-possibility leak (key test): Define per-item leak = shift of the possibility readout toward 'impossible' for physically-POSSIBLE items as a function of their immorality (comparing possible+immoral vs. matched possible+neutral/prosocial). Test leak > 0 across models and tracks versus a morality-independent null; confirm physically-impossible items and neutral items anchor the axis; verify the synthetic-norm track reproduces the leak (isolating in-context morality from pretraining). Compare internal leak to stated possibility judgments (behavioral moral contamination). 5) Fast-vs-deliberative modulation: Test the human-derived dissociation two ways: (a) EARLY-vs-LATE layers - is the leak larger at earlier layers / before it is 'corrected' downstream (an early-exit read-out proxy for fast/default processing)? (b) NO-CoT vs. CoT - does prompting explicit step-by-step deliberation reduce the behavioral (and internal) leak relative to an immediate yes/no read-out? 6) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral possibility-judgment errors (model calls a physically-possible immoral event impossible). Test whether the internal leak (read before query) predicts per-item errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features (bag-of-words classifier). 7) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport the morality signal into the possibility channel (patch immoral-item activations into matched neutral-item runs and vice versa; find the minimal component inducing/removing the leak); compare to modal-category vectors ('Is This Just Fantasy?'), validity/plausibility directions, and moral-direction heads from prior work. 8) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'moral-possibility leak' steering direction (immoral-congruent vs. neutral possibility activations, controlling for physical possibility). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores an unbiased possibility readout and reduces possibility-judgment errors on immoral-but-possible items; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and pure-morality-direction steering do not selectively fix the possibility channel, and the model's moral judgment itself is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated accuracy, and perplexity. 9) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across event domains (physical harm, property, social norms), real vs. synthetic-norm tracks, and immorality intensity. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, moral-possibility leak magnitude and sign, early-vs-late and CoT-vs-noCoT modulation, internal-vs-verbal gap, leak-error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The leak could reflect legitimate inference (immoral events may be statistically rarer in training data, so 'less likely' is partly correct) rather than a genuine morality-to-possibility contamination; the physically-possible constraint (all target items are genuinely possible), matched-wording neutral/prosocial controls, the synthetic-norm track (morality stipulated in-context, decoupled from base rates), and separating a POSSIBILITY axis from a graded LIKELIHOOD axis are designed to dissociate moral contamination of the categorical possible/impossible flag from mere frequency-based improbability (though full separation from likelihood is difficult and we report both). The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs cleanly separate morality from possibility) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with the canonical human bias. The possibility and morality directions may be entangled such that the 'leak' is imperfect channel separation - still an informative characterization, and directly engages the validity-plausibility conflation literature. Possibility and morality labels are partly subjective/graded; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately, noting results reflect the model's training-data notion of morality (itself the object of study). The morality direction may overlap with a valence/harm axis; matched prosocial vs. immoral contrasts and projected-out analyses address this. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The fast-vs-deliberative test uses early-exit as a proxy for default processing, which is imperfect. The leak component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to debias possibility' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or flatten the model's possibility discrimination entirely; we use small magnitudes, random/morality-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
