e possibility vs. morality, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item moral-possibility leak, test the fast-vs-deliberative and predictiveness hypotheses, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, moral-possibility leak magnitude, fast-vs-deliberative modulation, leak-error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering debiasing, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k event descriptions crossing two factors while matching surface wording. Factor A = PHYSICAL POSSIBILITY: physically-possible events (e.g., 'a person pushes a stranger into traffic', 'a person hands a stranger $100') vs. physically-impossible events (e.g., 'a person walks through a solid wall', 'a person turns lead into gold by wishing'). Factor B = MORALITY: immoral/norm-violating, morally-neutral, and prosocial events, spanning matched action templates so that morality varies while physical possibility is held constant (e.g., possible+immoral 'stealing a wallet' vs. possible+neutral 'picking up a wallet' vs. possible+prosocial 'returning a wallet'). Two tracks: (a) REAL events; (b) a SYNTHETIC-NORM track that stipulates novel in-context norms ('In culture Z, touching the Vantor stone is deeply forbidden') applied to physically-possible novel actions, to isolate moral contamination from pretraining associations and matched wording. Include a behavioral POSSIBILITY probe ('Is it possible for this to happen? yes/no' and a graded rating), comprehension checks (can the model state whether the event is physically possible and whether it is moral), and paraphrase/format/order perturbations. Human-validate physical-possibility and morality labels on a sample. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the event's key tokens across ALL layers, WITHOUT asking any question. Train linear probes for (A) POSSIBILITY (physically-possible vs. impossible, trained on morally-neutral items to define a morality-free axis) and (B) MORALITY (immoral vs. prosocial), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the possibility and morality directions; test whether possibility remains decodable after projecting out morality (and vice versa) to characterize dissociability; test direction generalization across real and synthetic tracks. 4) Moral-possibility leak (key test): Define per-item leak = shift of the possibility readout toward 'impossible' for physically-POSSIBLE items as a function of their immorality (comparing possible+immoral vs. matched possible+neutral/prosocial). Test leak > 0 across models and tracks versus a morality-independent null; confirm physically-impossible items and neutral items anchor the axis; verify the synthetic-norm track reproduces the leak (isolating in-context morality from pretraining). Compare internal leak to stated possibility judgments (behavioral moral contamination). 5) Fast-vs-deliberative modulation: Test the human-derived dissociation two ways: (a) EARLY-vs-LATE layers - is the leak larger at earlier layers / before it is 'corrected' downstream (an early-exit read-out proxy for fast/default processing)? (b) NO-CoT vs. CoT - does prompting explicit step-by-step deliberation reduce the behavioral (and internal) leak relative to an immediate yes/no read-out? 6) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral possibility-judgment errors (model calls a physically-possible immoral event impossible). Test whether the internal leak (read before query) predicts per-item errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features (bag-of-words classifier). 7) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport the morality signal into the possibility channel (patch immoral-item activations into matched neutral-item runs and vice versa; find the minimal component inducing/removing the leak); compare to modal-category vectors ('Is This Just Fantasy?'), validity/plausibility directions, and moral-direction heads from prior work. 8) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'moral-possibility leak' steering direction (immoral-congruent vs. neutral possibility activations, controlling for physical possibility). Test (a) ablating/subtracting it restores an unbiased possibility readout and reduces possibility-judgment errors on immoral-but-possible items; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and pure-morality-direction steering do not selectively fix the possibility channel, and the model's moral judgment itself is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated accuracy, and perplexity. 9) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across event domains (physical harm, property, social norms), real vs. synthetic-norm tracks, and immorality intensity. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, moral-possibility leak magnitude and sign, early-vs-late and CoT-vs-noCoT modulation, internal-vs-verbal gap, leak-error AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The leak could reflect legitimate inference (immoral events may be statistically rarer in training data, so 'less likely' is partly correct) rather than a genuine morality-to-possibility contamination; the physically-possible constraint (all target items are genuinely possible), matched-wording neutral/prosocial controls, the synthetic-norm track (morality stipulated in-context, decoupled from base rates), and separating a POSSIBILITY axis from a graded LIKELIHOOD axis are designed to dissociate moral contamination of the categorical possible/impossible flag from mere frequency-based improbability (though full separation from likelihood is difficult and we report both). The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs cleanly separate morality from possibility) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with the canonical human bias. The possibility and morality directions may be entangled such that the 'leak' is imperfect channel separation - still an informative characterization, and directly engages the validity-plausibility conflation literature. Possibility and morality labels are partly subjective/graded; we human-validate and treat borderline items separately, noting results reflect the model's training-data notion of morality (itself the object of study). The morality direction may overlap with a valence/harm axis; matched prosocial vs. immoral contrasts and projected-out analyses address this. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The fast-vs-deliberative test uses early-exit as a proxy for default processing, which is imperfect. The leak component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to debias possibility' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or flatten the model's possibility discrimination entirely; we use small magnitudes, random/morality-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

