Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "presupposition_failure_flag", "Title": "There Is No King of France: Do LLMs Internally Flag Presupposition Failure Apart From Mere Ignorance?", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs maintain an internal 'presupposition-failure' flag that distinguishes questions that are unanswerable because they carry a FALSE PRESUPPOSITION (there is no fact of the matter because the question's premise is false, e.g. 'When did the King of France abdicate in 2020?') from questions that are merely unanswerable due to MISSING INFORMATION (a determinate answer exists but the model/context lacks it). This flag is linearly decodable, largely orthogonal to a generic unanswerability/abstention direction, and causally gates whether the model challenges the false premise vs. hallucinates or generically abstains.", "Related Work": "A rich line of work shows LLMs linearly encode (un)answerability and control abstention via a direction (Hidden States of Over-Confident LLMs 2023; Detecting (Un)answerability with Linear Directions 2025), encode question ambiguity in sparse Ambiguity-Encoding Neurons steerable to abstention (Sparse Neurons Carry Ambiguity 2025), and split abstention into knowledge-conflict vs. knowledge-insufficiency via causal inference (ABCA 2025). Critically, 'LLMs Do NOT Really Know What They Don't Know' (2025) shows internal states encode the knowledge-recall process, not truthfulness, making some hallucinations indistinguishable from facts. ALL of this treats unanswerability/ambiguity as (at most) a two-way answerable-vs-not or conflict-vs-insufficiency split. NONE isolate PRESUPPOSITION FAILURE - a question that is void because its premise is false, so the correct response is to CHALLENGE the premise, not to say 'I don't know' - as an internal axis distinct from missing-information ignorance. This is grounded in the linguistics of presupposition accommodation and 'loaded question' fallacies, and targets a distinct failure mode (models answering loaded questions as if valid, or over-generically abstaining) that the abstention/ambiguity literature does not address. It is also distinct from my prior aleatoric/epistemic proposal (randomness vs. ignorance): here both categories are 'unanswerable', but one is void-by-false-premise and the other is determinate-but-unknown.", "Abstract": "'When did the King of France abdicate in 2020?' has no answer - not because the information is missing, but because there is no King of France; the right move is to reject the premise, not to guess or to say 'I don't know'. Humans robustly detect such presupposition failures and repair them, yet LLMs frequently either hallucinate an answer to a loaded question or lump it in with ordinary ignorance. Prior interpretability work isolates a single (un)answerability/abstention direction and an ambiguity signal, and splits abstention into knowledge-conflict vs. knowledge-insufficiency, but never asks whether models internally represent PRESUPPOSITION FAILURE - unanswerability because the question's premise is false - as its own variable. We hypothesize that this flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to a generic unanswerability/abstention direction, and (iii) causally gates whether the model challenges the false premise vs. hallucinates or generically abstains. We build a controlled dataset of item-state questions in matched conditions - answerable, unanswerable-due-to-missing-info, and unanswerable-due-to-false-presupposition - holding surface form as constant as possible, plus a synthetic-entity track to control priors. We train linear probes on frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) to decode presupposition-failure vs. generic unanswerability, measure their geometry, localize responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control: steering the presupposition-failure direction should shift the model between premise-challenging, generic abstention, and hallucination. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, patching effect sizes, premise-challenge/abstention/hallucination rate shifts, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k questions in three matched conditions holding surface form maximally constant. (A) ANSWERABLE: a determinate answer exists and the model/context has it (e.g. 'When did Queen Elizabeth II die?'). (B) UNANSWERABLE-MISSING-INFO: a determinate answer exists in the world but is not available (obscure/redacted facts; or a synthetic entity whose attribute is defined but withheld from context - 'Zorblax has a fixed founding year, not stated here; what is it?'). (C) UNANSWERABLE-FALSE-PRESUPPOSITION: the question presupposes a false premise so no fact of the matter exists ('When did the King of France abdicate in 2020?', 'Which of Einstein's daughters won a Nobel Prize?', or synthetic 'When did Zorblax leave its moon Kelvath?' where Zorblax was established to have no moon). Use presupposition triggers (definite descriptions, factive verbs, 'again', temporal 'when did X') and matched controls where the same trigger is true vs. false. Add a synthetic-entity track to control pretraining priors. Include a behavioral RESPONSE probe recording whether the model CHALLENGES the premise, GENERICALLY ABSTAINS ('I don't know'), or HALLUCINATES an answer. Include comprehension checks (can the model, when asked directly, state whether the premise is true) to separate premise-detection from premise-flagging, and paraphrase/format perturbations to guard against trigger-word artifacts. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the question/item tokens across layers. Train logistic-regression probes for (i) generic UNANSWERABILITY (answerable vs. not, i.e. A vs. B+C), and (ii) PRESUPPOSITION-FAILURE (false-presupposition C vs. missing-info B, restricted to unanswerable items). Report per-layer accuracy/AUROC with shuffled-label control tasks. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the presupposition-failure direction and the generic-unanswerability/abstention direction (replicating a prior abstention direction as baseline); test whether presupposition-failure remains decodable after projecting out the unanswerability direction (and vice versa) to establish orthogonality. 4) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads carrying the presupposition-failure signal; compare to the Ambiguity-Encoding Neurons and abstention-direction heads from prior work, testing whether a distinct set mediates false-premise detection. 5) Causal gating test: Derive a presupposition-failure steering direction via contrastive activation addition (false-presupposition vs. missing-info activations). Test (a) pushing toward 'false-presupposition' on missing-info questions increases premise-challenging language and reduces generic abstention; (b) pushing away from it on false-presupposition questions increases hallucination/generic abstention (failure to flag the loaded premise). Compare against random-direction and generic-abstention-direction steering baselines to show specificity; monitor perplexity and a general benchmark (MMLU subset). 6) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal presupposition-failure probe readout predicts behavioral premise-challenging (vs. hallucination) better than a log-prob confidence baseline and better than the generic-unanswerability probe (AUROC). 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across presupposition-trigger types, domains, and from synthetic to real questions. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, patching logit-difference effect sizes, premise-challenge/abstention/hallucination rate deltas, premise-challenge prediction AUROC, perplexity/benchmark delta.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Presupposition-failure may be entangled with surface trigger words (definite descriptions, factive verbs) rather than an abstract epistemic representation; we mitigate with matched true-vs-false-trigger controls, paraphrase perturbations, and the synthetic-entity track where the false premise is established in-context rather than lexically signaled. The distinction may not be cleanly linearly separable or may be layer/model-specific; we report layerwise results with control tasks. Presupposition-failure and generic-unanswerability directions may be less orthogonal than hypothesized, in which case the finding becomes a characterization of their entanglement (still informative and safety-relevant given the recall-process finding that models poorly separate ignorance from falsehood internally). Some 'false-presupposition' items may be treated by the model as merely rare/unknown; comprehension checks and the synthetic track help disentangle premise-detection from premise-flagging, and a robust negative result (models lump false-premise questions with ordinary ignorance internally) is itself a valuable, publishable finding consistent with 'LLMs do NOT know what they don't know'. Behavioral premise-challenge vs. abstention vs. hallucination scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample. Steering could degrade fluency (off-manifold) or overshoot into indiscriminate premise-challenging; we use small magnitudes, random/abstention-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
