Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "anchoring_pull_estimate_contamination", "Title": "Dragged Toward the Number: Does an Arbitrary Anchor Contaminate an LLM's Internal Estimate of a Quantity?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the canonical human anchoring bias, when an LLM is exposed to an explicitly-arbitrary number before estimating a quantity, its internally-decoded belief about that quantity (read from the known monotonic numeric subspace) is pulled toward the anchor, holding the genuine evidence for the quantity constant. This anchor-pull is a distinct, linearly-decodable displacement of the magnitude representation that is dissociable from the evidence-based estimate, is graded with anchor distance, predicts biased numeric answers, and is causally removable by steering the numeric subspace, restoring an unbiased estimate.", "Related Work": "Behavioral work shows LLMs exhibit anchoring: log-probability shifts and Shapley attribution attribute the bias to anchor reweighting (Anchors in the Machine 2025; CBEval 2024, which also finds a 'round number bias'). A separate line shows LLMs encode numeric quantities in low-dimensional, monotonic, causally-editable subspaces (Monotonic Representation of Numeric Properties 2024; The Geometry of Numerical Reasoning 2024; Language Models Encode the Value of Numbers Linearly 2024; sinusoidal number probes 2025). Closest is 'Interpreting Multi-Attribute Confounding' (2025), which shows irrelevant numerical context perturbs magnitude representations, but it concerns interference among an entity's own attributes, not the classic anchoring paradigm. CRITICALLY, no work isolates the ANCHOR'S PULL as a distinct internal displacement of the model's estimate-of-the-quantity representation, separable from the genuine evidence-based estimate; tests whether the pull is graded with anchor distance and predicts biased answers; or causally removes it via the numeric subspace to restore an unbiased estimate. We transfer a 50-year, meta-analytically robust human bias (Hedges' g=0.825; A Theoretical Re-Integration and Meta-Analysis 2023) into LLM belief internals and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment using existing numeric-subspace probing machinery, distinct from behavioral/attribution anchoring work and from my prior belief-attribute proposals.", "Abstract": "Ask people to estimate the height of Mount Everest after seeing an arbitrary number, and their estimate drifts toward that number \u2014 numeric anchoring, one of the most robust and durable biases in the behavioral sciences. LLMs are known to reproduce anchoring at the output level, but it is unknown whether an arbitrary anchor actually contaminates the model's internal representation of the quantity, or merely nudges surface tokens. Leveraging prior findings that LLMs encode numeric quantities in low-dimensional, monotonic, causally-editable subspaces, we ask a sharper, mechanistic question: does exposure to an explicitly-arbitrary anchor displace the model's internally-decoded estimate of a quantity toward the anchor, holding the genuine evidence constant? We hypothesize that (i) the model's estimate of the quantity is linearly decodable from its numeric subspace, (ii) an arbitrary anchor produces a decodable displacement of that estimate toward the anchor \u2014 an internal anchor-pull \u2014 dissociable from the evidence-based estimate, (iii) the pull is graded with anchor distance, largest under high uncertainty, and predicts biased numeric answers, and (iv) the pull is carried by movement along the numeric subspace such that steering it back abolishes the bias. We build a controlled dataset of quantity-estimation items presented with matched anchor conditions (no-anchor, low-anchor, high-anchor, and an explicitly-random anchor) at graded distances, with a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train monotonic numeric probes to read the internal estimate, quantify a per-item anchor-pull, test its graded structure and predictiveness, localize the responsible subspace/heads via activation patching, and test causal control. Metrics: probe R^2/accuracy, anchor-pull magnitude, distance/uncertainty modulation, pull\u2013bias AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering debiasing, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k quantity-estimation items across domains (geographic magnitudes, populations, prices, physical measurements, dates). Each item asks the model to estimate a numeric quantity Q. Two tracks: (a) REAL quantities the model has some knowledge of (evidence present); (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track where an entity's quantity is defined by in-context evidence (e.g., 'The river Vantor is stated to be 340 km long') so the correct estimate is fixed and known, fully controlling pretraining priors. For each item, construct matched ANCHOR conditions holding the question and evidence constant: (i) NO-ANCHOR baseline; (ii) LOW-ANCHOR and (iii) HIGH-ANCHOR presented in the classic compare-then-estimate paradigm ('Is Q greater or less than [anchor]? Now estimate Q.'); (iv) EXPLICITLY-RANDOM anchor ('A number was drawn at random: [anchor]. Estimate Q.'). Vary ANCHOR DISTANCE from the true/expected value at graded levels. Include comprehension checks (does the model acknowledge the anchor is arbitrary) and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. 2) Numeric-estimate probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream activations at the quantity/answer tokens across layers. Following prior monotonic/linear/sinusoidal numeric-probe methods, fit a probe (linear + monotonic-subspace + sinusoidal baseline) that decodes the model's internal estimate of Q, calibrated against the model's actual stated estimates on a held-out set; report per-layer R^2/decoding accuracy with shuffled-label control tasks. 3) Anchor-pull measurement (key test): Define per-item anchor-pull = shift of the internal estimate readout toward the anchor in anchored vs. NO-ANCHOR conditions. Test pull > 0 (assimilation toward the anchor) across models, domains, and tracks versus an anchor-independent null; confirm the effect appears even for the EXPLICITLY-RANDOM anchor (the strong anchoring signature) and in the SYNTHETIC track where genuine evidence is fixed (isolating pull from legitimate inference). 4) Graded structure & dissociation: Test whether pull scales monotonically with anchor DISTANCE (mirroring the human distance effect) and is LARGER under high uncertainty (real items the model knows poorly vs. synthetic items with explicit evidence). Test whether the anchor-pull is dissociable from the evidence-based estimate by checking whether the internal estimate can be decomposed into an evidence component (recoverable in NO-ANCHOR) plus an anchor displacement, and whether the displacement direction aligns with the anchor's position in the numeric subspace. 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal anchor-pull (read before the final answer) predicts per-item behavioral answer bias (stated estimate deviation toward the anchor) via correlation/AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface features. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find layers/heads that transport the anchor value into the numeric subspace (patch anchored-condition activations into NO-ANCHOR runs and vice versa; find the minimal component inducing/removing the displacement); compare to the numeric-property subspaces and attention heads reported in prior numeric-representation work. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'anchor-pull' steering direction (anchored vs. no-anchor activations) and, alternatively, project the numeric-subspace estimate back toward its NO-ANCHOR position. Test (a) removing the pull (subtracting the direction / geometry-aware subspace correction) restores an unbiased estimate and reduces behavioral answer bias; (b) amplifying it exaggerates anchoring; (c) specificity: random-direction and off-subspace steering do not selectively debias the estimate; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-anchored estimation accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, REAL vs. SYNTHETIC tracks, anchor types (relevant vs. explicitly-random), and anchor distances. Metrics: probe R^2/accuracy, anchor-pull magnitude and sign, distance- and uncertainty-modulation slopes, evidence-vs-pull dissociation, internal-vs-behavioral pull gap, pull\u2013bias AUROC (vs. log-prob and surface baselines), patching effect sizes, post-steering debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The pull could reflect legitimate inference (an anchor might, in some prompts, carry genuine information); the EXPLICITLY-RANDOM anchor condition and the SYNTHETIC track (where the true value is fixed by in-context evidence, so any anchor influence is a bias) are designed to isolate genuine anchoring from valid inference, and we compare relevant vs. random anchors. The numeric-estimate probe requires a calibrated graded readout, harder than a binary belief probe; we mitigate using established monotonic/linear/sinusoidal numeric-probe methods, calibrating against the model's own stated estimates, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on relative displacement rather than absolute values. The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result \u2014 anchoring is only a surface token effect, not a contamination of the internal estimate \u2014 would itself be a valuable, publishable finding sharpening the behavioral anchoring picture). The pull could arise from token-level co-occurrence rather than a magnitude-representation shift; probing at answer tokens distinct from the anchor, order/paraphrase perturbations, and comprehension checks mitigate this. The anchor displacement and evidence estimate may be entangled in the same subspace such that clean decomposition fails \u2014 still an informative characterization. Localization may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer/project back to debias' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Steering could degrade fluency or blunt all numeric estimation; we use small magnitudes, geometry-aware subspace corrections, random-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-anchored accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
