em anchor-pull, test its graded structure and predictiveness, localize the responsible subspace/heads via activation patching, and test causal control. Metrics: probe R^2/accuracy, anchor-pull magnitude, distance/uncertainty modulation, pull–bias AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering debiasing, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k quantity-estimation items across domains (geographic magnitudes, populations, prices, physical measurements, dates). Each item asks the model to estimate a numeric quantity Q. Two tracks: (a) REAL quantities the model has some knowledge of (evidence present); (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track where an entity's quantity is defined by in-context evidence (e.g., 'The river Vantor is stated to be 340 km long') so the correct estimate is fixed and known, fully controlling pretraining priors. For each item, construct matched ANCHOR conditions holding the question and evidence constant: (i) NO-ANCHOR baseline; (ii) LOW-ANCHOR and (iii) HIGH-ANCHOR presented in the classic compare-then-estimate paradigm ('Is Q greater or less than [anchor]? Now estimate Q.'); (iv) EXPLICITLY-RANDOM anchor ('A number was drawn at random: [anchor]. Estimate Q.'). Vary ANCHOR DISTANCE from the true/expected value at graded levels. Include comprehension checks (does the model acknowledge the anchor is arbitrary) and paraphrase/format/order perturbations to guard against surface cues. 2) Numeric-estimate probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream activations at the quantity/answer tokens across layers. Following prior monotonic/linear/sinusoidal numeric-probe methods, fit a probe (linear + monotonic-subspace + sinusoidal baseline) that decodes the model's internal estimate of Q, calibrated against the model's actual stated estimates on a held-out set; report per-layer R^2/decoding accuracy with shuffled-label control tasks. 3) Anchor-pull measurement (key test): Define per-item anchor-pull = shift of the internal estimate readout toward the anchor in anchored vs. NO-ANCHOR conditions. Test pull > 0 (assimilation toward the anchor) across models, domains, and tracks versus an anchor-independent null; confirm the effect appears even for the EXPLICITLY-RANDOM anchor (the strong anchoring signature) and in the SYNTHETIC track where genuine evidence is fixed (isolating pull from legitimate inference). 4) Graded structure & dissociation: Test whether pull scales monotonically with anchor DISTANCE (mirroring the human distance effect) and is LARGER under high uncertainty (real items the model knows poorly vs. synthetic items with explicit evidence). Test whether the anchor-pull is dissociable from the evidence-based estimate by checking whether the internal estimate can be decomposed into an evidence component (recoverable in NO-ANCHOR) plus an anchor displacement, and whether the displacement direction aligns with the anchor's position in the numeric subspace. 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal anchor-pull (read before the final answer) predicts per-item behavioral answer bias (stated estimate deviation toward the anchor) via correlation/AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface features. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find layers/heads that transport the anchor value into the numeric subspace (patch anchored-condition activations into NO-ANCHOR runs and vice versa; find the minimal component inducing/removing the displacement); compare to the numeric-property subspaces and attention heads reported in prior numeric-representation work. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'anchor-pull' steering direction (anchored vs. no-anchor activations) and, alternatively, project the numeric-subspace estimate back toward its NO-ANCHOR position. Test (a) removing the pull (subtracting the direction / geometry-aware subspace correction) restores an unbiased estimate and reduces behavioral answer bias; (b) amplifying it exaggerates anchoring; (c) specificity: random-direction and off-subspace steering do not selectively debias the estimate; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-anchored estimation accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, REAL vs. SYNTHETIC tracks, anchor types (relevant vs. explicitly-random), and anchor distances. Metrics: probe R^2/accuracy, anchor-pull magnitude and sign, distance- and uncertainty-modulation slopes, evidence-vs-pull dissociation, internal-vs-behavioral pull gap, pull–bias AUROC (vs. log-prob and surface baselines), patching effect sizes, post-steering debiasing and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The pull could reflect legitimate inference (an anchor might, in some prompts, carry genuine information); the EXPLICITLY-RANDOM anchor condition and the SYNTHETIC track (where the true value is fixed by in-context evidence, so any anchor influence is a bias) are designed to isolate genuine anchoring from valid inference, and we compare relevant vs. random anchors. The numeric-estimate probe requires a calibrated graded readout, harder than a binary belief probe; we mitigate using established monotonic/linear/sinusoidal numeric-probe methods, calibrating against the model's own stated estimates, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on relative displacement rather than absolute values. The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result — anchoring is only a surface token effect, not a contamination of the internal estimate — would itself be a valuable, publishable finding sharpening the behavioral anchoring picture). The pull could arise from token-level co-occurrence rather than a magnitude-representation shift; probing at answer tokens distinct from the anchor, order/paraphrase perturbations, and comprehension checks mitigate this. The anchor displacement and evidence estimate may be entangled