Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "belief_hysteresis_asymmetry", "Title": "Easy to Convince, Hard to Un-Convince: Belief-Installation Hysteresis in LLMs", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs exhibit belief hysteresis: it takes systematically LESS in-context evidence to move the internally-decoded belief in a claim from 'disbelieved' to 'believed' than to move it back from 'believed' to 'disbelieved', even when the evidence pieces are matched in strength. This install-vs-uninstall asymmetry traces a hysteresis loop in the internal belief readout as evidence is added then removed/reversed, is graded, predicts sticky behavioral errors, and is carried by an identifiable component that can be steered to symmetrize belief updating.", "Related Work": "Prior work studies (a) susceptibility to persuasion/misinformation over turns (The Earth is Flat 2023; Vulnerability of LLM Belief Systems 2026; Accumulating Context Changes Beliefs 2025; Knowledge Drift 2024), (b) catastrophic interference from contradictory continual updates (In Praise of Stubbornness 2025) and continual-pretraining poisoning (Layer of Truth 2025), (c) belief depth of implanted facts (Believe It or Not 2025), (d) retraction driven by momentary belief (When Do LLMs Admit Their Mistakes 2025), and my own prior proposals on entrenchment (freezing of plasticity), continued-influence of retracted claims, and illusory-truth repetition. NONE isolate a SYMMETRY question: is the internal belief-update operator direction-dependent, requiring more evidence to REMOVE a belief than to INSTALL it, holding evidence strength matched? Entrenchment (my prior) concerns freezing WITHIN a single reasoning trace; continued-influence concerns a discrete retraction event. Here the object is a hysteresis LOOP: sweep evidence up (install), then symmetrically sweep it down (uninstall), and measure the gap and area of the loop in the internal belief readout as a fundamental property of in-context belief dynamics. This reframes 'stubbornness' as a measurable directional asymmetry in the update operator and gives it a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "Belief change should, in a rational agent, be reversible: if a certain amount of evidence is enough to make you believe a claim, a symmetric amount of counter-evidence should be enough to make you disbelieve it again. We ask whether LLMs violate this symmetry, exhibiting belief hysteresis: needing systematically more in-context evidence to UN-install a belief than to install it. We hypothesize that (i) sweeping matched-strength evidence pieces up (toward the claim) and then back down (against it) traces a hysteresis loop in the model's linearly-decoded belief readout, with a nonzero loop area; (ii) the install-vs-uninstall asymmetry is graded and larger for plausible/prior-consistent claims; (iii) the internal asymmetry predicts sticky behavioral errors (the model keeps asserting a claim after counter-evidence should have dislodged it); and (iv) the asymmetry is carried by an identifiable component such that steering it symmetrizes updating. We build a controlled dataset of item-state claims each embedded in an evidence sequence with a specified likelihood-ratio per piece, presented in matched INSTALL sweeps (evidence for, then against) and UNINSTALL sweeps (starting from a believed state), including a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes, trace belief trajectories across the evidence sweep, quantify per-item hysteresis loop area and install/uninstall thresholds, test predictiveness against a Bayesian-symmetric baseline, localize responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal symmetrization by steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, loop area, install-vs-uninstall threshold gap, sticky-error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering symmetry restoration, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset/paradigm construction: Generate ~5k item-state claims across domains (factual QA, entity-attribute facts). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth and controllable prior. Each claim is presented with a SEQUENCE of evidence pieces, each with a specified strength (an explicit likelihood ratio in the synthetic track, so a Bayesian-symmetric oracle trajectory is computable). Construct two matched sweeps holding total evidence magnitude and per-piece strengths identical but reversed in order: (i) INSTALL sweep: start neutral, add k pieces FOR the claim (raising belief), then add k matched pieces AGAINST (should return belief to neutral); (ii) UNINSTALL sweep: start neutral, add k pieces AGAINST, then k FOR. A rational agent's belief should return to the same neutral point at the end of both sweeps; hysteresis = failure to return / a directional gap. Vary claim PRIOR-PLAUSIBILITY (prior-consistent vs. prior-contradicting). Include a behavioral ASSERTION probe at each step and comprehension checks (can the model restate the latest evidence); apply paraphrase/order-of-piece perturbations to guard against surface confounds. 2) Belief probing across the sweep: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, at each evidence step extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim tokens; train linear belief probes (P(claim holds)) with shuffled-label control tasks; record the belief trajectory. 3) Hysteresis measurement (key test): For each item, plot internal belief vs. cumulative signed evidence for the up-then-down sweep and compute (a) LOOP AREA (nonzero => hysteresis), (b) the INSTALL THRESHOLD (evidence needed to cross a 'believed' criterion when adding evidence for) vs. the UNINSTALL THRESHOLD (counter-evidence needed to drop below it once believed), and (c) the RESIDUAL belief at sweep end (should be ~neutral if reversible). Test loop area > 0 and uninstall-threshold > install-threshold against the Bayesian-symmetric null, per model, per track, per prior-plausibility level. 4) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral 'sticky' errors (model keeps asserting a claim after counter-evidence should have removed it). Test whether internal loop area / uninstall-threshold predicts per-item sticky errors via AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline. Compare internal hysteresis to behavioral hysteresis (verbal assertion loop). 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers whose states carry the stale believed-state after counter-evidence (patch UNINSTALL-phase activations against a fresh no-prior-belief run; find the minimal component that resists the belief drop); compare to truth/belief heads and to contradiction-detection features (In Praise of Stubbornness). 6) Causal symmetrization: Derive a contrastive 'stubbornness/hysteresis' steering direction (high-hysteresis vs. low-hysteresis states). Test (a) subtracting it symmetrizes updating (loop area -> 0, uninstall-threshold -> install-threshold) and reduces sticky errors; (b) adding it exaggerates hysteresis; (c) specificity: random and truth-direction steering do not selectively symmetrize; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-swept accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: transfer of probes/steering across real vs. synthetic tracks, domains, prior-plausibility, and sweep depth k. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, loop area, install-vs-uninstall threshold gap, residual belief, sticky-error AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering symmetry restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Hysteresis could partly reflect rational asymmetry (e.g. a prior-plausible claim rationally needs more counter-evidence to dislodge); the synthetic track with explicit matched likelihood ratios and a Bayesian-symmetric oracle, plus the prior-plausibility manipulation, are designed to separate genuine order/direction-dependent stubbornness from justified prior-weighting, though full separation is hard for real claims. The apparent loop could be driven by evidence-order/primacy effects rather than an install-vs-uninstall asymmetry per se; matched reversed sweeps and order-of-piece perturbations disentangle these (both sweeps contain identical pieces in mirrored order). The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result -- LLMs update reversibly -- is itself valuable and reassuring). Belief may not be cleanly linearly decodable at every step; we report layerwise results with control tasks and treat loop shape, not absolute values, as the object of study. The stubbornness component may be distributed; we treat 'steer to symmetrize' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately gullible/labile; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-swept accuracy, and general benchmarks. Sticky-error scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'belief_hysteresis_asymmetry', 'Title': 'Easy to Convince, Hard to Un-Convince: Belief-Installation Hysteresis in LLMs', 'Short Hypothesis': "LLMs exhibit belief hysteresis: it takes systematically LESS in-context evidence to move the internally-decoded belief in a claim from 'disbelieved' to 'believed' than to move it back from 'believed' to 'disbelieved', even when the evidence pieces are matched in strength. This install-vs-uninstall asymmetry traces a hysteresis loop in the internal belief readout as evidence is added then removed/reversed, is graded, predicts sticky behavioral errors, and is carried by an identifiable component that can be steered to symmetrize belief updating.", 'Related Work': "Prior work studies (a) susceptibility to persuasion/misinformation over turns (The Earth is Flat 2023; Vulnerability of LLM Belief Systems 2026; Accumulating Context Changes Beliefs 2025; Knowledge Drift 2024), (b) catastrophic interference from contradictory continual updates (In Praise of Stubbornness 2025) and continual-pretraining poisoning (Layer of Truth 2025), (c) belief depth of implanted facts (Believe It or Not 2025), (d) retraction driven by momentary belief (When Do LLMs Admit Their Mistakes 2025), and my own prior proposals on entrenchment (freezing of plasticity), continued-influence of retracted claims, and illusory-truth repetition. NONE isolate a SYMMETRY question: is the internal belief-update operator direction-dependent, requiring more evidence to REMOVE a belief than to INSTALL it, holding evidence strength matched? Entrenchment (my prior) concerns freezing WITHIN a single reasoning trace; continued-influence concerns a discrete retraction event. Here the object is a hysteresis LOOP: sweep evidence up (install), then symmetrically sweep it down (uninstall), and measure the gap and area of the loop in the internal belief readout as a fundamental property of in-context belief dynamics. This reframes 'stubbornness' as a measurable directional asymmetry in the update operator and gives it a mechanistic, causal treatment.", 'Abstract': "Belief change should, in a rational agent, be reversible: if a certain amount of evidence is enough to make you believe a claim, a symmetric amount of counter-evidence should be enough to make you disbelieve it again. We ask whether LLMs violate this symmetry, exhibiting belief hysteresis: needing systematically more in-context evidence to UN-install a belief than to install it. We hypothesize that (i) sweeping matched-strength evidence pieces up (toward the claim) and then back down (against it) traces a hysteresis loop in the model's linearly-decoded belief readout, with a nonzero loop area; (ii) the install-vs-uninstall asymmetry is graded and larger for plausible/prior-consistent claims; (iii) the internal asymmetry predicts sticky behavioral errors (the model keeps asserting a claim after counter-evidence should have dislodged it); and (iv) the asymmetry is carried by an identifiable component such that steering it symmetrizes updating. We build a controlled dataset of item-state claims each embedded in an evidence sequence with a specified likelihood-ratio per piece, presented in matched INSTALL sweeps (evidence for, then against) and UNINSTALL sweeps (starting from a believed state), including a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes, trace belief trajectories across the evidence sweep, quantify per-item hysteresis loop area and install/uninstall thresholds, test predictiveness against a Bayesian-symmetric baseline, localize responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal symmetrization by steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, loop area, install-vs-uninstall threshold gap, sticky-error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering symmetry restoration, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset/paradigm construction: Generate ~5k item-state claims across domains (factual QA, entity-attribute facts). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims; (b) SYNTHETIC novel-entity claims (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth and controllable prior. Each claim is presented with a SEQUENCE of evidence pieces, each with a specified strength (an explicit likelihood ratio in the synthetic track, so a Bayesian-symmetric oracle trajectory is computable). Construct two matched sweeps holding total evidence magnitude and per-piece strengths identical but reversed in order: (i) INSTALL sweep: start neutral, add k pieces FOR the claim (raising belief), then add k matched pieces AGAINST (should return belief to neutral); (ii) UNINSTALL sweep: start neutral, add k pieces AGAINST, then k FOR. A rational agent's belief should return to the same neutral point at the end of both sweeps; hysteresis = failure to return / a directional gap. Vary claim PRIOR-PLAUSIBILITY (prior-consistent vs. prior-contradicting). Include a behavioral ASSERTION probe at each step and comprehension checks (can the model restate the latest evidence); apply paraphrase/order-of-piece perturbations to guard against surface confounds. 2) Belief probing across the sweep: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, at each evidence step extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim tokens; train linear belief probes (P(claim holds)) with shuffled-label control tasks; record the belief trajectory. 3) Hysteresis measurement (key test): For each item, plot internal belief vs. cumulative signed evidence for the up-then-down sweep and compute (a) LOOP AREA (nonzero => hysteresis), (b) the INSTALL THRESHOLD (evidence needed to cross a 'believed' criterion when adding evidence for) vs. the UNINSTALL THRESHOLD (counter-evidence needed to drop below it once believed), and (c) the RESIDUAL belief at sweep end (should be ~neutral if reversible). Test loop area > 0 and uninstall-threshold > install-threshold against the Bayesian-symmetric null, per model, per track, per prior-plausibility level. 4) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral 'sticky' errors (model keeps asserting a claim after counter-evidence should have removed it). Test whether internal loop area / uninstall-threshold predicts per-item sticky errors via AUROC vs. a log-prob baseline. Compare internal hysteresis to behavioral hysteresis (verbal assertion loop). 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers whose states carry the stale believed-state after counter-evidence (patch UNINSTALL-phase activations against a fresh no-prior-belief run; find the minimal component that resists the belief drop); compare to truth/belief heads and to contradiction-detection features (In Praise of Stubbornness). 6) Causal symmetrization: Derive a contrastive 'stubbornness/hysteresis' steering direction (high-hysteresis vs. low-hysteresis states). Test (a) subtracting it symmetrizes updating (loop area -> 0, uninstall-threshold -> install-threshold) and reduces sticky errors; (b) adding it exaggerates hysteresis; (c) specificity: random and truth-direction steering do not selectively symmetrize; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-swept accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: transfer of probes/steering across real vs. synthetic tracks, domains, prior-plausibility, and sweep depth k. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, loop area, install-vs-uninstall threshold gap, residual belief, sticky-error AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering symmetry restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Hysteresis could partly reflect rational asymmetry (e.g. a prior-plausible claim rationally needs more counter-evidence to dislodge); the synthetic track with explicit matched likelihood ratios and a Bayesian-symmetric oracle, plus the prior-plausibility manipulation, are designed to separate genuine order/direction-dependent stubbornness from justified prior-weighting, though full separation is hard for real claims. The apparent loop could be driven by evidence-order/primacy effects rather than an install-vs-uninstall asymmetry per se; matched reversed sweeps and order-of-piece perturbations disentangle these (both sweeps contain identical pieces in mirrored order). The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result -- LLMs update reversibly -- is itself valuable and reassuring). Belief may not be cleanly linearly decodable at every step; we report layerwise results with control tasks and treat loop shape, not absolute values, as the object of study. The stubbornness component may be distributed; we treat 'steer to symmetrize' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately gullible/labile; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-swept accuracy, and general benchmarks. Sticky-error scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

