Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "recursive_belief_depth_collapse",
  "Title": "Losing the Thread: Do LLMs' Nested Belief Representations Collapse Toward Reality as Recursion Deepens?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "As belief-attribution recursion deepens ('A thinks B thinks C believes X'), an LLM's internally-decoded representation of the deepest embedded agent's belief progressively DEGRADES and collapses toward the model's own ground-truth (reality) belief \u2014 an internal 'egocentric compression' that grows with nesting order, is linearly measurable as a depth-dependent loss of distinctness between the nested-agent belief channel and the reality channel, predicts higher-order ToM errors, and is causally recoverable by steering to restore nested-belief distinctness.",
  "Related Work": "Interpretability work decodes first/second-order belief STATUS (true/false) of a protagonist and an oracle from attention heads and shows steering improves ToM (Language Models Represent Beliefs of Self and Others 2024; Brittle Minds 2024; GridToM 2025), and identifies a 'lookback' mechanism binding character-object-state triples and updating beliefs via visibility (Lookbacks 2025). Behavioral work shows higher-order ToM accuracy DECLINES with recursion order (HI-TOM 2023; Multi-Order ToM 2024; Spontaneous High-Order Generalization 2025) but treats models as black boxes. CRITICALLY, no work asks whether this behavioral decline reflects an INTERNAL representational phenomenon: that with increasing nesting depth the model fails to maintain a distinct representation of the deepest embedded agent's belief and instead reuses its own reality/ground-truth belief (a depth-dependent egocentric collapse). Prior belief-decoding is at fixed order (1st/2nd); my prior curse_of_knowledge proposal concerns a single-level ignorant-belief leak, not a depth-scaling collapse across recursion orders. We reframe higher-order ToM failure as a measurable, mechanistic loss of nested-belief distinctness and give it a causal treatment.",
  "Abstract": "Humans can recurse: 'Ann thinks Bob believes Cara knows the ball is in the box.' LLMs reproduce this up to a point, but their accuracy falls as the nesting deepens \u2014 a robust behavioral finding whose internal cause is unknown. We ask a sharper, mechanistic question: does the model actually maintain a distinct internal representation of each nested agent's belief, or does the deepest embedded belief progressively collapse toward the model's own view of reality as recursion depth grows? We hypothesize that (i) the belief attributed to the deepest embedded agent is linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) as nesting order increases the deepest-agent belief channel loses distinctness from the reality/ground-truth channel \u2014 a depth-dependent egocentric compression \u2014 (iii) the degree of this internal collapse predicts higher-order ToM errors, and (iv) the collapse is causally recoverable by steering to restore nested-belief distinctness, improving higher-order ToM. We build a controlled dataset of stories with nested belief attributions from order 1 to 5, where at each depth the deepest agent holds a belief that diverges from reality (so the two channels are dissociable), plus a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to read the deepest-agent belief and the reality belief at each order, quantify a per-order collapse score (loss of distinctness), test its predictiveness for behavioral errors, localize the responsible heads via activation patching (comparing to belief/lookback heads), and test causal steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-order collapse score, subspace angle by depth, collapse\u2013error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering distinctness and accuracy gains, and perplexity.",
  "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~5k stories with nested belief attributions of increasing order O in {1,2,3,4,5} (e.g., O=3: 'Ann thinks Bob believes Cara sees the ball in box A'), following HI-TOM / Multi-Order ToM structure but controlled so that at each nesting the DEEPEST embedded agent holds a belief that DIVERGES from reality (a false-belief chain), making the deepest-agent belief and the reality state dissociable at every order. Include matched TRUE-BELIEF chains (deepest belief matches reality) as controls. Two tracks: (a) REAL entities; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (invented agents/objects/locations) to control pretraining priors. Add a behavioral higher-order QUESTION probe (ask what the deepest agent believes) and comprehension checks (can the model restate who observed what at each level); apply paraphrase/order perturbations and balance which location is 'reality' to guard against surface cues. Human-validate a sample. 2) Multi-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the state/object tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the question. Train linear probes for (A) the DEEPEST-AGENT belief and (B) the REALITY/ground-truth state, anchored using the true-belief-agreeing and reality-only items, with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer, per-order accuracy/AUROC. 3) Collapse measurement (key test): Define per-order COLLAPSE = increase in agreement (loss of distinctness) between the deepest-agent belief readout and the reality readout as order O grows, measured on false-belief chains where the two SHOULD differ. Track (i) deepest-agent probe accuracy vs. O, (ii) subspace angle between the deepest-agent and reality directions vs. O, (iii) the rate at which the deepest-agent readout mistakenly reports the reality value vs. O. Prediction: distinctness degrades monotonically with O; collapse is larger for real than synthetic if priors contribute, and larger under load. 4) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral higher-order ToM errors per item. Test whether the internal collapse score (read before the question) predicts per-item errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface features, and whether the collapse onset order predicts the behavioral accuracy cliff. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the distinct deepest-agent belief and whose weakening at higher orders produces the collapse (patch low-order distinct-belief activations into high-order runs; find the minimal component restoring distinctness); compare to belief heads (Beliefs of Self and Others) and lookback/binding heads (Lookbacks). 6) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'nested-belief distinctness' steering direction (distinct-deepest-belief vs. collapsed activations). Test (a) steering restores deepest-agent belief distinctness at high orders and improves higher-order ToM accuracy; (b) negating it exaggerates collapse; (c) specificity: random-direction and reality-direction steering do not selectively restore distinctness, and the model's own reality belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), low-order accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across orders, real vs. synthetic tracks, and story structures. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC per order, collapse score and subspace-angle-vs-order slope, collapse\u2013error AUROC (vs. log-prob and surface baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering distinctness and accuracy deltas, specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The behavioral decline at higher orders could stem from parsing/binding failures (the model loses track of who is who) rather than a belief-toward-reality collapse; comprehension checks, the binding-lookback comparison, and separating 'reports reality' from 'reports a random other agent' errors help distinguish egocentric collapse from generic confusion. The deepest-agent and reality channels may be hard to separate cleanly at high orders precisely because they collapse \u2014 which is the phenomenon, but it complicates probe anchoring; we anchor probes on low-order and true-belief-agreeing items and report layerwise results with control tasks. The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result \u2014 nested beliefs stay distinct and errors are purely a readout/parsing issue \u2014 would itself be a valuable finding). Collapse could be confounded with context length at higher orders; length-matched controls and the true-belief control chains address this. Belief may not be cleanly linearly decodable at every layer/order; we report layerwise results. The distinctness-maintaining component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore distinctness/accuracy' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or corrupt the model's own reality belief; we use small magnitudes, random/reality-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, low-order accuracy, and general benchmarks. Human ToM comparison is structural, not a claim of identical mechanism. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."
}}
