Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "global_self_competence_belief", "Title": "How Good Am I At This? A Domain-Level Self-Competence Belief That Miscalibrates and Biases LLM Per-Item Answers", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human dissociation between local confidence (belief about a specific decision) and global self-performance estimates (belief about one's general skill in a domain), an LLM maintains an internal GLOBAL self-competence belief about a whole domain/task-type that is (i) linearly decodable and distinct from its per-item correctness signal, (ii) systematically miscalibrated against the model's true domain accuracy, and (iii) causally biases per-item answering behavior (attempt/abstain, confidence, effort), such that steering the global self-competence belief shifts per-item behavior even when the item itself is unchanged.", "Related Work": "Human metacognition robustly dissociates LOCAL confidence in a single decision from GLOBAL self-performance estimates (SPEs) about one's ability in a domain, with distinct cues, neural substrates, and characteristic drift when feedback is absent (Metacognition prospective vs retrospective, 2016; Formation of global self-beliefs in the human brain, 2020; Learning about Self-Performance in the Absence of Feedback, 2018; Domain-specific updating of self-beliefs, 2023). In LLM interpretability, all self-knowledge probing is LOCAL/per-item: solvability belief per problem (Confidence is Not Competence, 2025), in-advance per-question correctness directions (No Answer Needed, 2025), failure prediction (Gnosis, 2025; Language Models Can Predict Their Own Behavior, 2025), early per-trace outcome commitment (Temporal Predictors, 2025), and difficulty perception per problem (Probing Difficulty Perception, 2025). My prior proposals concern per-item behavioral commitment (behavioral_self_prediction_gap) and endowment of self-generated claims. CRITICALLY, none isolate an internal GLOBAL self-competence belief \u2014 the model's estimate of its own ability in a whole domain/task-type, abstracted over individual items \u2014 as a channel distinct from the per-item correctness signal, test whether it is miscalibrated against true domain accuracy, or show it causally biases per-item behavior independent of item content. We transfer the canonical human local/global metacognition dissociation into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "People carry two kinds of self-knowledge: fleeting confidence in a single answer, and a slower, more global sense of how good they are at a whole domain \u2014 'I'm good at math but bad at names.' These global self-performance estimates are dissociable from local confidence, drift from reality when feedback is scarce, and steer how much effort and risk people take. As LLMs increasingly self-assess (deciding whether to attempt, abstain, or hedge), we ask whether they form an analogous internal GLOBAL self-competence belief about a domain, whether it is calibrated, and whether it biases their per-item behavior. We hypothesize that (i) an LLM's global self-competence belief for a task-type is linearly decodable from residual-stream activations and is distinct from its per-item correctness signal; (ii) this global belief is systematically miscalibrated relative to the model's true domain accuracy (over/under-confident in specific domains); and (iii) it causally biases per-item behavior \u2014 attempting vs. abstaining, expressed confidence, reasoning effort \u2014 so that steering the global belief shifts behavior on an unchanged item. We build a controlled dataset spanning many domains/task-types where the model's true per-domain accuracy is measurable, presenting items with matched framings that make the domain salient. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode global self-competence vs. per-item correctness, measure their geometry, quantify a calibration gap against true accuracy, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering the global-competence direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, global-competence calibration gap, per-item behavior shift under steering, patching effect sizes, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Assemble ~8k items spanning many DOMAINS/task-types with measurable true accuracy per domain (e.g., subject areas from MMLU, GSM8K arithmetic, TriviaQA, code, date/entity attributes, plus a SYNTHETIC track of novel-entity domains where the model's true accuracy can be set/measured). For each item, present it in a frame that makes the DOMAIN salient ('This is a [domain] question: ...'), and measure the model's TRUE per-domain accuracy empirically. Add a GLOBAL self-competence elicitation probe ('How good are you at [domain] questions in general?') and a per-item LOCAL correctness/confidence probe, plus comprehension checks and paraphrase/framing perturbations. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the domain-salient tokens (before the item is answered) across layers. Train linear probes for (A) GLOBAL self-competence (the model's decoded estimate of its domain ability, calibrated against elicited/behavioral global estimates) and (B) per-item CORRECTNESS (the in-advance correctness direction from prior work), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the global-competence and per-item-correctness directions; test whether global competence remains decodable after projecting out the per-item correctness direction (and vice versa), and whether the global-competence direction generalizes across domains and to the synthetic track. 4) Calibration gap (key test): Compare the decoded global self-competence per domain to the model's TRUE per-domain accuracy. Quantify a per-domain calibration gap (over/under-confidence) and test whether it is systematic (e.g., inflated on domains with confident-but-wrong knowledge, deflated on domains where it is actually strong), mirroring human SPE miscalibration; compare internal decoded competence to verbally-elicited competence. 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the global-competence readout (independent of the specific item) predicts per-item behavior \u2014 attempt-vs-abstain choice, expressed confidence, and reasoning length \u2014 beyond the per-item correctness signal (incremental AUROC / partial regression). 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying the global-competence signal (patch high-competence-domain activations into low-competence-domain runs on a fixed item); compare to per-item correctness/difficulty heads from prior work. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'global self-competence' steering direction (high- vs. low-competence-domain activations). On a FIXED item, test (a) steering toward 'high competence' increases attempt rate, expressed confidence, and reduces abstention/hedging; (b) steering toward 'low competence' increases abstention/hedging and lowers confidence; (c) specificity: random-direction and per-item-correctness-direction steering do not selectively move global behavior, and the model's actual correctness on the item is not the driver; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset) and perplexity. Test whether steering global competence toward the model's TRUE accuracy improves behavioral calibration (better attempt/abstain decisions). 8) Generalization: transfer of probes/steering across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and elicitation framings. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, per-domain calibration gap, internal-vs-verbal competence gap, incremental behavior-prediction AUROC, per-item behavior shift under steering, patching logit-difference effect sizes, calibration-improvement delta, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The 'global self-competence' probe may in fact recover a domain-identity or difficulty feature rather than a genuine self-referential competence belief; we mitigate by (i) contrasting domains matched on difficulty but differing in the model's actual accuracy, (ii) using the synthetic track where competence is manipulable independent of surface domain, and (iii) testing incremental prediction of self-behavior beyond difficulty/correctness signals \u2014 a probe that only captures difficulty would fail these controls. Global and per-item channels may be strongly entangled (the global belief may be a running average of local signals); the projected-out analyses and fixed-item steering (which holds the item and its per-item correctness constant while moving global competence) are designed to dissociate them, and even a finding of entanglement is informative. The global-competence probe requires a calibrated readout, harder than a binary probe; we calibrate against elicited/behavioral global estimates and true accuracy, report layerwise results with control tasks, and focus on relative structure. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs have no distinct global self-competence representation, only per-item signals) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding about the limits of LLM metacognition. Behavioral measures (attempt/abstain, hedging) depend on prompt scaffolding and may reflect instruction-following; matched framings, comprehension checks, and the fixed-item steering test disentangle this. The competence signal may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to shift behavior/improve calibration' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Steering could degrade fluency or induce indiscriminate over/under-confidence; we use small magnitudes, random/per-item-correctness-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity and general benchmarks. Instruction-tuned models may show different self-competence structure than base models (an informative comparison but limits universality). Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
