e responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering the global-competence direction. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, global-competence calibration gap, per-item behavior shift under steering, patching effect sizes, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Assemble ~8k items spanning many DOMAINS/task-types with measurable true accuracy per domain (e.g., subject areas from MMLU, GSM8K arithmetic, TriviaQA, code, date/entity attributes, plus a SYNTHETIC track of novel-entity domains where the model's true accuracy can be set/measured). For each item, present it in a frame that makes the DOMAIN salient ('This is a [domain] question: ...'), and measure the model's TRUE per-domain accuracy empirically. Add a GLOBAL self-competence elicitation probe ('How good are you at [domain] questions in general?') and a per-item LOCAL correctness/confidence probe, plus comprehension checks and paraphrase/framing perturbations. 2) Two-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the domain-salient tokens (before the item is answered) across layers. Train linear probes for (A) GLOBAL self-competence (the model's decoded estimate of its domain ability, calibrated against elicited/behavioral global estimates) and (B) per-item CORRECTNESS (the in-advance correctness direction from prior work), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the global-competence and per-item-correctness directions; test whether global competence remains decodable after projecting out the per-item correctness direction (and vice versa), and whether the global-competence direction generalizes across domains and to the synthetic track. 4) Calibration gap (key test): Compare the decoded global self-competence per domain to the model's TRUE per-domain accuracy. Quantify a per-domain calibration gap (over