ty/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The 'global self-competence' probe may in fact recover a domain-identity or difficulty feature rather than a genuine self-referential competence belief; we mitigate by (i) contrasting domains matched on difficulty but differing in the model's actual accuracy, (ii) using the synthetic track where competence is manipulable independent of surface domain, and (iii) testing incremental prediction of self-behavior beyond difficulty/correctness signals — a probe that only captures difficulty would fail these controls. Global and per-item channels may be strongly entangled (the global belief may be a running average of local signals); the projected-out analyses and fixed-item steering (which holds the item and its per-item correctness constant while moving global competence) are designed to dissociate them, and even a finding of entanglement is informative. The global-competence probe requires a calibrated readout, harder than a binary probe; we calibrate against elicited/behavioral global estimates and true accuracy, report layerwise results with control tasks, and focus on relative structure. The effect may be absent internally — a robust negative result (LLMs have no distinct global self-competence representation, only per-item signals) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding about the limits of LLM metacognition. Behavioral measures (attempt/abstain, hedging) depend on prompt scaffolding and may reflect instruction-following; matched framings, comprehension checks, and the fixed-item steering test disentangle this. The competence signal may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to shift behavior/improve calibration' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact component. Steering could degrade fluency or induce indiscriminate over/under-confidence; we use small magnitudes, random/per-item-correctness-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity and general benchmarks. Instruction-tun