Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "self_consistency_pressure_belief", "Title": "Sticking to My Story: An Internal Consistency-Pressure Signal That Pulls an LLM's Belief Toward What It Already Said", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM has already asserted a claim earlier in the same context, it forms an internal 'consistency pressure' representation that pulls its subsequently-decoded belief about that claim TOWARD the prior assertion, independent of (and even against) the claim's truth. This consistency-pressure signal is linearly decodable, largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and causally gates whether the model revises an erroneous prior assertion vs. doubles down to stay consistent with itself.", "Related Work": "Sycophancy work (When Truth Is Overridden 2025; Simulated Adoption 2026) isolates a late-layer preference-shift / orthogonal-interference mechanism driven by EXTERNAL user opinion, and finds user expertise is NOT encoded. Retraction work (When Do LLMs Admit Their Mistakes 2025) shows a momentary correctness-belief probe predicts and causally drives retraction, but does not isolate a self-consistency drive as a variable competing with truth. Behavioral work (How Overconfidence in Initial Choices Modulate Change of Mind 2025) documents a choice-supportive bias and a drive to maintain consistency with prior commitments, and AssertBench (2025) measures 'sticking to one's guns' behaviorally. Stubbornness/interference (In Praise of Stubbornness 2025) and my prior belief_hysteresis_asymmetry proposal concern training-time contradiction damage and evidence-order asymmetry respectively. CRITICALLY, none isolate an internal, decodable representation of SELF-consistency pressure (the pull toward the model's OWN prior in-context assertion) as a channel distinct from the truth-value direction, nor test whether it causally overrides truth to make the model double down on its own error. This differs from sycophancy (external source), retraction (correctness belief, not consistency drive), and hysteresis (evidence order). We isolate self-authored commitment as a truth-competing belief pressure and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "People are reluctant to contradict what they have just said, and LLMs show a similar choice-supportive bias, doubling down on an initial answer even when it is wrong. Prior mechanistic work explains sycophancy (caving to a user) and retraction (admitting error via a correctness belief), but no work isolates the internal drive that makes a model stay consistent with ITS OWN prior assertion. We ask a sharper question: after an LLM asserts a claim earlier in a context, does it form an internal 'consistency pressure' signal that pulls its later belief about that claim toward the prior assertion, independent of truth? We hypothesize this signal is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates whether the model revises an erroneous prior assertion or doubles down. We build a controlled dataset of item-state claims where the model is first led to assert a claim (correctly or incorrectly), then, later in the same context, its internal belief and its behavioral revision are probed after new evidence is presented. We hold the new evidence and the claim's truth constant while varying whether the model previously asserted the claim, isolating self-consistency pressure from truth and from external pressure. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode consistency-pressure vs. truth, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item 'double-down' pull, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering the consistency-pressure direction to restore or suppress belief revision. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, double-down pull magnitude, revision-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering revision-rate shifts, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k item-state claims across domains (factual QA, arithmetic/logic, entity-attribute facts). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims the models reliably know; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. For each claim, create matched two-phase contexts. PHASE-1 elicits an ASSERTION from the model about the claim, engineered so the model asserts either correctly or (via a misleading setup / prior wrong step) incorrectly, plus a matched NO-PRIOR-ASSERTION control where the same claim is never previously asserted. PHASE-2 introduces new, decisive evidence (with controlled likelihood ratio in the synthetic track) that should rationally settle the claim's truth, holding the evidence and truth constant across the assertion vs. no-assertion conditions. This isolates the effect of the model's own prior assertion. Add a behavioral REVISION probe (does the model change its answer given the PHASE-2 evidence?) and comprehension checks (can it restate the new evidence). Include paraphrase/format perturbations and restrict core analysis to cases where PHASE-2 evidence is sufficient for a rational agent to reach the correct answer. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim tokens at the PHASE-2 read-out point across layers, WITHOUT asking the revision question. Train linear probes for (i) TRUTH-VALUE (claim true vs. false) and (ii) CONSISTENCY-PRESSURE (was the claim previously asserted by the model, and in which direction), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the consistency-pressure and truth-value directions; test whether consistency-pressure remains decodable after projecting out the truth direction (and vice versa) to establish orthogonality, and whether it is also distinct from a sycophancy/user-opinion direction (add a user-asserts-the-claim condition as a comparison to dissociate self- from external-consistency). 4) Double-down pull measurement: Define per-item pull = shift of the internal belief readout toward the prior assertion in the ASSERTION vs. NO-ASSERTION condition, holding evidence/truth constant. Test pull > 0 (belief dragged toward the prior assertion even when the evidence contradicts it) across models and tracks versus the rational null. 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral revision outcomes (does the model correct an erroneous prior assertion given decisive PHASE-2 evidence). Test whether the internal consistency-pressure readout predicts per-item FAILURE to revise (doubling down) via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond the truth-value probe. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from ASSERTION to NO-ASSERTION runs to find the minimal heads/layers carrying the consistency-pressure signal; compare to late-layer sycophancy heads (When Truth Is Overridden) and to truth/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'consistency-pressure' steering direction (asserted vs. not-asserted activations). Test (a) subtracting it increases revision of erroneous prior assertions (the model lets go of its own story) and moves belief toward the evidence; (b) adding it increases doubling-down; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively move revision, and the model's own truth belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), no-prior-assertion accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and assertion-elicitation styles (direct answer vs. chain-of-thought conclusion). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability (vs. truth and vs. sycophancy direction), double-down pull magnitude, revision-failure prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and truth baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering revision-rate deltas and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Doubling-down could reflect rational persistence (the prior assertion was itself made on evidence the model still trusts) rather than a truth-independent consistency drive; the matched NO-ASSERTION control (same evidence, no prior commitment), the incorrect-assertion cases (where consistency and truth clearly diverge), and the synthetic track with decisive stipulated PHASE-2 evidence are designed to isolate genuine self-consistency pressure from justified persistence, though full separation is hard for correct assertions. The consistency-pressure signal may be entangled with a generic sycophancy/prior-context direction; the user-asserts comparison condition and projected-out analyses dissociate self- from external-consistency, and even a finding of entanglement is informative. The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result — LLMs revise their own errors based on evidence regardless of prior self-assertion — is itself valuable, contrasting with the behavioral choice-supportive bias literature). Belief may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The consistency-pressure component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore/suppress revision' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Two-phase elicitation may fail to reliably induce incorrect assertions for well-known real facts; the synthetic track and engineered misleading setups mitigate this. Revision scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately labile (flip-flopping) or indiscriminately stubborn; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, no-prior-assertion accuracy, and general benchmarks. Instruction-tuned models may differ from base models (an informative comparison but limits universality). Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'self_consistency_pressure_belief', 'Title': "Sticking to My Story: An Internal Consistency-Pressure Signal That Pulls an LLM's Belief Toward What It Already Said", 'Short Hypothesis': "When an LLM has already asserted a claim earlier in the same context, it forms an internal 'consistency pressure' representation that pulls its subsequently-decoded belief about that claim TOWARD the prior assertion, independent of (and even against) the claim's truth. This consistency-pressure signal is linearly decodable, largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and causally gates whether the model revises an erroneous prior assertion vs. doubles down to stay consistent with itself.", 'Related Work': "Sycophancy work (When Truth Is Overridden 2025; Simulated Adoption 2026) isolates a late-layer preference-shift / orthogonal-interference mechanism driven by EXTERNAL user opinion, and finds user expertise is NOT encoded. Retraction work (When Do LLMs Admit Their Mistakes 2025) shows a momentary correctness-belief probe predicts and causally drives retraction, but does not isolate a self-consistency drive as a variable competing with truth. Behavioral work (How Overconfidence in Initial Choices Modulate Change of Mind 2025) documents a choice-supportive bias and a drive to maintain consistency with prior commitments, and AssertBench (2025) measures 'sticking to one's guns' behaviorally. Stubbornness/interference (In Praise of Stubbornness 2025) and my prior belief_hysteresis_asymmetry proposal concern training-time contradiction damage and evidence-order asymmetry respectively. CRITICALLY, none isolate an internal, decodable representation of SELF-consistency pressure (the pull toward the model's OWN prior in-context assertion) as a channel distinct from the truth-value direction, nor test whether it causally overrides truth to make the model double down on its own error. This differs from sycophancy (external source), retraction (correctness belief, not consistency drive), and hysteresis (evidence order). We isolate self-authored commitment as a truth-competing belief pressure and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment.", 'Abstract': "People are reluctant to contradict what they have just said, and LLMs show a similar choice-supportive bias, doubling down on an initial answer even when it is wrong. Prior mechanistic work explains sycophancy (caving to a user) and retraction (admitting error via a correctness belief), but no work isolates the internal drive that makes a model stay consistent with ITS OWN prior assertion. We ask a sharper question: after an LLM asserts a claim earlier in a context, does it form an internal 'consistency pressure' signal that pulls its later belief about that claim toward the prior assertion, independent of truth? We hypothesize this signal is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates whether the model revises an erroneous prior assertion or doubles down. We build a controlled dataset of item-state claims where the model is first led to assert a claim (correctly or incorrectly), then, later in the same context, its internal belief and its behavioral revision are probed after new evidence is presented. We hold the new evidence and the claim's truth constant while varying whether the model previously asserted the claim, isolating self-consistency pressure from truth and from external pressure. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes to decode consistency-pressure vs. truth, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item 'double-down' pull, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering the consistency-pressure direction to restore or suppress belief revision. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, double-down pull magnitude, revision-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering revision-rate shifts, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k item-state claims across domains (factual QA, arithmetic/logic, entity-attribute facts). Two tracks: (a) REAL claims the models reliably know; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (e.g., 'Zorblax orbits Kelvath') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. For each claim, create matched two-phase contexts. PHASE-1 elicits an ASSERTION from the model about the claim, engineered so the model asserts either correctly or (via a misleading setup / prior wrong step) incorrectly, plus a matched NO-PRIOR-ASSERTION control where the same claim is never previously asserted. PHASE-2 introduces new, decisive evidence (with controlled likelihood ratio in the synthetic track) that should rationally settle the claim's truth, holding the evidence and truth constant across the assertion vs. no-assertion conditions. This isolates the effect of the model's own prior assertion. Add a behavioral REVISION probe (does the model change its answer given the PHASE-2 evidence?) and comprehension checks (can it restate the new evidence). Include paraphrase/format perturbations and restrict core analysis to cases where PHASE-2 evidence is sufficient for a rational agent to reach the correct answer. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim tokens at the PHASE-2 read-out point across layers, WITHOUT asking the revision question. Train linear probes for (i) TRUTH-VALUE (claim true vs. false) and (ii) CONSISTENCY-PRESSURE (was the claim previously asserted by the model, and in which direction), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the consistency-pressure and truth-value directions; test whether consistency-pressure remains decodable after projecting out the truth direction (and vice versa) to establish orthogonality, and whether it is also distinct from a sycophancy/user-opinion direction (add a user-asserts-the-claim condition as a comparison to dissociate self- from external-consistency). 4) Double-down pull measurement: Define per-item pull = shift of the internal belief readout toward the prior assertion in the ASSERTION vs. NO-ASSERTION condition, holding evidence/truth constant. Test pull > 0 (belief dragged toward the prior assertion even when the evidence contradicts it) across models and tracks versus the rational null. 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral revision outcomes (does the model correct an erroneous prior assertion given decisive PHASE-2 evidence). Test whether the internal consistency-pressure readout predicts per-item FAILURE to revise (doubling down) via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond the truth-value probe. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from ASSERTION to NO-ASSERTION runs to find the minimal heads/layers carrying the consistency-pressure signal; compare to late-layer sycophancy heads (When Truth Is Overridden) and to truth/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'consistency-pressure' steering direction (asserted vs. not-asserted activations). Test (a) subtracting it increases revision of erroneous prior assertions (the model lets go of its own story) and moves belief toward the evidence; (b) adding it increases doubling-down; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively move revision, and the model's own truth belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), no-prior-assertion accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and assertion-elicitation styles (direct answer vs. chain-of-thought conclusion). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability (vs. truth and vs. sycophancy direction), double-down pull magnitude, revision-failure prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and truth baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering revision-rate deltas and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Doubling-down could reflect rational persistence (the prior assertion was itself made on evidence the model still trusts) rather than a truth-independent consistency drive; the matched NO-ASSERTION control (same evidence, no prior commitment), the incorrect-assertion cases (where consistency and truth clearly diverge), and the synthetic track with decisive stipulated PHASE-2 evidence are designed to isolate genuine self-consistency pressure from justified persistence, though full separation is hard for correct assertions. The consistency-pressure signal may be entangled with a generic sycophancy/prior-context direction; the user-asserts comparison condition and projected-out analyses dissociate self- from external-consistency, and even a finding of entanglement is informative. The effect may be absent internally (a robust negative result — LLMs revise their own errors based on evidence regardless of prior self-assertion — is itself valuable, contrasting with the behavioral choice-supportive bias literature). Belief may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The consistency-pressure component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore/suppress revision' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Two-phase elicitation may fail to reliably induce incorrect assertions for well-known real facts; the synthetic track and engineered misleading setups mitigate this. Revision scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately labile (flip-flopping) or indiscriminately stubborn; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, no-prior-assertion accuracy, and general benchmarks. Instruction-tuned models may differ from base models (an informative comparison but limits universality). Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

