Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "counterfactual_dual_meaning_belief", "Title": "Two Worlds at Once: Do LLMs Internally Hold Both the Supposed and the Backgrounded-Factual Meaning of a Counterfactual?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the canonical psycholinguistic 'dual meaning' of counterfactual conditionals (a subjunctive like 'If Tom had studied, he would have passed' conveys BOTH a supposed dependency AND the backgrounded fact that Tom did NOT study and failed), an LLM processing a counterfactual simultaneously maintains two internally-decodable belief layers about the same proposition: the SUPPOSED belief (P holds in the counterfactual world) and the BACKGROUNDED-FACTUAL belief (P is actually false). These two layers are separately linearly decodable, largely orthogonal, and causally dissociable: which layer dominates the model's downstream answer can be steered, and failures to maintain the factual layer produce a characteristic error (treating the counterfactual antecedent as actually true).", "Related Work": "Cognitive/psycholinguistic work robustly establishes counterfactual DUAL MEANING: subjunctive-counterfactuals express a supposition while implying the opposite factual state of affairs (Understanding Counterfactuality: A Review, Kulakova & Nieuwland 2016, 119 citations; Marking the counterfactual: ERP evidence for pragmatic processing of German subjunctives 2014, 48 citations). In LLM interpretability, 'Counterfactual reasoning: in-context emergence' (2025) finds a latent concept is linearly encoded with noise-abduction heads, and the 'Competition of Mechanisms' line (Ortu et al. 2024; reproductions 2025) traces how factual-recall vs. in-context counterfactual tokens compete, localizing factual/counterfactual info to subject/attribute positions and attention heads. Truth-probe work isolates a single linear truth direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024). CRITICALLY, the competition-of-mechanisms work treats fact vs. counterfactual as a WINNER-TAKE-ALL competition for the output token and does NOT ask whether the model concurrently maintains TWO belief layers (supposed AND backgrounded-factual) about the SAME proposition, as human dual-meaning theory predicts; nor does it isolate these as separately decodable, orthogonal, steerable channels, nor connect the loss of the factual layer to the specific error of endorsing a false antecedent. My prior suppositional_belief_gating proposal concerns a binary endorsed-vs-entertained framing flag; this proposal is specifically about the two CO-PRESENT belief layers that are the signature of counterfactual conditionals. We transfer a canonical cognitive-science phenomenon (counterfactual dual meaning) into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "When we say 'If the dice had been rigged, the game would have been unfair,' we simultaneously entertain a hypothetical world (rigged dice, unfair game) AND signal a fact about the real world (the dice were NOT rigged, the game WAS fair). Decades of psycholinguistics establish this counterfactual 'dual meaning' as a hallmark of human comprehension. LLMs increasingly reason under counterfactual premises (planning, causal reasoning, hypotheticals, safety scenarios), yet it is unknown whether they preserve both layers or collapse into one. We ask a sharp mechanistic question: when an LLM reads a counterfactual conditional, does it internally maintain TWO separable belief layers about the antecedent proposition P \u2014 a SUPPOSED belief (P holds, in the counterfactual world) and a BACKGROUNDED-FACTUAL belief (P is actually false)? We hypothesize both layers are (i) separately linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal, and (iii) causally dissociable, such that steering can shift which layer governs downstream answers, and that incomplete maintenance of the factual layer produces a characteristic error \u2014 treating the false antecedent as actually true. We build a controlled dataset of counterfactual conditionals, matched indicative/factual and simple-suppositional frames, and synthetic-entity items to control priors, plus factual-inference probes ('In reality, is P true?') and consequent-inference probes. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear probes for the supposed and factual layers, measure their geometry, localize the responsible heads via activation patching (comparing to competition-of-mechanisms heads), and test causal steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, layer-dominance shift under steering, patching effect sizes, factual-endorsement error rate, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k items each anchored on a proposition P and rendered in matched frames: (a) COUNTERFACTUAL subjunctive ('If P had been the case, then Q would have happened' \u2014 implying NOT-P in reality); (b) INDICATIVE/FACTUAL control ('P is the case, so Q happens'); (c) SIMPLE-SUPPOSITION control ('Suppose P; then Q' \u2014 open supposition, no factual implication about P). Two tracks: (i) REAL propositions the model can evaluate; (ii) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (e.g., 'If Zorblax had orbited Kelvath, ...') with stipulated ground truth to control pretraining priors. Add probes: a FACTUAL-INFERENCE probe ('In reality, is P true?' \u2014 correct answer for counterfactuals is NO), a SUPPOSED/CONSEQUENT-INFERENCE probe ('Within the described scenario, does Q hold?'), and comprehension checks. Apply paraphrase/mood-cue perturbations to guard against surface-cue artifacts (e.g., 'had'/'would'). Human-validate a sample. 2) Two-layer probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the antecedent P tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking a question. Train linear probes for (A) SUPPOSED belief in P (P holds in the counterfactual world) and (B) BACKGROUNDED-FACTUAL belief in P (P is actually false), anchored using the indicative-factual and simple-supposition frames plus reality-only items, with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry / co-presence (key test): Test that in the COUNTERFACTUAL condition BOTH layers are simultaneously decodable (supposed-P high AND factual-P low), unlike the indicative frame (both align) or a hallucinated-fact frame. Compute cosine/subspace angle between the supposed and factual directions; test whether the factual layer remains decodable after projecting out the supposed layer (and vice versa) to establish separability. Track per-layer emergence of each layer (does the factual/backgrounded layer arise later, mirroring pragmatic processing?). 4) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral factual-endorsement errors (model wrongly says P is actually true, i.e., collapses to the supposed layer). Test whether the internal strength of the backgrounded-factual layer (read before query) predicts per-item correct factual inference via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface features. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying each layer, and those whose weakening collapses the factual layer into the supposed one (patch counterfactual activations into indicative runs and vice versa); compare to the factual/counterfactual competition heads and subject/attribute positions from Ortu et al. and noise-abduction heads from counterfactual-emergence work. 6) Causal control: Derive contrastive steering directions for each layer (counterfactual-supposed vs. factual activations). Test (a) amplifying the supposed layer / suppressing the factual layer increases false-antecedent endorsement (the model treats the counterfactual premise as real); (b) amplifying the factual layer restores correct factual inference while preserving in-scenario consequent reasoning; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively move layer dominance, and consequent-inference within the scenario is preserved when appropriate; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), indicative-frame accuracy, and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across counterfactual types (past/present subjunctive, nested counterfactuals), real vs. synthetic tracks, and mood-cue phrasings. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, dual-layer co-presence rate, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, per-layer emergence curves, factual-inference prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and surface baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, layer-dominance shift and false-antecedent-endorsement deltas under steering, specificity checks, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The 'supposed' and 'backgrounded-factual' layers may be hard to separate cleanly because both concern the same proposition P; the matched indicative/simple-supposition frames, synthetic-entity track, and probes anchored on frames where the two layers agree vs. diverge are designed to dissociate them, and projected-out analyses quantify any residual entanglement (which is itself informative). The dual layers might not be genuinely co-present internally \u2014 the model may simply implement a winner-take-all competition (as the competition-of-mechanisms work suggests) collapsing to one belief; a robust negative result (no separable factual layer during counterfactual processing) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting LLM processing with human dual-meaning comprehension and sharpening the competition-of-mechanisms picture. The layers could be decoded via surface mood cues ('had'/'would') rather than abstract belief representations; paraphrase/mood perturbations, comprehension checks, and the synthetic track mitigate this. Belief may not be cleanly linearly decodable at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The responsible components may be distributed rather than discrete heads; we treat 'steer to shift layer dominance / restore factual inference' as the causal test rather than pinning exact heads. Steering could degrade fluency or destroy the model's ability to reason within the counterfactual scenario (over-suppressing the supposed layer); we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, consequent-inference accuracy, indicative-frame accuracy, and general benchmarks. Human dual-meaning comparison is structural, not a claim of identical mechanism. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
