Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "defeasibility_flag_belief", "Title": "Rule or Rule-of-Thumb? An Internal Defeasibility Flag That Gates How a Single Counterexample Revises an LLM's Belief", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs internally tag a general belief about a category as either DEFEASIBLE (a default/generic that tolerates exceptions, e.g. 'birds fly') or STRICT (an exception-intolerant universal, e.g. 'all bachelors are unmarried'). This defeasibility flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates counterexample-driven revision: presenting a single exception should collapse belief in a strict universal but only mildly dent belief in a defeasible generic; steering the flag reverses this asymmetry, and mis-flagging predicts the two canonical human/LLM errors (misreading generics as universals -> overgeneralization; and unstable beliefs that overreact to one exception).", "Related Work": "Behavioral work robustly shows LLMs mishandle generics and defeasible reasoning: they conflate generics with universals and overgeneralize (Penguins Don't Fly 2022; Exceptions, Instantiations, and Overgeneralization 2024), fail to maintain stable beliefs when exceptions or irrelevant facts are added (Are LLMs classical or nonmonotonic reasoners? 2024), and vary widely on 20 defeasible patterns with CoT often hurting (Generics and Default Reasoning in LLMs 2025); MLMs are insensitive to quantification yet encode a generic-form meaning (Interpretation of Generalization in MLMs 2023). Classic interpretability isolates a linear truth-value direction (Geometry of Truth 2023; Truth-value judgment 2024). CRITICALLY, all defeasibility/generics work is behavioral or about quantifier probabilities; none isolate an internal DEFEASIBILITY flag (defeasible-default vs. strict-universal) as a decodable variable distinct from truth-value, nor test whether it causally gates how a single counterexample revises the belief. This differs from my prior essentialist_belief_mutability proposal, which tags a specific instance's property as essential vs. accidental; here the axis is the LOGICAL FORM of a general belief (exception-tolerant generic vs. exception-intolerant universal) and its role in counterexample-driven, non-monotonic revision. We transfer a canonical non-monotonic-reasoning phenomenon into LLM belief internals with a mechanistic, causal treatment.", "Abstract": "'Birds fly' and 'all squares have four sides' look grammatically similar but behave differently under a counterexample: a penguin leaves the first belief essentially intact, while a single three-sided 'square' would destroy the second. Humans track this difference (defeasible generics vs. strict universals), and LLMs are notorious for confusing them - overgeneralizing generics into universals, or letting one exception (or even an irrelevant fact) destabilize a belief. We ask a mechanistic question: does an LLM internally represent whether a general belief is defeasible or strict, and does that representation govern how a counterexample revises it? We hypothesize that a 'defeasibility' flag is (i) linearly decodable from residual-stream activations, (ii) largely orthogonal to the truth-value direction, and (iii) causally gates counterexample-driven revision - so that beliefs internally tagged strict collapse under one exception while those tagged defeasible barely move, and steering the flag reverses this asymmetry. We build a controlled dataset of general claims labeled defeasible (generics) vs. strict (analytic/definitional universals), matched in surface form, plus a synthetic-category track that stipulates a rule's logical form in-context to control priors, each paired with a single counterexample. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3, Qwen2.5, Gemma-2, Mistral) we train linear probes to decode defeasibility vs. truth, measure their geometry, quantify a per-item revision-plasticity gap under a counterexample, test whether internal mis-flagging predicts overgeneralization and instability errors, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, counterexample-revision gap, error-prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering plasticity reversal, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k general claims labeled by LOGICAL FORM: (a) DEFEASIBLE generics that tolerate exceptions ('Birds fly', 'Dogs have four legs', 'Metals conduct electricity'), (b) STRICT universals that do not ('All squares have four sides', 'All bachelors are unmarried', 'Every prime greater than 2 is odd'). Balance across domains (biology, artifacts, math/definitions, social) and MATCH surface form where possible (bare-plural generics vs. explicitly-quantified universals, and paraphrases that swap 'birds fly' <-> 'all birds fly' to test form vs. cue). Two tracks: (i) REAL claims; (ii) a SYNTHETIC novel-category track that stipulates a rule's form in-context ('Every zorb is by definition three-eyed [strict]' vs. 'Zorbs typically have three eyes [defeasible]'), controlling pretraining priors and dissociating form from lexical triggers. For each claim, construct a matched SINGLE-COUNTEREXAMPLE ('You encounter a bird that cannot fly' / 'You encounter a square with three sides'), plus an IRRELEVANT-FACT control ('Lions have manes') and a SUPPORTING-INSTANCE control ('Owls fly'). Add a behavioral REVISION probe (does the model retract/weaken the general claim after the counterexample?) and comprehension checks (can it restate the claim's form); apply paraphrase/format perturbations. Human-validate defeasible/strict labels on a sample. 2) Probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the claim tokens across layers. Train logistic-regression probes for (i) TRUTH-VALUE (true vs. false general claims) and (ii) DEFEASIBILITY (defeasible-generic vs. strict-universal), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the defeasibility and truth-value directions; test whether defeasibility remains decodable after projecting out truth (and vice versa) to establish orthogonality, and whether the direction generalizes across real and synthetic tracks and across surface-matched paraphrases. 4) Counterexample-revision gap (key test): Present each true general claim, then its single counterexample, and measure (a) internal belief change via the truth probe and (b) behavioral retraction. Define per-item plasticity. Prediction: STRICT universals show large belief collapse under one counterexample (correct) while DEFEASIBLE generics show only mild denting (correct); mis-behavior appears as generics collapsing like universals (instability) or universals resisting (over-rigidity). Also test the IRRELEVANT-FACT and SUPPORTING-INSTANCE controls: a well-behaved model should not revise a generic given either, reproducing/dissociating the human 'unstable belief' finding. 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal defeasibility readout (read before the counterexample) predicts per-item overgeneralization errors (treating a generic as universal in a downstream 'does it hold for ALL?' probe) and instability errors (revising a generic given irrelevant/supporting info), via AUROC beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond surface lexical features. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers carrying the defeasibility signal and gating counterexample revision (patch defeasible-claim activations into strict-claim runs and vice versa at the counterexample point; find the minimal component that flips revision behavior); compare to truth/belief heads. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'defeasibility' steering direction (defeasible vs. strict activations, controlling for truth). Test (a) steering strict claims toward 'defeasible' makes them resist the counterexample (over-tolerant); (b) steering generics toward 'strict' makes one counterexample collapse them (over-brittle); (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively move revision, and the model's factual belief is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), non-manipulated accuracy, and perplexity. Test whether steering toward the CORRECT form improves calibrated revision. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across domains, real vs. synthetic tracks, and quantifier-explicit vs. bare-generic phrasings. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability vs. truth, counterexample-revision plasticity gap (strict vs. defeasible; internal and behavioral), overgeneralization/instability error-prediction AUROC (vs. log-prob and lexical baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering plasticity reversal and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Revision behavior could be driven by the claim's pretraining confidence rather than a distinct defeasibility flag; the synthetic novel-category track (form stipulated in-context, decoupled from prior strength), surface-matched paraphrases, and conditioning error-prediction on a confidence baseline are designed to dissociate defeasibility from raw confidence. The flag may be entangled with surface cues (bare plural vs. 'all'/'every', 'by definition' vs. 'typically'); we mitigate with paraphrase perturbations, comprehension checks, and the synthetic track where cues are balanced. Defeasibility and truth directions may be less orthogonal than hypothesized, in which case the finding characterizes their entanglement (still informative). The defeasible/strict label is partly graded/subjective (some generics are near-universal); we human-validate and treat borderline items separately. The effect may be absent internally - a robust negative result (LLMs' behavioral generic/universal confusion is NOT backed by an internal defeasibility representation gating revision) is itself a valuable, publishable finding constraining the generics-in-LLMs literature. Probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The gating component may be distributed; we treat 'steer to reverse/calibrate plasticity' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Behavioral retraction/overgeneralization scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately rigid or labile; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, non-manipulated accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
