Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "transitive_belief_leakage", "Title": "Does Belief Survive the Chain? Probing Whether LLMs Propagate Uncertainty Across Inferential Steps or Leak Certainty Into Their Conclusions", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM internally derives a conclusion C from a chain of individually-uncertain premises (A\u2248p1, A\u2192B\u2248p2, B\u2192C\u2248p3), a rational agent's belief in C should be BOUNDED by (and roughly the product of) the link beliefs, degrading as the chain lengthens. We hypothesize LLMs instead exhibit 'certainty leakage': the internally-decoded belief in the conclusion is systematically HIGHER than the product of the internally-decoded link beliefs and decays far more slowly than probability theory dictates as chain length grows \u2014 an internal over-propagation of certainty that is decodable, predicts long-chain overconfidence errors, and is carried by an identifiable component that can be steered to restore proper uncertainty attenuation.", "Related Work": "Prior work shows LLM internal beliefs skew CoT via confirmation bias (Unveiling Confirmation Bias in CoT 2025), that internal consistency across layers signals reasoning reliability (Calibrating Reasoning with Internal Consistency 2024), that multi-hop reasoning activates a distribution of intermediate answers in middle layers (Distributional Reasoning in LLMs 2024), and that belief/truth directions are linearly decodable and context-sensitive causal mediators of in-context inference (Truth-value judgment 2024; Language Models with Rationality/REFLEX 2023 for belief-graph consistency). Behavioral work on incoherent probability judgments and the conjunction fallacy (my prior internal_belief_coherence_gap) concerns STATIC conjunctions P(A\u2227B) at a single query, not inferential chains. Belief-manifold work (Shape of Beliefs 2026) shows posteriors' mean/variance are encoded but studies single-distribution updating, not multi-step deductive propagation. CRITICALLY, no prior work asks whether an LLM's INTERNAL belief in a DERIVED conclusion respects the multiplicative attenuation of uncertainty across a chain of uncertain premises, whether certainty LEAKS (conclusion believed more than the weakest/product of links warrants), how this scales with CHAIN LENGTH, or whether an identifiable component can be steered to restore attenuation. We reframe long-chain reasoning overconfidence as an internal uncertainty-propagation failure and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from static-coherence, confirmation-bias, and layerwise-consistency work.", "Abstract": "A rational reasoner who is only 80% sure of each of five inferential steps should be far less than 80% sure of the final conclusion \u2014 uncertainty compounds along a chain. Yet LLMs are notorious for stating long derivations with unwarranted confidence. Prior interpretability work shows internal beliefs are linearly decodable and shape chain-of-thought, but none ask whether the model's INTERNAL belief in a derived conclusion respects the multiplicative attenuation of uncertainty across the chain that produced it. We hypothesize 'certainty leakage': the internally-decoded belief in a conclusion C is systematically higher than the product of the internally-decoded beliefs in its constituent links, and decays far more slowly than probability theory dictates as chain length grows. We further hypothesize that (i) both link-beliefs and conclusion-belief are linearly decodable, (ii) the leakage magnitude grows with chain length and predicts long-chain reasoning errors, and (iii) it is carried by an identifiable component such that steering it restores proper attenuation, improving calibration on long chains without harming short ones. We build controlled inference-chain items where each link's belief strength is stipulated (via explicit likelihood-ratio-style premises and a synthetic-entity track to control priors) and chain length is varied from 1 to 6. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read each link-belief and the conclusion-belief, compute a per-item leakage score against the product baseline, trace its length-scaling, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, leakage magnitude vs. product baseline, length-decay slope vs. rational, leakage\u2013error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering attenuation restoration and calibration gains, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~5k inferential-chain items where a conclusion C follows from a chain of links (A; A\u2192B; B\u2192C; ...) of controllable length L in {1,2,3,4,5,6}. Each link is assigned an explicit belief strength (e.g., 'usually/probably/possibly' calibrated to stipulated probabilities, or explicit '80% of the time' framings) so a rational product-of-links belief in C is computable. Two tracks: (a) REAL commonsense/factual chains; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (invented entities/relations, e.g., 'Most zorbs are vantar; most vantar glow; so this zorb glows') with fully stipulated link probabilities to control pretraining priors and give an exact rational conclusion belief. Include matched conditions varying only chain length while holding the weakest-link strength constant, plus a CERTAIN-CHAIN control (all links = ~1.0, where the conclusion SHOULD stay high) to dissociate leakage from generic length/context decay. Add a behavioral CONCLUSION-CONFIDENCE probe ('How confident are you that C? Rate 0-100'), comprehension checks (can the model restate each link's strength), and paraphrase/order perturbations. 2) Belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at each LINK proposition's tokens and at the CONCLUSION proposition's tokens across layers. Train a linear/graded BELIEF-STRENGTH probe (regressing a calibrated belief score, anchored on single-premise items with known stipulated probabilities and shuffled-label control tasks); report per-layer accuracy/R^2. Read the internal belief for each link and for the conclusion WITHOUT asking the confidence question. 3) Leakage measurement (key test): Define per-item leakage = internal conclusion-belief minus the product (and minus the min, the weakest-link) of internal link-beliefs. Test leakage > 0 (conclusion over-believed relative to the product/weakest link) across models and tracks versus the rational-attenuation null; confirm the CERTAIN-CHAIN control shows no spurious decay so that any leakage in uncertain chains is not a length artifact. 4) Length scaling: Fit conclusion-belief vs. chain length L for uncertain chains and compare the internal decay slope to the rational product decay slope; the leakage prediction is a much flatter internal decay (certainty over-propagated) than probability theory dictates. Compare verbal-output decay to internal decay. 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral long-chain errors (the model asserts a conclusion that does not actually follow with the stated confidence, or errs on the synthetic ground-truth). Test whether internal leakage (read before the query) predicts per-item long-chain overconfidence errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond chain length itself. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the conclusion-belief and inflate it above the product of links (patch short-chain vs. long-chain conclusion activations, and patch weak-link activations into the conclusion representation; find the minimal component that lowers/raises conclusion-belief toward the product); compare to belief/truth heads (Truth-value judgment) and the middle-layer intermediate-answer distribution (Distributional Reasoning). 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'certainty-leakage' steering direction (leaky vs. properly-attenuated conclusion activations). Test (a) subtracting it lowers conclusion-belief toward the product-of-links / rational value on uncertain chains and improves calibration/accuracy on long chains; (b) adding it exaggerates over-propagation; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively restore attenuation, and CERTAIN-CHAIN conclusions (which should stay high) are preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), short-chain accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across real vs. synthetic tracks, chain lengths, and link-strength distributions. Metrics: probe accuracy/R^2, leakage magnitude vs. product and weakest-link baselines, internal-vs-rational length-decay slopes, internal-vs-verbal gap, leakage\u2013error AUROC (vs. log-prob and chain-length baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering attenuation restoration, calibration (ECE) and long-chain accuracy deltas, certain-chain specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The graded belief-strength probe is harder to calibrate than a binary truth probe; miscalibration could bias the leakage estimate. We mitigate by anchoring on single-premise items with known stipulated probabilities, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on the RELATIVE comparison between conclusion-belief and the product/weakest-link of internally-decoded link-beliefs (a within-representation contrast that does not require absolute calibration). Apparent leakage could reflect the model using additional valid evidence for C beyond the stated chain (real track); the SYNTHETIC novel-entity track with fully stipulated link probabilities isolates chain-only propagation. Slow decay with length could be a generic context-retention artifact rather than certainty over-propagation; the CERTAIN-CHAIN control (which should stay high) and the length-matched weakest-link-constant condition dissociate genuine leakage from length effects. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs internally attenuate uncertainty appropriately, and overconfidence is only a verbalization artifact) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding. Reading belief at 'link' vs. 'conclusion' tokens is imperfect since positions differ; we anchor probes at matched proposition tokens and use paraphrase perturbations. The leakage component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore attenuation' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately under-confident on valid chains; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, short-chain and certain-chain accuracy, and general benchmarks. Long-chain error scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'transitive_belief_leakage', 'Title': 'Does Belief Survive the Chain? Probing Whether LLMs Propagate Uncertainty Across Inferential Steps or Leak Certainty Into Their Conclusions', 'Short Hypothesis': "When an LLM internally derives a conclusion C from a chain of individually-uncertain premises (A≈p1, A→B≈p2, B→C≈p3), a rational agent's belief in C should be BOUNDED by (and roughly the product of) the link beliefs, degrading as the chain lengthens. We hypothesize LLMs instead exhibit 'certainty leakage': the internally-decoded belief in the conclusion is systematically HIGHER than the product of the internally-decoded link beliefs and decays far more slowly than probability theory dictates as chain length grows — an internal over-propagation of certainty that is decodable, predicts long-chain overconfidence errors, and is carried by an identifiable component that can be steered to restore proper uncertainty attenuation.", 'Related Work': "Prior work shows LLM internal beliefs skew CoT via confirmation bias (Unveiling Confirmation Bias in CoT 2025), that internal consistency across layers signals reasoning reliability (Calibrating Reasoning with Internal Consistency 2024), that multi-hop reasoning activates a distribution of intermediate answers in middle layers (Distributional Reasoning in LLMs 2024), and that belief/truth directions are linearly decodable and context-sensitive causal mediators of in-context inference (Truth-value judgment 2024; Language Models with Rationality/REFLEX 2023 for belief-graph consistency). Behavioral work on incoherent probability judgments and the conjunction fallacy (my prior internal_belief_coherence_gap) concerns STATIC conjunctions P(A∧B) at a single query, not inferential chains. Belief-manifold work (Shape of Beliefs 2026) shows posteriors' mean/variance are encoded but studies single-distribution updating, not multi-step deductive propagation. CRITICALLY, no prior work asks whether an LLM's INTERNAL belief in a DERIVED conclusion respects the multiplicative attenuation of uncertainty across a chain of uncertain premises, whether certainty LEAKS (conclusion believed more than the weakest/product of links warrants), how this scales with CHAIN LENGTH, or whether an identifiable component can be steered to restore attenuation. We reframe long-chain reasoning overconfidence as an internal uncertainty-propagation failure and give it a mechanistic, causal treatment, distinct from static-coherence, confirmation-bias, and layerwise-consistency work.", 'Abstract': "A rational reasoner who is only 80% sure of each of five inferential steps should be far less than 80% sure of the final conclusion — uncertainty compounds along a chain. Yet LLMs are notorious for stating long derivations with unwarranted confidence. Prior interpretability work shows internal beliefs are linearly decodable and shape chain-of-thought, but none ask whether the model's INTERNAL belief in a derived conclusion respects the multiplicative attenuation of uncertainty across the chain that produced it. We hypothesize 'certainty leakage': the internally-decoded belief in a conclusion C is systematically higher than the product of the internally-decoded beliefs in its constituent links, and decays far more slowly than probability theory dictates as chain length grows. We further hypothesize that (i) both link-beliefs and conclusion-belief are linearly decodable, (ii) the leakage magnitude grows with chain length and predicts long-chain reasoning errors, and (iii) it is carried by an identifiable component such that steering it restores proper attenuation, improving calibration on long chains without harming short ones. We build controlled inference-chain items where each link's belief strength is stipulated (via explicit likelihood-ratio-style premises and a synthetic-entity track to control priors) and chain length is varied from 1 to 6. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes to read each link-belief and the conclusion-belief, compute a per-item leakage score against the product baseline, trace its length-scaling, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal control by steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, leakage magnitude vs. product baseline, length-decay slope vs. rational, leakage–error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering attenuation restoration and calibration gains, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~5k inferential-chain items where a conclusion C follows from a chain of links (A; A→B; B→C; ...) of controllable length L in {1,2,3,4,5,6}. Each link is assigned an explicit belief strength (e.g., 'usually/probably/possibly' calibrated to stipulated probabilities, or explicit '80% of the time' framings) so a rational product-of-links belief in C is computable. Two tracks: (a) REAL commonsense/factual chains; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (invented entities/relations, e.g., 'Most zorbs are vantar; most vantar glow; so this zorb glows') with fully stipulated link probabilities to control pretraining priors and give an exact rational conclusion belief. Include matched conditions varying only chain length while holding the weakest-link strength constant, plus a CERTAIN-CHAIN control (all links = ~1.0, where the conclusion SHOULD stay high) to dissociate leakage from generic length/context decay. Add a behavioral CONCLUSION-CONFIDENCE probe ('How confident are you that C? Rate 0-100'), comprehension checks (can the model restate each link's strength), and paraphrase/order perturbations. 2) Belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at each LINK proposition's tokens and at the CONCLUSION proposition's tokens across layers. Train a linear/graded BELIEF-STRENGTH probe (regressing a calibrated belief score, anchored on single-premise items with known stipulated probabilities and shuffled-label control tasks); report per-layer accuracy/R^2. Read the internal belief for each link and for the conclusion WITHOUT asking the confidence question. 3) Leakage measurement (key test): Define per-item leakage = internal conclusion-belief minus the product (and minus the min, the weakest-link) of internal link-beliefs. Test leakage > 0 (conclusion over-believed relative to the product/weakest link) across models and tracks versus the rational-attenuation null; confirm the CERTAIN-CHAIN control shows no spurious decay so that any leakage in uncertain chains is not a length artifact. 4) Length scaling: Fit conclusion-belief vs. chain length L for uncertain chains and compare the internal decay slope to the rational product decay slope; the leakage prediction is a much flatter internal decay (certainty over-propagated) than probability theory dictates. Compare verbal-output decay to internal decay. 5) Predictiveness: Collect behavioral long-chain errors (the model asserts a conclusion that does not actually follow with the stated confidence, or errs on the synthetic ground-truth). Test whether internal leakage (read before the query) predicts per-item long-chain overconfidence errors via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond chain length itself. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that carry the conclusion-belief and inflate it above the product of links (patch short-chain vs. long-chain conclusion activations, and patch weak-link activations into the conclusion representation; find the minimal component that lowers/raises conclusion-belief toward the product); compare to belief/truth heads (Truth-value judgment) and the middle-layer intermediate-answer distribution (Distributional Reasoning). 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'certainty-leakage' steering direction (leaky vs. properly-attenuated conclusion activations). Test (a) subtracting it lowers conclusion-belief toward the product-of-links / rational value on uncertain chains and improves calibration/accuracy on long chains; (b) adding it exaggerates over-propagation; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively restore attenuation, and CERTAIN-CHAIN conclusions (which should stay high) are preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), short-chain accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across real vs. synthetic tracks, chain lengths, and link-strength distributions. Metrics: probe accuracy/R^2, leakage magnitude vs. product and weakest-link baselines, internal-vs-rational length-decay slopes, internal-vs-verbal gap, leakage–error AUROC (vs. log-prob and chain-length baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering attenuation restoration, calibration (ECE) and long-chain accuracy deltas, certain-chain specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The graded belief-strength probe is harder to calibrate than a binary truth probe; miscalibration could bias the leakage estimate. We mitigate by anchoring on single-premise items with known stipulated probabilities, reporting layerwise results with control tasks, and focusing on the RELATIVE comparison between conclusion-belief and the product/weakest-link of internally-decoded link-beliefs (a within-representation contrast that does not require absolute calibration). Apparent leakage could reflect the model using additional valid evidence for C beyond the stated chain (real track); the SYNTHETIC novel-entity track with fully stipulated link probabilities isolates chain-only propagation. Slow decay with length could be a generic context-retention artifact rather than certainty over-propagation; the CERTAIN-CHAIN control (which should stay high) and the length-matched weakest-link-constant condition dissociate genuine leakage from length effects. The effect may be absent internally — a robust negative result (LLMs internally attenuate uncertainty appropriately, and overconfidence is only a verbalization artifact) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding. Reading belief at 'link' vs. 'conclusion' tokens is imperfect since positions differ; we anchor probes at matched proposition tokens and use paraphrase perturbations. The leakage component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to restore attenuation' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately under-confident on valid chains; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, short-chain and certain-chain accuracy, and general benchmarks. Long-chain error scoring on open-ended output needs an LLM-judge plus human validation on a sample. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

