istral-7B) we train linear probes to decode recognition, truth, and knowledge, measure their geometry, quantify a per-claim recognition-to-truth leak, test its predictiveness for hallucination, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal steering. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle, projected-out decodability, leak magnitude, leak–hallucination AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering decoupling, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k subject-relation-attribute claims crossing three factors. (A) ENTITY FAMILIARITY: highly-recognized/popular entities (frequent in pretraining), low-familiarity/rare real entities, and fully SYNTHETIC novel entities (never seen) to anchor the unrecognized end. (B) CLAIM VERACITY: true vs. false attribute claims. (C) KNOWLEDGE status: whether the model actually knows the correct attribute (measured empirically by closed-book accuracy). Crucially include the diagnostic RECOGNIZED-BUT-UNKNOWN cell (familiar entity, but the specific fact is not reliably recalled) versus RECOGNIZED-AND-KNOWN and UNRECOGNIZED cells. Use subject-popularity/frequency estimates to set familiarity, and match claim surface form across familiarity levels (paraphrase/format perturbations) to guard against lexical confounds. Add a behavioral TRUTH-JUDGMENT probe ('Is this claim true? + confidence') and comprehension checks. Human-validate a sample. 2) Three-channel probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the entity/attribute tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the truth question. Following prior entity-recognition and truth-probe methods, train linear probes for (R) RECOGNITION/FAMILIARITY (recognized vs. unrecognized entity), (T) TRUTH-VALUE (true vs. false claim), and (K) KNOWLEDGE (will the model correctly recall this fact), with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Geometry: Compute cosine/subspace angle between the recognition, truth, and knowledge directions; test whether truth remains decodable after projecting out recognition (and vice versa), and whether recognition is distinct from the genuine-knowledge direction, to characterize the leak's source. 4) Recognition-to-truth leak (key test): Define per-claim leak = shift of the internal truth readout toward 'true' as a function of entity recognition, holding knowledge and veracity constant (comparing recognized-but-unknown vs. unrecognized cells for matched FALSE claims). Test leak > 0 across models versus a recognition-independent null; confirm the strongest leak in the RECOGNIZED-BUT-UNKNOWN cell (familiarity without diagnostic knowledge), mirroring the human cue-familiarity FOK. Replicate the human finding that recognition's effect on internal truth exceeds a matched in-context-repetition manipulation. Compare internal leak to stated truth judgments (behavioral recognition-based truth effect). 5) Predictiveness: Test whether the internal leak (read before any query) predicts familiarity-driven associated hallucination (confidently asserting a false attribute about a recognized entity) via AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond the knowledge probe. 6) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation to find heads/layers that transport the recognition signal into the truth channel (patch recognized-entity activations into unrecognized-entity runs and vice versa; find the minimal component that raises/lowers the truth readout); compare to entity-recognition SAE directions and truth/belief heads from prior work. 7) Causal control: Derive a contrastive 'recognition-to-truth leak' steering direction (recognized vs. unrecognized activations, controlling for knowledge). Test (a) subtracting it decouples recognition from truth — lowering unwarranted belief in false claims about recognized entities and reducing associated hallucination; (b) amplifying it exaggerates the leak; (c) specificity: random-direction and truth-direction steering do not selectively decouple, and genuine-knowledge recall is preserved; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), known-fact accuracy, and perplexity. 8) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across entity types (people, places, works, organizations), familiarity levels, and real vs. synthetic entities. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, subspace angle and projected-out decodability, recognition-to-truth leak magnitude and sign, recognition-vs-repetition comparison, internal-vs-verbal gap, leak–hallucination AUROC (vs. log-prob and knowledge baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering decoupling and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The leak could reflect legitimate inference (claims about popular entities are, on average, more likely to be true in training data) rather than a spurious recognition-to-truth contamination; the FALSE-claim cells (where recognition and truth diverge), the matched RECOGNIZED-BUT-UNKNOWN vs. UNRECOGNIZED comparison at held-constant veracity/knowledge, and the synthetic-entity anchor are designed to isolate genuine leakage from valid frequency-based inference (though full separation is difficult). The recognition and knowledge channels may be hard to separate (recognition often co-occurs with knowledge); the recognized-but-unknown cell and projected-out analyses are built to dissociate them, and even a characterization of entanglement is informative and directly engages the 'recall-process-not-truth' finding. The effect may be absent internally — a robust negative result (recognition does not contaminate the truth channel) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with the human recognition-based truth effect. Familiarity estimated from subject popularity is noisy; we use multiple frequency proxies and the synthetic anchor. The truth/recognition probes may not be cleanly linear at every layer; we report layerwise results with control tasks. The leak component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'steer to decouple recognition from truth' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact head. Steering could degrade fluency or make the model distrust all familiar content; we use small magnitudes, random/truth-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, known-fact accuracy, and general benchmarks. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

