Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "belief_dilution_effect", "Title": "Watered Down: Does Irrelevant Information Literally Dilute an LLM's Internal Belief Signal?", "Short Hypothesis": "Mirroring the human dilution effect (non-diagnostic information weakens the pull of diagnostic evidence), adding irrelevant, non-diagnostic facts to a context weakens an LLM's internally-decoded belief in a conclusion that its diagnostic evidence supports \u2014 the belief regresses toward neutral. We further hypothesize this internal dilution is realized geometrically as a REDUCTION IN MAGNITUDE of the belief signal along the truth direction (a literal 'radial dilution'), not merely an angular rotation, and that it predicts diluted behavioral judgments and is causally reversible by rescaling the belief component.", "Related Work": "The dilution effect \u2014 non-diagnostic information weakening the implications of diagnostic information, yielding less extreme judgments \u2014 is a canonical, heavily-cited human finding (Tversky & Kleinman 1981, 611 citations; The Dilution Effect and Perceptual Decision Making 2015; The Moral Dilution Effect 2025; Restoration of Diluted Judgment 2008). A long-standing open debate is whether dilution is genuine dilution of the diagnostic signal or an inference of missing evidence. In LLM interpretability, belief/truth is a linearly-decodable, context-sensitive causal mediator that is even sensitive to IRRELEVANT context (Truth-value judgment: belief directions are context sensitive 2024), belief posteriors form curved manifolds whose mean/variance shift with evidence (The Shape of Beliefs 2026), and 'Simulated Adoption' (2026) provides the crucial geometric framing for in-context conflict as either RADIAL magnitude dilution or ANGULAR orthogonal rotation of the truth direction. CRITICALLY, no work isolates the classic dilution effect as an internal belief phenomenon: whether appending non-diagnostic facts weakens the internal belief in a diagnostically-supported conclusion, whether this is realized as radial magnitude reduction vs. angular rotation, whether it reflects genuine dilution vs. missing-evidence inference, or whether it can be causally reversed by rescaling the belief signal. This transfers a canonical human bias into LLM belief internals and, by importing the radial-vs-angular decomposition from Simulated Adoption, gives it a novel mechanistic, causal treatment distinct from all my prior belief-attribute proposals (which concern repetition, source independence, anchoring, halo, etc., never non-diagnostic dilution).", "Abstract": "Told that a student scored in the top 5% of a hard exam, people confidently predict she is excellent; add that she owns a cat and jogs on weekends \u2014 utterly irrelevant \u2014 and their confidence drops. This dilution effect, one of the most reproduced biases in judgment research, has never been examined inside LLMs at the level of their beliefs. As LLMs increasingly reason over long, cluttered contexts (RAG passages padded with irrelevant text, verbose user messages), we ask a sharp mechanistic question: does appending non-diagnostic information weaken the model's internally-decoded belief in a conclusion its diagnostic evidence supports, and if so, how is that weakening realized geometrically? We hypothesize that (i) adding irrelevant facts regresses the internal belief readout toward neutral \u2014 an internal dilution effect; (ii) this is realized as a reduction in the MAGNITUDE of the belief signal along the truth direction (radial dilution) rather than an angular rotation of it; (iii) the internal dilution predicts diluted behavioral judgments and grows with the amount of irrelevant material; and (iv) it is causally reversible by rescaling the belief component along the truth direction, restoring undiluted judgments. We build a controlled dataset of diagnostic-evidence items paired with matched non-diagnostic padding (and length-matched diagnostic and neutral-token controls), with a synthetic-entity track to control priors. On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) we train linear belief probes, decompose the belief signal into radial and angular components, quantify per-item dilution, test the missing-evidence-inference alternative, localize the responsible heads via activation patching, and test causal rescaling. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, dilution magnitude, radial-vs-angular decomposition, dose-response, dilution-error AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-rescaling restoration, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Generate ~6k judgment items each with a DIAGNOSTIC evidence statement that strongly supports a conclusion C about an item (e.g., 'Alex scored in the top 2% on a rigorous certification exam' -> 'Alex is highly competent'; entity-attribute inferences). Two tracks: (a) REAL items; (b) a SYNTHETIC novel-entity track (invented people/objects/attributes) to control pretraining priors and stipulate diagnosticity. For each item, construct matched conditions holding the diagnostic evidence constant: (i) DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY baseline; (ii) DILUTED \u2014 the diagnostic evidence PLUS k non-diagnostic facts about the same item (irrelevant hobbies, unrelated demographics), at graded amounts k in {1,2,4,8}; controls: (iii) LENGTH-MATCHED NEUTRAL padding (topically-unrelated filler not about the item, controls for pure context length); (iv) EXTRA-DIAGNOSTIC (adding a second genuinely relevant fact, which should NOT dilute and may strengthen); (v) a MISSING-EVIDENCE-CUE variant where the non-diagnostic facts are framed as an exhaustive dossier ('here is everything known about Alex') vs. an incidental aside, to test the inference-of-missing-evidence account (dilution should be larger when the padding implies the diagnostic fact is all-there-is). Add a behavioral JUDGMENT probe ('How confident are you that C? 0-100'), comprehension checks (can the model identify which facts are relevant), and paraphrase/order perturbations. Human-validate diagnosticity/non-diagnosticity labels on a sample. 2) Belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the conclusion's key tokens across layers, WITHOUT asking the judgment question. Train a linear BELIEF probe (P(C holds), graded) with shuffled-label control tasks; report per-layer accuracy/AUROC. 3) Dilution measurement (key test): Define per-item dilution = belief(DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY) - belief(DILUTED), i.e. regression of the belief readout toward neutral. Test dilution > 0 across models, domains, and tracks versus the rational null (irrelevant facts should not change belief); confirm the LENGTH-MATCHED NEUTRAL control does NOT produce the same regression (so dilution is driven by non-diagnostic facts ABOUT THE ITEM, not context length), and that EXTRA-DIAGNOSTIC does not dilute. Fit a dose-response of dilution vs. k. Compare internal dilution to behavioral judgment dilution (verbal dilution effect). 4) Radial-vs-angular geometry (mechanistic key test): Following the Simulated Adoption decomposition, project the conclusion representation onto the truth/belief direction and decompose the change under dilution into a RADIAL component (magnitude reduction along the truth direction) and an ANGULAR component (rotation away from the truth direction). Test whether dilution is dominated by radial magnitude loss (literal 'watering down') vs. angular displacement, per model and layer. 5) Missing-evidence-inference test: Compare dilution in the exhaustive-dossier vs. incidental-aside framings (condition v). A larger dilution under the exhaustive framing supports the inference-of-missing-evidence account; equal dilution supports genuine signal dilution \u2014 adjudicating the long-standing human-literature debate inside the model. 6) Predictiveness: Test whether internal dilution (read before the query) predicts per-item behavioral judgment regression via correlation/AUROC, beyond a log-prob baseline and beyond context length. 7) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from DILUTED to DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY runs to find heads/layers that carry the diluted (attenuated) belief signal; contrast with truth/belief heads and with heads that attend to the non-diagnostic tokens. 8) Causal control: Derive the truth direction (difference-in-means) and test a geometry-aware RESCALING intervention \u2014 amplify the radial belief component along the truth direction under dilution. Test (a) rescaling restores undiluted belief and behavioral judgments; (b) suppressing it exaggerates dilution; (c) specificity: random-direction and pure-angular steering do not selectively restore the belief; (d) collateral checks on general benchmarks (MMLU subset), diagnostic-only accuracy, and perplexity. 9) Generalization: Test probe/rescaling transfer across domains (competence judgments, entity attributes, prediction tasks), real vs. synthetic tracks, and dilution amounts. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, dilution magnitude and dose-response, radial-vs-angular decomposition shares, length-control and extra-diagnostic specificity, exhaustive-vs-incidental framing contrast, dilution-error AUROC (vs. log-prob and length baselines), patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-rescaling restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The regression toward neutral could reflect generic context-length effects or attention diffusion rather than a specific non-diagnostic-dilution phenomenon; the LENGTH-MATCHED NEUTRAL control (irrelevant filler NOT about the item), the EXTRA-DIAGNOSTIC control, and the requirement that dilution be driven by item-specific non-diagnostic facts are designed to dissociate genuine dilution from length/diffusion artifacts. The effect may be absent internally \u2014 a robust negative result (LLMs ignore non-diagnostic facts and preserve the belief signal) would itself be a valuable, publishable finding contrasting with the canonical human bias and reassuring for cluttered-context deployment. The genuine-dilution vs. missing-evidence-inference distinction may not fully resolve; the exhaustive-vs-incidental framing test provides evidence but, as in the human literature, may be only partially conclusive \u2014 still an informative contribution to a decades-old debate. The graded belief probe requires calibration; we anchor on diagnostic-only items with known belief and report layerwise results with control tasks, focusing on relative change. The radial-vs-angular decomposition depends on a well-estimated truth direction; we use difference-in-means with cross-validation and report per-layer results, and note architectures may differ (Simulated Adoption found norm dynamics vary by model). Rescaling could degrade fluency or over-amplify belief indiscriminately; we use small magnitudes, random/angular-direction baselines, and monitor perplexity, diagnostic-only accuracy, and general benchmarks. Diagnosticity labels are partly subjective; we human-validate a sample. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
