Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "belief_rigidity_ontogeny", "Title": "Learning to Be Stubborn: The Developmental Emergence of Belief Entrenchment Across Pretraining", "Short Hypothesis": "As a language model pretrains and a fact's parametric belief strengthens, the model's willingness to OVERRIDE that belief with in-context counter-evidence progressively DECAYS: per-fact, the pretraining checkpoint at which a belief becomes linearly decodable predicts the checkpoint at which that belief becomes context-unrevisable. Belief entrenchment (the failure of RAG-style in-context correction) is thus an emergent ontogenetic property that co-develops with, and is causally tied to, parametric belief consolidation rather than being a fixed trait of the final model.", "Related Work": "Pretraining-dynamics work characterizes WHEN factual knowledge is acquired and forgotten (How Do LLMs Acquire Factual Knowledge 2024; How do LMs learn facts 2025 - three-phase plateau; Probing Across Time 2021), that linear representations of relations form at frequency thresholds (On Linear Representations and Pretraining Data Frequency 2025), and that KNOWLEDGE ENTROPY decays over pretraining, impairing new-knowledge integration (Knowledge Entropy Decay 2024). Component-evolution work tracks head/FFN specialization over training (Time Course MechInterp 2025; Crosscoding Through Time 2025). Inference-time work shows final models over-rely on parametric priors and resist in-context correction (context-vs-prior conflict, RAG faithfulness). CRITICALLY, none link the two: no work asks whether, per individual fact, the emergence of a decodable parametric belief CAUSES the loss of in-context revisability (belief entrenchment), nor traces this acquisition-to-rigidity tradeoff across checkpoints, nor tests whether the same mechanism (e.g. knowledge-entropy decay / consolidated recall circuits) that consolidates the belief is what makes it unrevisable. This reframes belief entrenchment as a developmental phenomenon with a per-fact ontogenetic timeline, distinct from acquisition/forgetting dynamics (which ignore in-context override) and from my prior inference-time-only belief proposals.", "Abstract": "Retrieval-augmented and correction-based systems assume a model will update its answer when the context contradicts what it knows. Yet models notoriously cling to their parametric priors. We ask a developmental question that prior pretraining-dynamics work has not: does a fact's belief become HARDER to override with in-context counter-evidence as the model learns it more strongly, and are these two timelines causally linked? We hypothesize that (i) for a given fact, there is a checkpoint at which its parametric belief becomes linearly decodable (acquisition) and a later checkpoint at which the model stops revising that belief when the context asserts the opposite (entrenchment); (ii) per-fact acquisition timing predicts entrenchment timing, so belief rigidity co-develops with consolidation rather than being uniform across the final model; and (iii) the consolidation mechanism (a strengthening parametric-recall circuit and declining reliance on context) causally drives the loss of revisability, so that intervening on it restores in-context updatability. Using open pretraining checkpoints (Pythia suite, OLMo-7B) with known training-data frequencies, we build controlled fact sets, present each fact under a matched in-context contradiction, and at each checkpoint measure (a) parametric belief via a linear probe, (b) in-context revision behavior, and (c) an internal 'context-vs-prior reliance' signal. We trace per-fact acquisition and entrenchment curves, test their predictive coupling against frequency and difficulty baselines, localize the consolidating recall component, and causally test whether attenuating it re-opens in-context revisability. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-fact acquisition/entrenchment checkpoints and their correlation, revision-rate trajectories, coupling AUROC vs. baselines, patching/ablation effect sizes, post-intervention revision restoration, and perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Assemble ~4k subject-relation-object facts spanning a wide range of pretraining-corpus frequencies (using published OLMo/Pythia term-frequency estimates), plus a matched difficulty control set. For each fact, construct (a) a closed-book PARAMETRIC query ('The capital of X is ___'), and (b) an IN-CONTEXT CONTRADICTION prompt asserting the opposite value ('Document: The capital of X is [wrong value]. Question: What is the capital of X?') with matched wording, so a context-faithful model would return the contradicted value. Include a NEUTRAL-context control (context restates the true value or is irrelevant) and comprehension checks (can the model quote the context value). 2) Checkpoint sweep: Use the Pythia suite (multiple sizes: 160M, 1.4B, 6.9B) across its released checkpoints and OLMo-7B intermediate checkpoints. At each checkpoint, for each fact measure: (i) PARAMETRIC BELIEF via a linear probe on residual-stream activations at the object token (trained per-checkpoint with shuffled-label control tasks) and via closed-book log-prob; (ii) IN-CONTEXT REVISION behavior = does the model output the contradicted context value vs. its parametric value; (iii) an internal CONTEXT-vs-PRIOR reliance signal (a linear probe / logit-lens measure of whether the answer token is driven by context tokens vs. the parametric recall pathway, e.g. via attention-to-context and activation-patching-based attribution). 3) Ontogeny curves (key test): Per fact, define the ACQUISITION checkpoint (first checkpoint where parametric belief probe crosses a criterion) and the ENTRENCHMENT checkpoint (first checkpoint where in-context revision rate drops below a criterion, i.e. the model stops accepting the contradiction). Test the coupling hypothesis: acquisition checkpoint predicts entrenchment checkpoint (per-fact correlation), and entrenchment lags acquisition by a characteristic interval. Test whether high-frequency facts entrench earlier. 4) Predictiveness against baselines: Test whether per-fact parametric belief strength at a given checkpoint predicts subsequent loss of in-context revisability (AUROC), beyond corpus frequency and beyond a difficulty baseline (partial regression), isolating the belief-consolidation contribution. 5) Localization: At a mid/late checkpoint, use activation patching / attention attribution to identify the parametric-recall component (heads/FFNs) that carries the entrenched belief and suppresses context reliance; compare across checkpoints to see this component strengthen as entrenchment sets in (linking to Time Course MechInterp / knowledge-entropy findings). 6) Causal intervention: On late (entrenched) checkpoints, test whether attenuating the recall component (ablation / down-scaling its contribution, or steering along a 'context-reliance' direction derived contrastively from early-vs-late or revised-vs-unrevised activations) RESTORES in-context revision on contradicted facts. Report (a) revision-rate recovery, (b) specificity: random-component ablation and neutral-context accuracy preserved, (c) collateral checks on general benchmarks (subset) and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test that acquisition->entrenchment coupling and the intervention transfer across model sizes, relation types, and frequency bands. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-fact acquisition/entrenchment checkpoint distributions and their correlation, revision-rate trajectories over checkpoints, entrenchment-prediction AUROC vs. frequency/difficulty baselines, patching/ablation logit-difference effect sizes, component-strength-over-training curves, post-intervention revision restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Entrenchment could simply track corpus frequency rather than a distinct consolidation-to-rigidity mechanism; the frequency-matched difficulty controls and the partial-regression predictiveness test (belief strength beyond frequency) are designed to isolate the belief-consolidation contribution. The acquisition-then-entrenchment ordering may not hold for all facts, or the two timelines may be inseparable (a fact might be revisable and unlearned, or entrenched from first acquisition) - a robust finding either way (coupled vs. decoupled ontogeny) is informative and publishable. In-context revision behavior depends on prompt scaffolding and could reflect instruction-following rather than belief rigidity; comprehension checks, neutral-context controls, and multiple contradiction phrasings mitigate this, though base (non-instruct) checkpoints may under-follow context, which we treat as part of the developmental signal and compare across contradiction strengths. The linear belief probe may not be cleanly decodable or comparable across checkpoints (representations drift); we retrain probes per-checkpoint with control tasks and corroborate with log-prob measures. The recall component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'attenuate to restore revisability' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact unit. Checkpoint availability limits us to Pythia/OLMo (specific data mixes); results may not transfer to other pretraining regimes or to very large closed models. Restoring context-reliance via intervention could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately gullible to any context; we use small magnitudes, random-component baselines, and monitor perplexity, neutral-context accuracy, and general benchmarks."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'belief_rigidity_ontogeny', 'Title': 'Learning to Be Stubborn: The Developmental Emergence of Belief Entrenchment Across Pretraining', 'Short Hypothesis': "As a language model pretrains and a fact's parametric belief strengthens, the model's willingness to OVERRIDE that belief with in-context counter-evidence progressively DECAYS: per-fact, the pretraining checkpoint at which a belief becomes linearly decodable predicts the checkpoint at which that belief becomes context-unrevisable. Belief entrenchment (the failure of RAG-style in-context correction) is thus an emergent ontogenetic property that co-develops with, and is causally tied to, parametric belief consolidation rather than being a fixed trait of the final model.", 'Related Work': 'Pretraining-dynamics work characterizes WHEN factual knowledge is acquired and forgotten (How Do LLMs Acquire Factual Knowledge 2024; How do LMs learn facts 2025 - three-phase plateau; Probing Across Time 2021), that linear representations of relations form at frequency thresholds (On Linear Representations and Pretraining Data Frequency 2025), and that KNOWLEDGE ENTROPY decays over pretraining, impairing new-knowledge integration (Knowledge Entropy Decay 2024). Component-evolution work tracks head/FFN specialization over training (Time Course MechInterp 2025; Crosscoding Through Time 2025). Inference-time work shows final models over-rely on parametric priors and resist in-context correction (context-vs-prior conflict, RAG faithfulness). CRITICALLY, none link the two: no work asks whether, per individual fact, the emergence of a decodable parametric belief CAUSES the loss of in-context revisability (belief entrenchment), nor traces this acquisition-to-rigidity tradeoff across checkpoints, nor tests whether the same mechanism (e.g. knowledge-entropy decay / consolidated recall circuits) that consolidates the belief is what makes it unrevisable. This reframes belief entrenchment as a developmental phenomenon with a per-fact ontogenetic timeline, distinct from acquisition/forgetting dynamics (which ignore in-context override) and from my prior inference-time-only belief proposals.', 'Abstract': "Retrieval-augmented and correction-based systems assume a model will update its answer when the context contradicts what it knows. Yet models notoriously cling to their parametric priors. We ask a developmental question that prior pretraining-dynamics work has not: does a fact's belief become HARDER to override with in-context counter-evidence as the model learns it more strongly, and are these two timelines causally linked? We hypothesize that (i) for a given fact, there is a checkpoint at which its parametric belief becomes linearly decodable (acquisition) and a later checkpoint at which the model stops revising that belief when the context asserts the opposite (entrenchment); (ii) per-fact acquisition timing predicts entrenchment timing, so belief rigidity co-develops with consolidation rather than being uniform across the final model; and (iii) the consolidation mechanism (a strengthening parametric-recall circuit and declining reliance on context) causally drives the loss of revisability, so that intervening on it restores in-context updatability. Using open pretraining checkpoints (Pythia suite, OLMo-7B) with known training-data frequencies, we build controlled fact sets, present each fact under a matched in-context contradiction, and at each checkpoint measure (a) parametric belief via a linear probe, (b) in-context revision behavior, and (c) an internal 'context-vs-prior reliance' signal. We trace per-fact acquisition and entrenchment curves, test their predictive coupling against frequency and difficulty baselines, localize the consolidating recall component, and causally test whether attenuating it re-opens in-context revisability. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-fact acquisition/entrenchment checkpoints and their correlation, revision-rate trajectories, coupling AUROC vs. baselines, patching/ablation effect sizes, post-intervention revision restoration, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Assemble ~4k subject-relation-object facts spanning a wide range of pretraining-corpus frequencies (using published OLMo/Pythia term-frequency estimates), plus a matched difficulty control set. For each fact, construct (a) a closed-book PARAMETRIC query ('The capital of X is ___'), and (b) an IN-CONTEXT CONTRADICTION prompt asserting the opposite value ('Document: The capital of X is [wrong value]. Question: What is the capital of X?') with matched wording, so a context-faithful model would return the contradicted value. Include a NEUTRAL-context control (context restates the true value or is irrelevant) and comprehension checks (can the model quote the context value). 2) Checkpoint sweep: Use the Pythia suite (multiple sizes: 160M, 1.4B, 6.9B) across its released checkpoints and OLMo-7B intermediate checkpoints. At each checkpoint, for each fact measure: (i) PARAMETRIC BELIEF via a linear probe on residual-stream activations at the object token (trained per-checkpoint with shuffled-label control tasks) and via closed-book log-prob; (ii) IN-CONTEXT REVISION behavior = does the model output the contradicted context value vs. its parametric value; (iii) an internal CONTEXT-vs-PRIOR reliance signal (a linear probe / logit-lens measure of whether the answer token is driven by context tokens vs. the parametric recall pathway, e.g. via attention-to-context and activation-patching-based attribution). 3) Ontogeny curves (key test): Per fact, define the ACQUISITION checkpoint (first checkpoint where parametric belief probe crosses a criterion) and the ENTRENCHMENT checkpoint (first checkpoint where in-context revision rate drops below a criterion, i.e. the model stops accepting the contradiction). Test the coupling hypothesis: acquisition checkpoint predicts entrenchment checkpoint (per-fact correlation), and entrenchment lags acquisition by a characteristic interval. Test whether high-frequency facts entrench earlier. 4) Predictiveness against baselines: Test whether per-fact parametric belief strength at a given checkpoint predicts subsequent loss of in-context revisability (AUROC), beyond corpus frequency and beyond a difficulty baseline (partial regression), isolating the belief-consolidation contribution. 5) Localization: At a mid/late checkpoint, use activation patching / attention attribution to identify the parametric-recall component (heads/FFNs) that carries the entrenched belief and suppresses context reliance; compare across checkpoints to see this component strengthen as entrenchment sets in (linking to Time Course MechInterp / knowledge-entropy findings). 6) Causal intervention: On late (entrenched) checkpoints, test whether attenuating the recall component (ablation / down-scaling its contribution, or steering along a 'context-reliance' direction derived contrastively from early-vs-late or revised-vs-unrevised activations) RESTORES in-context revision on contradicted facts. Report (a) revision-rate recovery, (b) specificity: random-component ablation and neutral-context accuracy preserved, (c) collateral checks on general benchmarks (subset) and perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test that acquisition->entrenchment coupling and the intervention transfer across model sizes, relation types, and frequency bands. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, per-fact acquisition/entrenchment checkpoint distributions and their correlation, revision-rate trajectories over checkpoints, entrenchment-prediction AUROC vs. frequency/difficulty baselines, patching/ablation logit-difference effect sizes, component-strength-over-training curves, post-intervention revision restoration and specificity, perplexity/benchmark deltas.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Entrenchment could simply track corpus frequency rather than a distinct consolidation-to-rigidity mechanism; the frequency-matched difficulty controls and the partial-regression predictiveness test (belief strength beyond frequency) are designed to isolate the belief-consolidation contribution. The acquisition-then-entrenchment ordering may not hold for all facts, or the two timelines may be inseparable (a fact might be revisable and unlearned, or entrenched from first acquisition) - a robust finding either way (coupled vs. decoupled ontogeny) is informative and publishable. In-context revision behavior depends on prompt scaffolding and could reflect instruction-following rather than belief rigidity; comprehension checks, neutral-context controls, and multiple contradiction phrasings mitigate this, though base (non-instruct) checkpoints may under-follow context, which we treat as part of the developmental signal and compare across contradiction strengths. The linear belief probe may not be cleanly decodable or comparable across checkpoints (representations drift); we retrain probes per-checkpoint with control tasks and corroborate with log-prob measures. The recall component may be distributed rather than a discrete head; we treat 'attenuate to restore revisability' as the causal test rather than pinning an exact unit. Checkpoint availability limits us to Pythia/OLMo (specific data mixes); results may not transfer to other pretraining regimes or to very large closed models. Restoring context-reliance via intervention could degrade fluency or make the model indiscriminately gullible to any context; we use small magnitudes, random-component baselines, and monitor perplexity, neutral-context accuracy, and general benchmarks."}
